P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,16,16,P18_p16_122,"exhibited good HER performance with a overpotential of 300 mV at 10 mA cm-2 and a small Tafel slope (110 mV dec-1 ), which is ascribed to the enhanced density of electron transfer at specific domains caused by the defective sites on DG. Currently, heteroatomdoped graphitic carbon materials have been applied in HER as well because of their large specific surface area and unique electronic properties. [126] Bamboo-like nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube based nickel oxide (NiOx@ BCNT) hybrid was designed through calcination of melamine/Ni(NO3)2·6H2O (Figure 8b,c)."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,16,16,P18_p16_123,"Excellent catalytic activity is shown with an overpotential of 79 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm-2 and considerable durability is obtained by adjusting the mass ratio of the precursors and the pyrolytic program (Figure 8d). The experimental results and DFT calculations showed that both bulk Ni and Ni on the interface of Ni/NiO contributed to the outstanding catalytic performance. Besides, the in situ generated Ni from NiO during the HER process also accelerated the hydrogen generation kinetics."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,16,16,P18_p16_124,"The lower energy barrier for H adsorption of cobalt-based materials make them promising catalysts for HER, which urges many researchers to develop various cobalt based nanomaterials. [16] Taking the advantage of H adsorption energy of Co, Zou and co-workers reported the hollow Co-based bimetallic sulfide (MxCo3-xS4, M = Zn, Ni, and Cu) polyhedra by a simple self-template strategy. [127] Simply homogenous bimetallic MOFs were transformed to hollow bimetallic sulfides by solvothermal sulfidation and thermal annealing."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,16,16,P18_p16_125,"Among various bimetallic catalysts, the Zn0.30Co2.70S4 exhibited superior HER activity (overpotential of 85 mV at 10 mA cm-2 in 1 m KOH solution) and stability because of the optimized Gibbs free energy for H* adsorption and improved electrical conductivity due to the homogeneous distribution of Zn in Co3S4 lattice as shown in Figure 9. [127] This study also showed that composition plays effective role in optimizing the Gibbs free energy for H* adsorption, while the morphology tunes the exposer of active sites. It is also found that hollow structure can minimize the mass transfer resistance to enhance the reaction kinetics."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,16,17,P18_p16_126,"Composite formation of two different materials to overcome their limitations is another hydrogen production over Zn0.30Co2.70S4 at pH = 14. Reproduced with permission. [127] Copyright 2016, American Chemical Society. SCIENCE NEWS www.advancedsciencenews.com"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_127,"fascinating technique, where Chen and co-workers designed RuO2/Co3O4 heterojunction by directly annealing a MOF derived Co-Ru complex under atmospheric conditions. [79] The catalyst showed a lower HER overpotential (89 mV at 10 mA cm-2 in 1 m KOH solution) and outstanding long-time durability, which indicated that a heterojunction created by transition metal oxides with small amounts of noble metal oxides can provide more active sites for high electrocatalytic performance, with assistance of efficient water dissociation by Ir. [79]"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_128,"Recently, coupling of Co-based active materials with carbon nanostructures and 3D self-supported electrodes are of immense importance and widely explored. Such as Feng et al. reported Co nanotubes decorated with TiO2 nanodots (NDS;NSNTs) supported on carbon fibers (TiO2-NDs@Co-NSNTs/CFs) as low cost but high-performance electrocatalysts for HER in alkaline medium. [128] The special electronic interactions between TiO2 and Co can change the surface electronic state of electrocatalysts and accordingly can activate the absorbed water molecules as well as optimize the free energy of hydrogen adsorption, which obviously promote HER kinetics."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_129,"[128] It has been proved that the heteroatoms doping having variable electronegativity or/and size is effective way to modify the electronic structure and surface chemistry of carbon. [9,10,129] Therefore, Wu and co-workers developed a simple pyrolysis approach to prepare nitrogen, oxygen, and sulfur tridoped carbon encapsulated Co9S8 (Co9S8@NOSC) nanomaterials using S- and Co(II)- containing polypyrrole solid precursors, which has very high affinity for water oxidation. [130] In contrast to bimetallic oxides, phosphides, and sulfides of Co, its selenides are also very active toward alkaline-HER."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_130,"As an example, Zhang and coworkers prepared an integrated electrocatalyst composed of 3D Ni1-xCoxSe2 mesoporous nanosheets network on nickel foam (NF) as substrate for direct use with tunable stoichiometry by the simple selenization method of NiCo-LDH nanosheets arrays. [48] An acid treatment was employed after selenization to create the mesoporosity and tune the surface chemistry to deliver low overpotential of 85 mV to achieve the current density of -10 mA cm-2 in 1 m KOH."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_131,"The DFT studies have verified that Ni0.89Co0.11Se2 possess the metallic states which have a high electrical conductivity, while existence of Co in the lattices of Ni0.89Co0.11Se2 in an optimal amount modified the adsorption of both H+ and H2O on Ni0.89Co0.11Se2 surfaces with appropriate free energies. Thus, Co is responsible for enhancing the HER catalytic activity along with excellent stability."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_132,"Compared to Co and Ni elements, Fe takes the advantage of its low price and high abundance in the earth crust but there are very limited reports on Fe-based HER electrocatalysts compared with Co and Ni-based materials. Recently, Fe based catalysts have been tailored to modify the electronic structure which has opened up the ways for Fe as an effective catalyst for alkaline HER. For example, Chen and co-workers prepared the NiFeP by simple phosphorization of NiFeOx and achieve the current density of 200 mA cm-2 at overpotential of 220 mV with low values of Tafel slop (78 mV dec-1 )."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_133,"[131] An iron foam was applied as a 3D current collector substrate which provided an intimate contact and high wettability to deliver excellent activity. In another report, microwave-electrochemical deposition of a Fe-Co alloy showed low overpotential of (145 mV), high exchange current density (20 mA cm-2 ) www.advancedscience.com"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_134,"and Tafel parameters close to those for Pt on stainless steel. [104] Use of stainless steel as support and current collector suggested that in the future microwave-electrochemical deposition method can help in providing novel sustainable alloy catalyst materials for a wider range of applications. [104] To take the advantage from the performance enhancing activities of carbon nanosheets, Guo and co-workers prepared the FeCo alloy on the carbon nanosheets in 3D morphology to expose more active sites and lower the onset potential of FeCo alloy to only -63 mV."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_135,"[33] It was also explored that the amounts of Fe and Co in resulting alloy have a strong impact on the activity of final alloy, thus an appropriate control over composition is necessary. Sun and co-workers reported Fe-doped CoP nanoarray on Ti foil (Fe-CoP/Ti) with high HER activity superior to CoP nanoarray, outperforming most of reported non noble-metal catalysts, with an extremely low overpotential of 78 mV for 10 mA cm-2 in 1 m KOH."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_136,"[105] Therefore, these studies open new opportunities in designing and utilizing monolithically integrated ternary or doped transition metal phosphides as well as their other compounds in various structure, e.g., nanoarrays as cost-effective and robust multifunctional materials for practical applications. [105]"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_137,"Although the 3d TM metals show good activity toward water oxidation, however Mn is not so good for hydrogen catalysis directly. In contrast to use solely as HER catalyst, Mn exhibits very high activity toward water electrolysis that is why Mn is extensively studied for oxygen evolution and reduction reactions. [8,37] Considering its high affinity for water dissociation and requirement for alkaline HER catalysis, Yang and coworkers developed bifunctional catalyst in alkaline electrolytes by introducing Mn3O4 in metallic Ni through hydrothermal reaction on the NF."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_138,"[37] Interestingly, the resulting Ni-Mn3O4/NF hybrid catalyst delivered excellent activity and stability toward HER by reaching the current density of 10 mA cm-2 only at very low overpotential of 10 mV in 1 m KOH. The activity and good stability of Ni-Mn3O4/NF hybrid is based on the bifunctional effect of Mn3O4 that accelerate the water dissociation and produce hydrogen intermediates while Ni adsorb the hydrogen intermediates and recombine them to molecular hydrogen. Furthermore, the Mn3O4 increases the metallic characteristics of Ni which greatly influence the charge transport among the electrode and electrolyte."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,17,P18_p17_139,"The introduction of bifunctionality through hybridization of two materials opens a new way in designing efficient catalyst materials for alkaline HER especially by overcoming the additional barrier of water dissociation. [34,71] Recently, heterogeneous nanostructures of CoS2@ WS2/CC have been developed to create unique nanointerfaces through the intermediator sulfur atom (Co-S-W), resulting in high activity as required only 97 mV to achieve current density of 10 mA cm-2 . [119] This strategy presents a unique method to tune the interface to activate the active sites and enhance the activities of catalysts. [132] 5.3. Nonmetal Electrocatalysts"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,17,18,P18_p17_140,"Efficient and low-cost nonmetal electrocatalysts mostly known as carbon based materials for the HER are highly desired for future renewable energy systems to get rid of high cost and expensive 21983844, 2018, 2, Downloaded from https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/advs.201700464, Wiley Online Library on [28/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License SCIENCE NEWS www.advancedsciencenews.com"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_141,"metallic counter parts. [97] Further, carbon based materials provide great opportunities to play with morphology and surface chemistry, while both of these factors play key role in exposing and stabilizing the active sites for efficient catalysis. [91,133-135] Carbon based materials also provides additional stability in alkaline electrolyte due to their oxidation resistive nature compared to metal based catalysts. [30] Although, carbon based materials have several advantages over metallic catalysts but there is a very limited work on carbon based catalysts especially for alkaline HER."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_142,"The reason behind this less exploration of carbon based materials as an alternative catalysts is their poor capability of water oxidation and simultaneous adsorption and recombination of proton to evolve hydrogen. However, by controlling the sp 2 and sp 3 hybridization degree the catalytic properties of the carbon based materials can be tuned. Further the incorporation of heteroatoms (metallic or nonmetallic elements) in the lattice planes of carbon nanostructure is also widely used to mobilize the electronic movements and break the surface neutrality, especially in case of graphitic carbon where heteroatoms can alter the density of state (DOS) of carbon and alter its electronic atmosphere."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_143,"[90,136,137] This kind of modification can enhance their abilities for high electrical conductions, surface reactions and active sites for chemical conversions and chemo/physio adsorptions of molecular components. The alloy formation of two nonmetallic atoms likewise carbon with nitrogen in graphitic structure is another opportunity to tune the catalytic activities of nonmetallic elements. [91] Recently researchers have demonstrated the ability to tailor metal-free C3N4 electrocatalyst to reduce water in to hydrogen both in alkaline and neutral environments."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_144,"[138] However, the poor conductivity of C3N4 is still limiting their application in catalysis, thus C3N4 needs to build a direct connection with conductive materials to improve their efficiencies for electrocatalysis. For example, Antonietti and co-workers presented a generic, facile, and easy water based method coupled with thermal annealing to grow highly ordered C3N4 on different substrates from surface-bound cyanuric acid and melamine supramolecular complexes and demonstrate the high suitability of this material to catalyze the HER."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_145,"[139] The metal-free catalyst generally delivers low overpotential values, which are comparable to those of non-noble metals while having reasonable current densities. The C3N4/FTO (fluorine-doped tin oxide) electrode demonstrated low overpotential ≈0.1 V versus RHE and with current densities of 0.8 mA cm-2 at the overpotential of 0.3 V in 0.1 m KOH. The current densities can be increased further by using different types of supportive materials like TiO2, ZnO, etc. For example, the C3N4/TiO2 electrocatalyst shows better current density (1.3 mA cm-2 at an overpotential of 0.3 V) at same overpotential than C3N4/FTO."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_146,"It is concluded that substrate surface chemistry also strongly effect the physical and chemical properties of the catalysts. Such as deposition of C3N4 on different substrates (as above TiO2 and FTO) strongly affects their morphologies, new interfaces, formation of surface defects or new active states as well as alter their electronic and optical properties, resulting in variable catalytic activities. Thus, strategies to tune the catalyst functions based on substrate and changing the composition of starting complex opens the opportunity for a new family of materials for HER."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_147,"Although these strategies have proved the active nature of nonmetallic materials for HER, but their current densities www.advancedscience.com are far poor than their metallic counterparts that needs to be improved for their practical applications in real devices. In this regards Xu and co-workers design a 3D O, N, and P tridoped porous graphitic nanocarbons (ONPPGC) directly grown on oxidized CC (OCC) as a binder free electrocatalyst for alkaline hydrogen catalysis. [140] The ONPPGC/OCC represents the first carbon nanomaterial based nonmetal class of catalyst for full water splitting, which was prepared by using aniline and phytic acid as precursors and OCC as supportive substrate."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_148,"In 1 M KOH solution, ONPPGC/OCC exhibited improved activity with an overpotential of 446 mV to achieve the current density of 10 mA cm-2 . A better value of Tafel slope (154 mV dec-1 ) for ONPPGC/OCC than bare CC (193 mV dec-1 ) proves that designing of heteroatom doped carbon can alter its nature through covalent interaction. Further it is also found that the strong coupling in ONPPGC/OCC also results good electrochemical stability as catalyst only require a potential of ≈0.4 V to deliver 7.6 mA cm-2 and current density then stabilized around this value for the 5 h reaction time, unprecedented stability."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_149,"The activity of ONPPGC/OCC shows that 3D porous electrodes can possess excellent activity and durability for all pH values to achieve water splitting in electrocatalytic system. When using ONPPGC/OCC as both anode and cathode, an efficient basic water electrolyzer demonstrates 10 mA cm-2 at a cell voltage of 1.66 V with superior stability, which inspires exploring 3D porous metal-free electrode for full water splitting."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_150,"Zheng and co-workers developed a homologous, metalfree electrocatalyst for HER in alkaline media consisting of self-supported sulfur-doped carbon nitride/carbon nanotube/ carbon fiber (S-C3N4-CNT-CF). [141] The CNTs are deposited in 3D fashion on the interconnected CF networks and thermally treated which make the overall structure highly, enhance the electrical conductivity and result robust mechanical stability. A π-π stacking is utilized for in situ growth of C3N4 on the surface of CNTs and CFs to form C3N4-CNT-CF hybrid structure which is further sulfurized for sulfur doping to form S-C3N4-CNT-CF."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_151,"In 1 m KOH, S-C3N4-CNT-CF electrode showed an onset potential of 50 mV and an overpotential of 131 mV versus RHE to achieve a current densities of 1 and 10 mA cm-2 , respectively, with a Tafel slope of 79 mV dec-1 . Moreover, it exhibited excellent stability for 30 h test duration in 1 m KOH solution, thus suggesting the subsequent sulfur doping along with unique porous structure can improve the HER performance of the C3N4-based electrodes. Substitutional doping and the introduction of nitrogen atoms are often used to modify the hexagonal lattice of carbon nanomaterials because pristine sp 2 carbon is inactive for most electrochemical reactions."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,18,P18_p18_152,"However, to expose more easily accessible active centers to the electrolyte for catalysis, more molecules and ions in the electrolyte need to pass through the facial plane of sp 2 carbons. Therefore, to improve mass transfer efficiency for HER, Zhang and co-workers prepared nitrogen-doped carbon microtubes or nitrogen-doped graphene microtubes with ultrathin walls of 1-4 nm and large inner voids of 1-2 µm to combine the advantages of NG and N-CNT. [142] The N-GMT were prepared through a simple carbonization of a mixture of glycin and dicyandiamide (DCDA) (Figure 10a-g)."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,18,19,P18_p18_153,"Here, nitrogen doping in GMT provided more active sites, resulting in significantly enhanced HER activity with a current density 21983844, 2018, 2, Downloaded from https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/advs.201700464, Wiley Online Library on [28/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License SCIENCE NEWS www.advancedsciencenews.com Current density (mA·cm-²), www.advancedscience.com"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,19,19,P18_p19_154,"Figure 10. Large-area SEM images of a) N-CNT, b) N-CNT+NGMT, and c) N-GMT, which are obtained from the same precursor (a 1:40 mixture of glycin and DCDA) at 900, 1,000, and 1100 °C, respectively. Detailed d) SEM, e) TEM, and f-g) HRTEM images of N-GMT, h) IR-corrected LSV curves of N-GMT in basic solution with various KOH concentrations, i) LSV curves of N-GMT before and after 1000 cycles of cyclic voltammetry in 0.1 and 6 m KOH solution. Scan rate is 10 mV s-1 . Reproduced with permission. [142] Copyright 2016, Springer."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,19,20,P18_p19_155,"of 10 mA cm-2 at overpotentials of 0.464 and 0.426 V in 0.1 and 6 m KOH solution, respectively (Figure 10h,i). To the best of our knowledge, this HER performance is the best in basic solution among the metal-free catalyst reported till now. The N-GMT promises to be an efficient HER catalyst for real applications in water splitting and chlor-alkali processes. These findings inspire exploiting structure dependent functions to design efficient metal-free graphene based nanocatalysts for SCIENCE NEWS www.advancedsciencenews.com"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_156,"electrochemical applications as well as other liquid phase catalytic reactions. Taking advantage of similar dual heteroatom doping, Qiao and co-workers developed ""graft-and-pyrolyze"" technique to develop N,S-codoped carbon materials, a new methodology fully different from older post treatment strategies. [30] Initially, PDA was deposited on the CNTs surface and then chemical grafting was done by thiol groups as S-precursors at room temperature. After pyrolyzing at high temperature, the N,S-CNTs hybrid delivered the current density of 5 mA cm-2 at very low over potential of 40 mV in 1 m KOH, outperformed many other carbon based HER catalysts."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_157,"Hence, proving that surface chemistry of carbon based materials is a key factor in tuning their catalytic abilities. 6. Conclusion and Future Perspectives"
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_158,"As one of the most important reactions of water catalysis, HER has gained extensive attention of researchers over the last decade and large number of catalysts have been developed to enhance the HER kinetics. Unfortunately, the HER kinetics and mechanism vary with nature of used electrolytes and becomes more tough as moving from acidic to alkaline one, more sluggish kinetics involve due to additional water dissociation step. This is the one reason why the best performing acidic medium catalysts have very poor activity in alkaline electrolytes, even the noble metals are suffering from poor stability and large overpotentials to achieve reasonable current densities."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_159,"The mechanism is not as simple as in case of acidic media where only hydrogen binding energies decide the reaction rate, while in alkaline electrolytes water adsorption and its dissociation, hydrogen adsorption/desorption energies and hydroxyl ions affinity to catalyst surface (poisoned the surface by occupying active site) decide the reaction pathway and rate. Thus, sluggish kinetics and poor activities of most of the catalysts limit the development in the alkaline HER catalysis, one of the most industrial and economically demanding hydrogen production systems."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_160,"To overcome the challenges associated with unclear sluggish reaction mechanism of alkaline HER, recently several efforts have been put forward by combining the two different catalysts. Likewise the decoration of Pt surfaces with Ni(OH)2 or introducing Mn3O4 into Ni metal based catalysts which can first dissociate the water to generate hydrogen intermediates that can be adsorb with nearby active sites for recombination to produce hydrogen."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_161,"Besides generating simple composite structures that increase the cost, complicated fabrication methods and utilize high price rare metals, designing of low cost TM or nonmetal based catalysts by tuning their compositions to alter the electronic and surface chemistries is a considerably better choice. Such combinations of various metals in their compounds and/or heterostructures with nonmetals provide an excellent way to generate bifunctionality (water dissociation and simultaneous hydrogen recombination) within a single catalyst to boost the hydrogen production in alkaline solutions."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_162,"In this regard, 3d metals-based catalysts have shown a promising future by delivering high current densities at low overpotentials, while bears good stability. To take these bifunctional catalysts to next step to improve their efficiencies further, their in situ designing on the highly conductive 3D substrates www.advancedscience.com were carried out. Direct growth on conductive supports can enhance their conductivity and active surface area as well as generate intimate contact with current collector to lower the charge transfer resistance and 3D morphologies expose more active sites and increase the mas transfer, ultimately improve the HER activity."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_163,"Although, newly developed strategies have shown high potential and make the HER possible in alkaline electrolytes with low overpotential and high stability but still several things need to be considered for stable and economical operations. In fact, the heterostructures take the advantages of unique electronic structure which results in exceptional properties like faster recombination rate, higher electronic mobility, better mass transport pathways, and finally long term stability."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_164,"Further, heterostructures also provide easy control over their surface tuning to expose the active sites, hence providing the opportunity to utilize maximum material in catalysis that ultimately helps in cost reduction. First of all the HER mechanism in alkaline electrolyte needs to be clarified using both theoretical studies and experimental results. The DFT calculations can be employed to see how the different surfaces affect the HER catalysis as well as what kind of relation exists among the adsorption/desorption energies of water, proton, and hydroxyl ions and how these affect the final reaction rate."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_165,"Then to verify these theoretical studies, in situ microscopic, structural, and compositional characterization should be employed, to see how the various surfaces are behaving during the HER catalysis under practical conditions and what factors are controlling the releasing of hydrogen from catalyst surface. It can also reveal that how the catalyst surface is changing during the HER reaction and to what extent it recover. Thus, taking in account all these theoretical and experimental findings, the tuning of advanced catalyst can be done to overcome the challenges associated with the alkaline electrolytes and limit their applications."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,20,P18_p20_166,"On the other hand, these findings will also lead the researchers toward developing integrated catalyst with perfectly required surface chemistry, composition, structure, and morphologies that can have the ability to lower the free energies and overpotential with long term stabilities. Besides developing new catalysts or/and modifications of existing catalyst, the cost should be kept in mind as higher cost of catalyst will increase the overall price of resulted hydrogen. The catalysts should be comprised of low-price metals/nonmetals and their synthesis method should be very facile that can be extended to industrial scale without any modification and can produce large amounts of hydrogen."
P18,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms ,Advanced Science - 2017 - Mahmood - Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen Evolution in Alkaline Electrolytes  Mechanisms .pdf,20,21,P18_p20_167,"Apart from the synthesis of catalyst, the hydrogen production systems should be integrated in such a way that they can easily be coupled with the other energy systems for its continuous production, storage, and easy transport to the end users. Acknowledgements N.M. and Y.Y. contributed equally to this work. The authors would like to acknowledge the support from the National Natural Science Foundation of China (51661145026, 21676193, 21506156), the Tianjin Municipal Natural Science Foundation (15JCZDJC37300, 16JCQNJC05200) and Pakistan Science Foundation PSF/257NSFC-Eng/P-UOL(02). SCIENCE NEWS www.advancedsciencenews.com Conflict of Interest The authors declare no conflict of interest. Keywords"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_000,"www.acsaem.org Article Ultrathin NiPt Single-Atom Alloy for Synergistically Accelerating Alkaline Hydrogen Evolution Liuxiang Huo, Chunqiao Jin, Jianli Tang, Xionghu Xu, Kai Jiang, Liyan Shang, Yawei Li, Jinzhong Zhang, Liangqing Zhu,* Junhao Chu, and Zhigao Hu* Cite This: ACS Appl. Energy Mater. 2022, 5, 15136-15145 ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations *sı Supporting Information"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_001,"ABSTRACT: Single-atom Pt-based catalysts display a promising strategy to enhance the atomic utilization of noble metals while attaining desirable electrocatalytic properties. Moreover, rationally selecting the substrate helps optimize the water dissociation energy as well as promote H* conversion. Herein, we dispersed monoatomic Pt with ultralow content onto porous nickel nanosheets to accelerate the reaction kinetics of the hydrogen evolution reaction in an alkaline medium. Density functional theory calculations reveal that the synergistic effects between Ni-Ni and Ni-Pt sites are jointly responsible for the highly efficient hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalysis."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_002,"Besides, ultrathin nanosheets with a porous structure of Ni substantially provide further enhancement. Consequently, the obtained NiPt alloy catalyst displays exceptional alkaline HER activity with a low overpotential of 18 mV at the specific current density of 10 mA cm -2 and a Tafel slope of 45 mV dec -1 , which significantly outperform the commercial Pt/C. This work provides valuable guidelines for the proper design and construction of single-atom alloy catalysts. KEYWORDS: single-atom Pt, NiPt alloy, porous nanosheets, synergistic effect, hydrogen evolution reaction ■ INTRODUCTION"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_003,"Hydrogen (H2), as environment-friendly energy with a high gravimetric energy density, is expected to advance the deep decarbonization of the energy market by taking the place of traditional fossil fuels. 1-3 Alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) powered by intermittent renewable electricity has emerged as a prospective method in recent years because of its preferable advantages like general stability, high product purity, and satisfactory industrial value. 4-6 To date, platinum (Pt)- based catalysts have been recognized as benchmark HER catalysts due to their optimum binding ability with hydrogen."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_004,"7-9 Some other platinum group metals like Ru, Rh, and Ir also show high performance for HER. 10-14 However, their high cost and scarcity restrict their large-scale commercial production. Single-atom catalysts have become an effective approach. For instance, Cu and Rh as bimetallic single atoms and nanoparticles anchored N-doped graphene surfaces could show excellent HER kinetics. 11 Moreover, employing carbonization of biomass support to immobilize single Ru atoms in waste-yeast biomass was also a remarkable strategy for saving cost while maintaining high intrinsic activity."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_005,"12 Hence, developing single-atom catalysts came to be considered a cost-efficient approach by maximizing the utilization efficiency of noble metals. 15-20 Besides, the HER catalytic activity of Pt is significantly lower in alkaline media compared with that in acidic media, which mainly suffers from sluggish kinetics in the Volmer step. 21-23 The Volmer step is the process where water"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_006,"molecules are dissociated into adsorbed hydrogen (H*) and a hydroxyl radical, which is one of the electrochemical reactions involved in alkaline HER. The other reaction is to convert H* to H2 by combining two adsorbed hydrogen atoms (Tafel step) or by bonding one hydrogen atom and one water molecule (Heyrovsky step). 24 Considering the electronic interaction between the support and monoatomic Pt in the interface, 25 rational design of a substrate is of great importance to minimize the barrier for cutting the strong OH-H bonds."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,1,P19_p1_007,"To our knowledge, most single-atom Pt coupled with electronegative supports (e.g., porous carbon matrix, 26 graphenes, 27 carbon nanospheres 28 ) generally exhibit unsatisfactory intrinsic activity due to the large energy consumption of water dissociation. However, nitrogen-doped graphene was developed as a promising support for Pt single atoms with highly efficient HER in recent reports. 29-31 Therefore, it is important to select more proper substrates for achieving highefficiency electrocatalytic activity. Nickel (Ni) is one of the most important materials in the application of electrocatalysis, usually acting as a cheap Received: August 29, 2022 Accepted: November 11, 2022"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,1,2,P19_p1_008,"Published: November 23, 2022 Figure 1. Synthesis process of Pt single atom anchored Ni nanosheets on nickel foam. Figure 2. (a) SEM, (b) TEM, (c) high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and corresponding SAED pattern images (inset), and (d) elemental mapping images of NiPt nanosheets. (e) Atomic-resolution high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images for Pt-Ni(OH)2. HAADF-STEM images after Fourier transformation filtering viewed from the (f) and (g) of Pt-Ni(OH)2. (h, i) Intensity profiles corresponding to labeled lines in (f) and (g), respectively."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,2,2,P19_p2_009,"alternative to precious metals. For alkaline catalysts, the hydroxide binding strength has been confirmed to correlate with the HER activity of Pt-based catalysts. 32 Too weak hydroxide adsorption strength will lead to slow water dissociation, while too strong hydroxide binding strength will lead to difficulties in hydroxide desorption. A moderate value is preferable. In the system of Pt(111) surfaces modified with 3dM metal hydr(oxy)oxide clusters (3d-M = Ni, Co, Fe, and Mn), the OH-M 2+δ bond strength activity of the transition metals follows the order of Ni > Co > Fe > Mn. Thus, the trend in HER activity for M 2+δ O δ (OH) 2-δ /Pt(111) catalyst surfaces is also Ni > Co > Fe > Mn."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,2,2,P19_p2_010,"33 This ability to promote the dissociation of water makes nickel hydroxide be used in the bifunctional catalytic mode for HER. 34 Similarly, Ni-based catalysts such as Ni-based alloys, Ni-based layered double hydroxide (LDH), and Ni-based phosphides have been found to possess a superior ability to catalyze water dissociation in alkaline media. 34-39 Herein, much progress has been achieved in constructing hybrid materials of Ni and Pt metals. For example, Pei et al. have designed a Pt@Ni3N catalyst to tune the binding abilities of hydroxyl ions (OH*) and hydrogen (H*) for improving the alkaline HER efficiency."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,2,2,P19_p2_011,"40 More recently, a two-dimensional (2D) NiO/Ni heterostructure nanosheet-supported single-atom Pt was reported to significantly boost the catalytic activity by tailoring the water dissociation barrier and promoting hydrogen conversion. 41 Although these strategies certainly reduced the energy barrier of water dissociation in the Volmer step, the HER performance was still limited by finite active sites. In this regard, the special nanostructure design of Ni-based support is especially vital, which provides abundant exposed active centers, along with powerful water splitting performance."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,2,3,P19_p2_012,"In this work, we utilize Ni(OH)2 nanosheets as selftemplates to prepare ultrathin porous Ni nanosheets with ultralow Pt loading (denoted as NiPt) as an alkaline HER catalyst. In this topochemical conversion, ultrathin Ni(OH)2 6 Figure 3. (a) XRD patterns and (b) Raman spectra (532 nm laser excitation wavelength) of NiPt/NF and Pt-Ni(OH)2. XPS spectra of (c) Ni2p and (d) Pt4s of NiPt and Pt-Ni(OH)2. (e) Pt L3-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra of NiPt, commercial Pt/C, and PtO2 and (f) the corresponding Fourier transform EXAFS spectra."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_013,"was one type of attractive layered template for preparing nonlayered porous Ni nanosheets, which can provide a wellretained structural framing to limit epitaxial lattice expansion and swelling of few-monolayer-thick structures during the alloying process. 24,42 Other than faster mass transfer and good electrical conductivity, the special porous and ultrathin nanosheet structure can also expose a high density of catalytic active sites. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations suggest that the NiPt catalyst shows optimized adsorption affinity toward H2O and H* while simultaneously decreasing the thermodynamic barriers for H2O splitting, thus facilitating overall alkaline HER."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_014,"The prepared NiPt electrode shows excellent HER performance in an alkaline electrolyte, which only needs 18 mV overpotential at 10 mA cm -2 with a negligible activity decay after 5000 cycles. In addition, the mass activity of NiPt at the overpotential of 100 mV is 25.1 A mg -1 , which is much higher than that of commercial Pt/C and numerous advanced catalysts. ■ RESULTS AND DISCUSSION"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_015,"Synthesis and Characterization of NiPt Alloy Catalysts. The fabrication processes of porous NiPt nanosheets are depicted schematically in Figure 1. The Ni(OH)2 nanoarrays, which are evenly distributed on the nickel foam, were prepared by hydrothermal treatment of H2O2 solution, urea, and nickel foam at 180 °C for 24 h (Figure S1). 43 After secondary hydrothermal treatment of the obtained Ni(OH)2 nanosheets and the solution of chloroplatinic acid pentahydrate, the assynthesized Pt-Ni(OH)2 could still retain the original shape of the precursor flake array (Figure S2)."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_016,"The Pt-Ni(OH)2 was subsequently reduced in an Ar/H2 atmosphere, thereby forming porous NiPt alloy nanosheets, which can be observed by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image in Figure 2a. The Pt-Ni(OH)2 nanosheets exhibit an ultrathin thickness of only 3.2 nm (Figure S5) by atomic force microscopy (AFM) measurement. Moreover, the thickness of the NiPt nanosheet only increases to 3.7 nm (Figure S6), which is attributed to the inevitable lattice expansion during the alloying process. 44 It is worth noting that the NiPt is flimsier than in the other reports, 45-47 indicating the superiority of this synthesized strategy."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_017,"Besides, transparent electron microscopy (TEM) was carried out to characterize the microstructure of the asprepared materials. Ultrathin flakelike Pt-Ni(OH)2 nanosheets are observed in Figure S7a, without any discernible metallic particles. The lattice distance of 0.15 nm corresponds to the (1 0 0) plane of hexagonal Ni(OH)2, and the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern in Figure S7b further reveals the crystalline nature of Pt-Ni(OH)2. Elemental mapping analysis of TEM images (Figure S8) confirms the homogeneous dispersion of Pt on Ni(OH)2 nanosheets, and the content of O element is 43.31 wt %, as collected by an energy-dispersive spectrometer (EDS, Figure S9)."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,3,P19_p3_018,"However, the EDS of NiPt shows a decrease of the oxygen concentration from 43.31 to 4.2 wt % (Figures 2d and S10), indicating that Ni(OH)2 successfully transformed into Ni. More precisely, the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) results of Pt-Ni(OH)2 and NiPt verified that the amounts of Pt doping are 0.18 and 0.3 wt %, respectively. The spherical-aberration-corrected TEM (STEM) images show that the Pt single atoms are evenly distributed (Figure 2e) and occupy the lattice site of Ni(OH)2 (Figure 2f-i). These results indicate that the NiPt alloy material should be a single-atom electrocatalyst likewise in one aspect as evidence."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,3,4,P19_p3_019,"The ultrathin porous NiPt nanosheet can also be observed from the TEM (mA cm²2) Catalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction Figure 4. (a) HER polarization curves of NiPt, Pt-Ni(OH)2, Ni, and Pt/C. (b) Comparison of overpotentials required to attain 10 and 100 mA cm -2 , and the mass activities of the prepared catalysts. (c) Corresponding Tafel slope derived from LSV curves. (d) Nyquist plots of the catalysts. (e) Double-layer capacitances (Cdl) of the catalysts. (f) Stability test of NiPt through cyclic potential scanning and the chronoamperometry method. (g) Comparison of the HER activity for NiPt with the reported catalysts, originating from Table S1, Supporting Information."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,4,4,P19_p4_020,"image (Figure 2b). As shown in Figure 2c, the lattice fringe of 0.204 nm is indexed to be the (111̅ ) plane of face-centered cubic Ni. The nanoscale frame and continuous porous tunnels of NiPt can not only promote smooth transportation of electrons but also provide a larger specific surface area and good nanoscale effect, which are conducive to achieving its high catalytic activity. 48"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,4,4,P19_p4_021,"The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern shows detailed phase evolution from the as-prepared Pt-Ni(OH)2 to NiPt. The typical peaks at 19.2, 33.1, 38.6, 59.1, and 62.7° are well indexed to the (001), (100), (101), (110), and (111) planes of Ni(OH)2 (JCPDS 14-0117), respectively, while the peaks located at approximately 44.6, 51.8, and 76.4° are attributed to the Ni foam substrate (JCPDS 04-0850) (Figure 3a). All of the characteristic peaks of Ni(OH)2 disappeared after the reduction process, which proves the conversion from Ni(OH)2 to Ni. No signal of Pt was found in XRD, implying that there are no apparent particles or agglomerations of Pt, 41 which agrees with the results of STEM."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,4,4,P19_p4_022,"Besides, grazing incidence Xray diffraction (GI-XRD) with small incidence angles was also recorded to detect more surficial information of NiPt, which shows similar diffraction peaks to typical XRD (Figure S14). The Raman spectra are employed to further reveal this transformation. The minor peak at 447 cm -1 and the intense peak at 3578 cm -1 correspond to the Ni-OH stretching vibration and the symmetric tensile vibration of hydroxyl groups of β-Ni(OH)2, 49 and the two peaks also disappear after the reduction process (Figure 3b). The chemical composition and electronic valence of the electrocatalyst are determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS)."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,4,4,P19_p4_023,"For the precursor PtNi(OH)2, the peaks centered at 855.5 and 873.1 eV are assigned to Ni 2p3/2 and Ni 2p1/2 of Ni 2+ , accompanied by the two shakeup satellite peaks at 861.2 and 879.1 eV (Figure 3c). Compared with Ni(OH)2, there are negative shifts in Ni XPS and O 1s XPS (Figure S11), indicating Pt has been successfully coupled into Ni(OH)2. Furthermore, considerable Ni 2p3/2 and Ni 2p1/2 peaks at 852.4 and 869.8 eV assigned to Ni 0 are observed in Ni 2p XPS of NiPt, and the peaks of 855.5 and 873.3 eV along with the satellite peaks are assigned to Ni 2+ , which is ascribed to the oxidation on the surface of NiPt."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,4,4,P19_p4_024,"Comparably, no characteristic peak of Ni 2+ was observed in Raman and GI-XRD results, suggesting that the main substance covered on the external surface are the Ni nanosheets. Besides, the peaks at 74.5 and 72.2 eV of Pt 4f7/2, which are shown in Figure 3d, reveal the presence of Pt 4+ and Pt 2+ in Pt-Ni(OH)2. The binding energies of Pt 4f7/2 and Pt 4f5/2 peaks at 71.3 and 74.5 eV for NiPt are lower than those of Pt-Ni(OH)2, which indicates the existence of Pt δ+ with a lower valence state in NiPt."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_025,"Figure 5. (a) Computational models and localized electric field distribution of NiPt, (b) Ni, and (c) Pt at the same coverage. (d) Calculated PDOS of NiPt, Ni, and Pt, with an aligned Fermi level. (e) Calculated d-band centers of NiPt, Ni, and Pt. (f) Water adsorption energy, (g) energy barriers of water dissociation kinetic, and (h) adsorption free energies of H* on the surface of the catalysts. (i) Mechanism of the NiPt network as an efficient catalyst toward large-scale water electrolysis in alkaline media (red, O; white, H; blue, Ni; purple, Pt)."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_026,"X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) is employed to corroborate the local structure of Pt single atoms because of its excellent sensitivity to atomic species. As shown in Figure 3e, the normalized Pt L3-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra are performed to exhibit differences among NiPt, Pt foil, and PtO2. The white-line intensity of NiPt is located between the Pt foil and PtO2, slightly higher than that of metallic Pt, suggesting that the valence state of Pt in NiPt is a little higher than 0. Combined with XPS results, it can be concluded that the valence of Pt δ+ is between 0 and +2."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_027,"The particular valence state of Pt δ+ in NiPt reveals the partial charge transfer from Pt to coordinated Ni, which is beneficial for stabilizing Pt atoms on the surface of Ni. In the obtained Fourier transformed extended X-ray absorption fine structure (FT-EXAFS) spectroscopy (Figure 3f), the major peak at 2.27 Å of NiPt showed a shorter bond length in comparison to that of the Pt foil, which is attributed to the loss of electrons in Pt. Furthermore, the successful anchoring of monoatomic Pt in Ni nanosheets has been further proved, which agrees well with the results of the atomically scattered Pt observed by the sphericalaberration TEM image."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_028,"Electrocatalytic Evaluation of HER. To shed light on the HER performance of the synthesized catalysts, all of the samples including NiPt, Pt-Ni(OH)2, and commercial Pt/C are tested by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements in a 1 M KOH electrolyte. Ni nanosheets were also prepared as one of the control samples to differentiate the effect of Ni and Pt on the catalyst. As shown in Figure 4a, NiPt alloy with only 0.3 wt % Pt single atoms exhibits superior HER activity compared with other fabricated catalysts."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_029,"NiPt can deliver overpotentials as low as 18 and 169 mV to reach current densities of 10 and 100 mA cm -2 (Figure 4b), respectively, dramatically outperforming Pt-Ni(OH)2 (71 and 379 mV), Ni (49 and 317 mV), and even commercial Pt/C (37 and 225 mV). Moreover, the mass activity of NiPt at an overpotential of 100 mV is 25.1 A mg -1 , which is about 83 times greater than that of the conventional Pt/C catalyst (0.3 A mg -1 ). These results suggest that the alkaline HER activity is maximized by the Pt single atoms anchored Ni nanosheets, significantly decreasing the content of noble metals on the support."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,5,5,P19_p5_030,"Besides, NiPt exhibits a Tafel slope of 45 mV dec -1 , smaller than that of Pt-Ni(OH)2 (139 mV dec -1 ), Ni (80 mV dec -1 ), and Pt/C (62 mV dec -1 ), demonstrating the faster HER kinetics (Figure 4c). The HER reaction dynamic of NiPt follows the typical Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanisms, in which the chemical inversion from H2O to adsorbed H (Volmer step) rather than H2 desorption (Heyrovsky step) is the rate-determining step. Compared with other electrode samples, such a small Tafel slope for NiPt reveals a significant promotion in the Volmer step."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_031,"In addition, the faster kinetics and higher HER catalytic activity coincide with the smallest charge-transfer resistance of NiPt and the largest electrochemical active area. As shown in Figure 4d, NiPt exhibits a quite small charge transfer resistance (Rct) of 0.36 Ω, less than that of Pt-Ni(OH)2 (0.779 Ω) and Ni (0.418 Ω). The results of electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) verify the accelerated charge transformation mechanics between the NiPt and electrolyte solution due to the boosted mass transfer of porous nanosheets and the optimized nanostructure."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_032,"The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of catalysts can be studied via electrochemical double-capacitance layer (Cdl) measurements (Figure S12). As shown in Figure 4e, NiPt possesses a higher Cdl of 33 mF cm -2 , which is higher than that of Pt-Ni(OH)2 (8 mF cm -2 ), Ni (18 mF cm -2 ), and Pt/C (26 mF cm -2 ). Thus, the enhanced catalytic activity of NiPt is more about the strong interaction between Pt single atoms and the Ni substrate than just the active surface area. Meanwhile, to evaluate the practicability of NiPt, the faradic efficiency (FE) was tested by the drainage method."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_033,"As shown in Figure S15, the quantity of H2 in the process of water splitting catalyzed by NiPt at the current density of 40 mA cm -2 was recorded. In addition, the FE for HER is about 95.2%, which is obtained by comparing the actual and calculated H2 production. The stability of NiPt was evaluated by long-term cyclic voltammetry (CV) between 0 and -0.6 V (vs RHE) with a scan rate of 50 mV s -1 and the chronoamperometry method. As illustrated in Figure 4f, NiPt shows negligible activity degradation after stability tests."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_034,"The structural stability of NiPt was further performed by SEM, XRD, and XPS after the durability test (Figures S4 and S13), of which the results exhibit hardly any changes in the surface topography, composition, and electronic structure, confirming the extraordinary structural stability of NiPt under alkaline HER. To the best of our knowledge, the electrocatalytic HER performance of the NiPt catalyst is almost optimal in comparison with the reported water-alkali electrocatalysts (Figure 4g and Table S1), which is attributed to electronic interactions of atomic Pt and Ni nanosheets."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_035,"Theoretical Investigations. To obtain insights into the internal mechanism of ameliorated HER performance, DFT calculations were performed to prove the effectiveness of the evolved configurations of Pt single atoms on NiPt. As shown in Figure 5a, the structural models of catalysts and the corresponding charge density difference patterns show that the Ni-Ni site can induce a larger charge density area in comparison with the Ni-Pt bridge site and the Pt-Pt site (Figure 5b,c), which is conducive to providing more electrons for the evolution of H."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_036,"50 The projected density of states (PDOS, Figure 5d) of pure Ni shows a higher occupancy near the Fermi level than that of Pt and Pt doped Ni, revealing the excessive activation capacity of Ni toward H2O. 51 The decreased DOS of NiPt near the Fermi level mainly derives from the addition of Pt single atoms, assisting in modulating the adsorption energy of H on the catalyst. Generally speaking, the higher the energy level of the d-band center, the stronger the affinity of the catalysts to hydrogen species due to the unoccupied and unfilled antibonding states by electrons."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_037,"As shown in Figure 5e, the downward shift of the d-band center between Ni and NiPt implies weaker adsorption toward hydrogen intermediates on the surface of NiPt so as to improve the H-spillover ability of the electrode. Moreover, the Gibbs free energies of water adsorption (ΔGHd 2O*) on the surface of catalysts are also calculated in Figure 5f. The ΔGHd 2O* of Pt/C is obviously higher than that of NiPt and Ni, which indicates the weakest water adsorption capacity and thus leads to sluggish alkaline HER kinetics."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_038,"Meanwhile, Ni and NiPt exhibit a lower energy barrier to drive water dissociation compared with Pt/C, which affirms the main role of the Ni substrate in facilitating the rate-determining step. For the reorganization of the dissociated proton (H*) and H2 evolution, the adsorption energy of hydrogen (ΔGH*) is regarded as a valid descriptor to estimate the intrinsic electrocatalytic activity in alkaline media."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_039,"29,52 As is shown in Figure 5h, the H binding energy of -0.3 eV on the NiPt surface exhibits a superior behavior of hydrogen adsorption, similar to the Pt/C surface at the same coverage, which proves that the hydrogen adsorption of the Ni substrate is improved by modification of Pt single atoms. Therefore, DFT calculation results unfold the intrinsic reaction mechanism of the prepared NiPt alloy, as concluded in Figure 5i. The Ni sites behave as active centers for the dissociation of water molecules and the migration of hydrogen, and the nearby Ni-Pt bridge sites are mainly responsible for accelerating hydrogen conversion."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_040,"As expected, this outcome matches well with the excellent catalytic activity in the alkaline condition verified through the electrochemical test. ■ CONCLUSIONS"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_041,"In summary, we have proposed a feasible strategy to greatly accelerate alkaline HER by constructing atomic dispersed Pt over ultrathin porous Ni substrates through a topochemical conversion of the Ni(OH)2 template. The atomic-resolution HAADF-STEM images and EXAFS displayed that the Pt atoms are atomically embedded in Ni nanosheets. The DFT calculations disclose the synergetic mechanism between the dual active sites including Ni-Ni and Ni-Pt sites on the surface. In detail, Ni-Pt possesses a low H binding energy (0.3 eV), which promotes H* conversion and H2 desorption. In the meanwhile, the incorporation of Pt leads to the downshift of the Ni d-band center, improving the H2 release rate."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_042,"In addition, numerous Ni-Ni sites having preferred adsorption toward H2O and a lower energy barrier for cleaving H-OH bonds are essential for the fast kinetics of water dissociation. Further improvement in the overall alkaline HER was achieved by the unique structure of Ni nanosheets, not only exposing abundant catalytically active sites but also boosting charge transport and mass transfer. Consequently, the NiPt singleatom alloy shows superior alkaline HER performance with 18 mV at 10 mA cm -2 and a mass activity of 25.1 A mg -1 , which is much better than those of commercial Pt/C and other referred alkaline HER catalysts."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_043,"For real applications, NiPt also exhibits outstanding stability with a negligible activity decay after 5000 cycles and long-term HER in 1 M KOH media. This work provides an avenue for developing Pt single-atom alloy catalysts with ultralow Pt loading and captures deeper insights into the bimetal synergistic effect. ■ EXPERIMENTAL SECTION"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_044,"Material Preparation. Ni foam, H2O2 solution, urea, chloroplatinic acid pentahydrate (H2PtCl6·5H2O), and Pt/C (20 %) were purchased from Aladdin industries (Shanghai, China). All of the chemicals were used as received without further purification. Deionized water (resistivity of 18.2 MΩ cm -3 ), prepared by going through an ultrapure purification system (Aqua Solutions), was used to configure all aqueous solutions. Ni(OH)2 nanosheets were synthesized following the approach of a previous report. 43 Hexagonal Ni(OH)2 nanosheet arrays were loaded on NF by the hydrothermal treatment of H2O2 solution, urea, and NF at 180 °C for 24 h."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,6,6,P19_p6_045,"For the synthesis of Pt-Ni(OH)2, the prepared Ni(OH)2 was added in 3 × 10 -4 M chloroplatinic acid solution followed by hydrothermal treatment in a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave at 180 °C for 12 h. For the preparation of NiPt, Pt-Ni(OH)2 was annealed at 350 °C for 8 h under a mixed Ar/H2 atmosphere."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_046,"Material Characterization. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained using a Hitachi 8020 equipped with a field-emission gun. Low-magnification TEM measurement, highresolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images, elemental mapping, and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) images were recorded on an FEI Talos F200x. Spherical-aberrationcorrected TEM images were characterized using a probe sphericalaberration corrector on a JEM-ARM300 thermal-field-emission microscope. Using the tapping mode, atomic force microscopy (AFM) was captured on a Bruker scanning probe microscope."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_047,"Raman spectroscopy analysis was carried out on a spectrometer (Jobin-Yvon LabRAM HR EVOLUTION micro Raman) with a laser wavelength of 532 nm. The XRD patterns were tested on a spectrometer (Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer) with Cu Kα radiation (λ = 1.5418 Å). The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectrum was collected on a Thermo Escalab 250xl instrument with an excitation source of monochromatized Al Kα (hυ = 1486.6 eV). The mass of the Pt content was determined through an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, Agilent 720)."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_048,"The X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) data were collected on the BL14W1 of the Shanghai Synchrotron Radiation Facility (SSRF), China. During XAFS measurements, the absorption edge (E0) position was calibrated using a Pt foil. Data analysis and fitting were performed with Athena in the Demeter package."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_049,"Electrochemical Measurement. All electrochemical tests were carried out with a typical three-electrode system in N2-saturated 1.0 M KOH solution on the CHI 760E workstation (Chenhua, China). A carbon rod and a Hg/HgO electrode were used as the counter electrode and the reference electrode, respectively. The as-prepared samples were cut into 0.5 × 0.5 cm 2 as the working electrode. The Pt/ C was mixed with 1 mL of water/ethanol/5% Nafion (v/v/v, 14:5:1) and sonicated for 5 min to prepare the catalyst ink. Then, 10 μL of ink solution was dropped onto the surface of the glassy carbon electrode (GCE) to prepare the comparative sample."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_050,"The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were tested by the CHI 760E instrument at the scanning rate of 5 mV s -1 with 90% IR compensation. The electrochemical durability test was performed at room temperature by applying cyclic voltammetry (CV) sweeps at a scan rate of 100 mV s -1 for 5000 cycles. Chronoamperometry tests were also used to prove the durability of NiPt, and the time-dependent voltage was recorded under a current density of 10 mA cm -2 for 21 h. EIS measurement was recorded in the frequency range from 0.01 to 100 kHz at an amplitude of 5 mV."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_051,"Centered at the open-circuit voltage, the doublelayer capacitance (Cdl) was calculated by recording the cyclic voltammetry curves at different scanning rates (20, 40, 60, 80, 100, and 120 mV s -1 ). The potential was converted to RHE by the equation E(RHE) = E(Hg/HgO) + 0.0591 pH + 0.098 V."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_052,"DFT Calculations. All first-principles calculations are performed using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) 53,54 in a spinpolarized manner. The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with the functionality described by the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional 55 was used for structure optimization. The projector augmented wave (PAW) method was used to describe the wave function of the core region, while the valence wave function was extended to a linear combination of plane waves with 400 eV cutoff energy. The DFT-D correction 56 was employed for the correct description of dispersion interaction."
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_054,"SEM images of the Ni(OH)2 template, Pt-Ni(OH)2, NiPt, and NiPt after the stability test; AFM images of PtNi(OH)2 and NiPt; transmission electron microscopy image, HRTEM image, SAED pattern, and elemental mapping of Pt-Ni(OH)2; EDS results of Pt-Ni(OH)2 samples and NiPt nanosheets; XPS patterns of Ni(OH)2 and Pt-Ni(OH)2 samples; CV curves with different scanning rates of NiPt, Ni, Pt-Ni(OH)2, and Pt/C samples; XRD pattern, XPS survey, Ni 2p XPS spectra, and Pt 4f XPS spectra of NiPt nanosheets on Ni foam after the stability test; GI-XRD pattern of NiPt nanosheets; amount of H2 measured by a drainage method; and comparison of HER performances for NiPt with the reported HER catalysts in alkaline electrolytes (PDF)"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,7,7,P19_p7_055,"■ AUTHOR INFORMATION Corresponding Authors Liangqing Zhu - Technical Center for Multifunctional Magneto-Optical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China; Email: lqzhu@ ee.ecnu.edu.cn"
