P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,8,8,P19_p8_059,"Kai Jiang - Technical Center for Multifunctional MagnetoOptical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China Liyan Shang - Technical Center for Multifunctional MagnetoOptical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China; orcid.org/0000-0003-2341-3978"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,8,8,P19_p8_060,"Yawei Li - Technical Center for Multifunctional MagnetoOptical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China; orcid.org/0000-0001-8776-5687 Jinzhong Zhang - Technical Center for Multifunctional Magneto-Optical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China; orcid.org/00000003-1511-4281"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,8,8,P19_p8_061,"Junhao Chu - Technical Center for Multifunctional MagnetoOptical Spectroscopy (Shanghai), Engineering Research Center of Nanophotonics & Advanced Instrument (Ministry of Education), Department of Physics, School of Physics and Electronic Science, East China Normal University, Shanghai 200241, China; Collaborative Innovation Center of Extreme Optics, Shanxi University, Taiyuan, Shanxi 030006, China Complete contact information is available at: https://pubs.acs.org/10.1021/acsaem.2c02793 Author Contributions"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,8,8,P19_p8_062,"L.H., C.J., L.Z., and Z.H. initiated and designed the experiments and wrote the manuscript. L.H., J.Z., and Y.L. conducted and gave discussion about the theoretical calculation. L.H., C.J., J.T., and X.X. carried out the experiment for sample fabrications. K.J., L.S., Z.H., and J.C. gave the experimental analyses and the underlying mechanism. All of the authors contributed to the manuscript preparation. Notes The authors declare no competing financial interest. ■ ACKNOWLEDGMENTS"
P19,ae2c02793,ae2c02793.pdf,8,8,P19_p8_063,"L.H. and C.J. contributed equally to this work. This work was financially supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant Nos. 62090013, 61974043, 61974044, and 62074058), the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2019YFB2203403), the Projects of Science and Technology Commission of Shanghai Municipality (Grant Nos. 21JC1402100 and 19511120100), and the Program for Professor of Special Appointment (Eastern Scholar) at Shanghai Institutions of Higher Learning and Shanghai Pujiang Program (20PJ1403600). ■ REFERENCES Turner, J. A. Sustainable hydrogen production. Science 2004, 305, 972-974."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_000,"www.acsami.org Research Article Regulating Hollow Carbon Cage Supported NiCo Alloy Nanoparticles for Efficient Electrocatalytic Hydrogen Evolution Reaction Bo Qi, † Wen Chang, † Qixin Xu, Luran Jiang, Sai An, Jin-Feng Chu,* and Yu-Fei Song* Cite This: ACS Appl. Mater. Interfaces 2023, 15, 12078-12087 ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations *sı Supporting Information"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_001,"ABSTRACT: The NiCo alloy is one of the most promising alternatives to the noble-metal electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER); however, its performance is largely restricted by insufficient active sites and low surface area. Here, we fabricated a hierarchical hollow carbon cage supported NiCo alloy (denoted as HC NiCo/C) and a bulk NiCo alloy (denoted as NiCo) by reduction of a partially ZIF-67 etched ZIF-67@NiCoLDH (LDH = layered double hydroxide) precursor and a fully ZIF67 etched NiCo-LDH precursor, respectively."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_002,"The as-prepared HC NiCo/C, in which the Ni29Co71 alloy nanocrystals with an average 6 nm size were encapsulated in graphitic carbon layers, provided a vastly increased electrochemically active surface area (ca. 13 times than the NiCo) and abundant catalytic active sites, which resulted in"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_003,"a higher HER performance with an overpotential of 99 mV than the 198 mV for NiCo at 10 mA cm -2 . Detailed experimental results suggested that only the HC NiCo/C possessed the active alloy surface composed of unsaturated Ni 0 and Co 0 atoms, and both the metal-support interaction and alloying effect influenced the electronic structure of Co and Ni in HC NiCo/C, whereas the NiCo exhibited pure Ni surface. Theoretical calculations further revealed the Ni29Co71 alloy surface in HC NiCo/C possessed the appropriate adsorption energy of the intermediate state (adsorbed H*)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_004,"This work provided new insight into the construction of the stable small-sized bimetallic alloy nanocatalysts by regulating the reduction precursors. KEYWORDS: bimetallic alloy, hollow structure, layered double hydroxides, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, hydrogen evolution reaction 1. INTRODUCTION"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_005,"The development of highly efficient electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in alkaline media is extremely vital for boosting electrochemical energy conversion and alleviating the overcombustion of fossil fuels by facilitating the production of hydrogen as an ideal energy carrier. 1,2 Currently, many precious metals (Pt, 3 Ru, 4 Au, 5 etc.) based electrocatalysts still dominate among the available electrode materials, while the widespread and large-scale applications are restricted by their scarcity, high cost, and inferior durability. Thus, it is imperative to design and manufacture HER electrocatalysts that can effectively replace precious metalbased electrode materials."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_006,"6-8 Among them, iron, cobalt, nickel, and other transition metals have been considered as potential substitutes. 9 The Ni-based materials are one of the most widely used commercial electrocatalysts for HER due to their high abundance on Earth, good electronic conductivity, chemical stability, and relatively high overall efficiency. 10-12 Among Nibased materials, NiCo alloys can perform considerable activity due to the synergistic effect of bimetals which work by tuning the electronic structure of active sites and then optimizing the free energy of hydrogen adsorption."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,1,P20_p1_007,"13-15 In order to further improve the electrocatalytic performance, the construction of the small NiCo alloy nanoparticles is prone to be a reasonable way for increasing the specific surface area and thereby expose more catalytic active sites. 16,17 However, the alloy nanoparticles are unstable and strongly prefer spontaneously aggregating during catalysis. 18 Recently, Zhao et al. synthesized porous N-doped carbon supported NiCo alloy nanoparticles by carbonizing reduction of the Ni 2+ soaked ZnCo bimetallic zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs). The NiCo nanoalloy catalysts exhibited good stability and excellent catalytic HER performance for ammonia borane hydrolysis."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,1,2,P20_p1_008,"19 Nevertheless, it still has great potential in further reducing the particle size and regulating the chemical composition of the catalytic active sites Received: January 11, 2023 Accepted: February 14, 2023 Published: February 27, 2023 on the alloy surface. For this purpose, the reduction precursors with specific compositions and rational structures are indispensable."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_009,"Layered double hydroxides (LDHs) are an effective precursor for the alloy topological transformation owing to their uniformly distributed divalent and trivalent cations, versatile chemical composition, and variable structural architectures. 20,21 The LDHs derived alloys enable close interaction between two metal elements and positive synergism for catalytic activity. Engineering the LDHs/ZIFs hierarchical structures have been reported by Lou et al. 22 and further be applied in photocatalysis, 23,24 pseudocapacitance, 25 zinc-air batteries, 26 and electrocatalytic reactions."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_010,"27 Through regulating the added amount of Ni(NO3)2, the LDHs/ZIFs could be obtained with unique hollow or yolk-shell morphologies, which bring numerous advantages like large surface area, low density, multiple interfaces, as well as a given Ni:Co ratio. 28 Therefore, it is interesting to use these LDHs/ZIFs as reduction precursors for the preparation of NiCo alloy nanocatalysts with small alloy size, stable supporter, fully exposed active sites, and controllable alloy composition."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_011,"Herein, we fabricated a NiCo bimetallic alloy electrocatalyst with hollow cage morphology (denoted as HC NiCo/C), in which the 6 nm NiCo alloy nanoparticles were uniformly distributed onto the hollow carbon cage and enwrapped by the graphene layers. The detailed morphological characterization and control materials suggested this unique morphology could only be obtained by applying the yolk-shell structured ZIF67@NiCo-LDH as a precursor, but neither the bare ZIF-67 nor the hollow cage NiCo-LDH could be. Moreover, ICP and XAFS analysis revealed that the accurate composition of this alloy was Ni29Co71 and the metallic surface defects were composed of both the unsaturated Ni 0 and Co 0 atoms."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_012,"Making full use of the merits of carbon supported hollow cage morphology, like large surface area, the defect sites, and the stable carbon supports, the HC NiCo/C showed an excellent HER performance. DFT calculations revealed that the alloying effect from the composition of Ni29Co71 alloy further enhanced HER performance. 2. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_013,"2.1. Preparation of the Reduction Precursors: ZIF-67, ZIF67@NiCo-LDH, and NiCo-LDH. The ZIF-67 was prepared according to a previous report. 25 Sixty milligrams of the as-synthesized ZIF-67 were dispersed in 30 mL of ethanol and 60 mg of Ni(NO3)2· 6H2O were added subsequently. After stirring for 30 min, the mixture was transferred to the autoclave and kept in the oven at 90 °C for 1 h. The yolk-shell structures of ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH were obtained by centrifugation after cooling the mixture to room temperature. The ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH was washed with ethanol several times and dried at 60 °C under vacuum conditions."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_014,"2.2. Preparation of the Metallic Catalysts: HC Co/C, HC NiCo/C, and NiCo. The HC NiCo/C was prepared by roasting reduction of the precursor ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH in H2/Ar condition. The precursor was placed on a corundum boat and put into a furnace. The furnace was heated up to 450 °C with a heating rate of 2 °C per min and kept for 1 h. Then, the furnace was slowly cooled down to room temperature under the same reduction atmosphere. A dark black powder was obtained and used for preparing the electrode directly. The control catalysts of HC Co/C and NiCo were prepared in the same method by using the precursors of NiCo-LDH and ZIF67, separately."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_015,"Material Characterization. Powder XRD patterns were recorded with a Rigaku XRD-6000 diffractometer under the following conditions: 40 kV, 40 mA, CuKα radiation. SEM images and mapping analytical data were obtained by using a Zeiss Supra 55 scanning electron microscope equipped with an energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) detector. Raman spectroscopy was performed with a Renishaw Raman spectrometer at a laser excitation wavelength of 532 nm. XPS measurements were performed with an AlKα-exciting X-radiation (PHI Quantera SXM). Wide and detailed spectra were collected at 0° takeoff angle, using a fixed analyzer transmission mode with channel widths of 1.0 and 0.1 eV, respectively."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_016,The binding energies were calibrated based on C 1s (284.8 eV). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) was conducted on a JEOL JEM-2100 microscope using an accelerating voltage of 400 kV. The X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) measurements were obtained from the 1W1B beamline of the Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility (BSRF).
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_017,"Electrochemical Measurement. Electrochemical measurements were performed with an Instrument CHI 660E in 1.0 M aqueous KOH solution at room temperature in a three-electrode setup (working electrode: HC NiCo/C, reference electrode: SCE, counter electrode: carbon rod). The working electrodes were prepared as follows: 5 mg of catalysts were dispersed in 1 mL of ethanol under ultrasonication, and then 2 mg of carbon black and 50 μL of Nafion were added to the above solution. The 210 μL mixture was slowly dripped onto the carbon paper with an area of 1 cm 2 and dried under vacuum. Linear sweep voltammetry was performed with a scan rate of 5 mV s -1 ."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_018,EIS measurements were carried out in 1.0 M aqueous KOH at different potentials in the frequency range of 0.1 to 10 5 Hz with an amplitude of 10 mV. The potential versus SCE was converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) via the Nernst equation as follows: ERHE = ESCE + 0.059 pH + E θ SCE.
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_019,"Computational Details. To understand the alloying effect of NiCo on hydrogen evolution reaction, first-principles calculations were carried out, based on the density functional theory (DFT) employing the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) 29 approximation for an exchange-correlation potential in Vienna Ab Initio Simulation Package (VASP). 30,31 To model the (111) and (100) surfaces of Ni and Co, four-layer p(3 × 3) slabs with 15 Å vacuum gap thickness were used. The k-point meshes of 4 × 4 × 1 were used for Brillouin zone integration, and the plane-wave basis set was within the kinetic cutoff energy of 400 eV."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_020,"The atomic positions were relaxed until the force on each atom was less than 0.05 eV/Å, and the convergence tolerance of the energy was set to be 10 -5 eV. To simulate HC NiCo/ C, NiCo, and HC Co/C catalysts, the models of Ni29Co71, pure Ni, and pure Co were built. The Ni29Co71 model was built by substituting partial Co atoms for surface Ni atoms. The adsorption energy of H* (ΔEH*) was calculated using ΔEH* = Esurf+H* - Esurf - 1/2EH2 in which Esurf+H* is the surface adsorbed energy with H*, Esurf is the clean surface energy, and EH2 is the gas-phase hydrogen energy. The H* adsorption Gibbs free energy was calculated with"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,2,P20_p2_021,"where ΔSH is the entropy and ΔEZPE is the zero-point energy. ΔEZPE - TΔSH* = 0.28 eV with the standard condition of T = 300 K. 32 The ΔGH* was obtained using ΔGH* = ΔEH* + 0.28 eV. To estimate the charge transfer after adsorption, we used the Bader's approach as numerically applied by Henklemann's group. 33 To understand the charge redistribution after H* adsorption on Ni29Co71 surfaces, we plotted the charge density difference by subtracting the densities of H* and Ni29Co71 surface from the density of Ni29Co71-H* as expressed below"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,2,3,P20_p2_022,"where ρNi29Co71-H*, ρNi29Co71, and ρH* are the charge densities of the Ni29Co71-H* system, and the corresponding parts of Ni29Co71 alloy and H*, respectively. Ni29Co71(100)-H* and Ni29Co71(111)-H* represented that the Ni29Co71 alloy adsorbed H* through the (100) facets and (111) facets. Scheme 1. Schematic Illustration of the Synthesis Process of HC NiCo/C with Hollow Cage Morphology and NiCo Alloy with Collapsed Aggregation Morphology Figure 1. SEM and HR-TEM images of the precursors and products: (a) ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH and (b, c, f) resultant HC NiCo/C; (d) NiCo-LDH and (e) resultant bulk NiCo alloy. 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,3,3,P20_p3_023,"The synthetic strategies for the hollow cage alloy catalyst HC NiCo/C, and the control catalyst bulk NiCo alloy were illustrated in Scheme 1. First, reduction precursors of ZIF-67@ NiCo-LDH and NiCo-LDH were prepared by taking advantage of Lou's method. 22 The dodecahedral ZIF-67 nanocrystals could be etched under the conditions of Ni(NO3)2 ethanol solution following the formation of hollow cage NiCo-LDH on their surface. The formation mechanism was reported and the etching degree could be controlled by adjusting the equivalent of Ni(NO3)2."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,3,3,P20_p3_024,"22,28 The partially ZIF-67 etched ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH with a yolk-shell structure was obtained by adding one equivalent of Ni(NO3)2 and the fully ZIF-67 etched NiCo-LDH with hollow cage structure was obtained by adding four equivalent of Ni(NO3)2. Subsequently, the above precursors were reduced at 450 °C under H2/Ar flow to obtain the NiCo alloy electrocatalysts: HC NiCo/C and NiCo. In addition, the pristine ZIF-67 was calcinated under the same reductive conditions to obtain HC Co/C as the other control catalyst (Figure S1)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_025,"Figure 2. (a) XRD patterns of NiCo, HC NiCo/C, and metallic Ni (PDF# 04-0850), Co (PDF# 15-0806). XPS spectra of (b) C 1s for HC NiCo/C, (c) Ni 2p, and (d) Co 2p for NiCo and HC NiCo/C, respectively."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_026,"Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) were used to characterize the morphology of precursors and calcined catalysts. The pristine ZIF-67 showed a solid dodecahedron structure with a smooth surface (Figure S2a). With adding 1 equiv of Ni(NO3)2·6H2O, the ZIF-67 was partially etched into a ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH yolk-shell structure. The ZIF-67@ NiCo-LDH retained a dodecahedral shape and got a rough surface (Figure 1a and Figure S2b and S3a). With adding 4 equiv of Ni(NO3)2·6H2O, the fully etched NiCo-LDH was obtained with a hollow cage structure, thicker LDH shell, and more rough surface (Figure 1d and Figure S2c and S3b)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_027,"The composition of these precursors was further characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and FT-IR (Figure S5). The XRD pattern of ZIF-67 exhibited distinctive diffraction peaks, which were in accord with the published results. 22,23 For the NiCoLDH, the XRD diffraction peaks observed at 2θ values of 11.8°, 23.6°, 34.0°, 43.8°, and 60.8° could be indexed successfully to the (003), (006), (012), and (110) plane reflections of the NiCo-LDH phase, respectively. The XRD pattern of ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH displayed a superimposition of the LDH phase and ZIF-67 phase, indicating the ZIF-67 was partially etched, which was consistent with the results of the HR-TEM image."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_028,"FT-IR spectra provided further evidence for the controllable etching degree of ZIF-67 that the NiCo-LDH showed typical peaks of NO3 - intercalated LDH at 1380 and 634 cm -1 , and the ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH showed the same superimposition of LDH and ZIF peaks."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_029,"After calcination, the morphology of the corresponding products showed a significant difference. As shown in the SEM images, both the HC Co/C and HC NiCo/C maintained the dodecahedral shape and their surfaces became rough and composed of small particles (Figure S2d, e). However, for the NiCo alloy composite obtained from NiCo-LDH precursor, the original hollow cage polyhedron structure collapsed and heavily aggregated together, resulting in irregular morphology (Figure 1e and Figure S2f). The TEM image of HC Co/C indicated a hollow structure with little shrinkage in the skeleton."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_030,"A large number of carbon nanotubes were grown on the outer surface, which might be formed by the catalytic effect of Co nanoparticles under a reductive atmosphere (Figure S3c, d). As for the HC NiCo/C, a hollow structure was observed with a relatively rigid shell as compared to the HC Co/C. The morphology discrepancy among the three products implied that the ZIF-67 in precursors played a crucial role in generating the hollow cage structures and the pure hollow NiCo alloy was unstable during the reduction process. The HR-TEM images of HC NiCo/C suggested that the NiCo alloy existed as the nanoparticles in the carbon matrix (Figure 1b and Figure S3e, f)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_031,"These nanoparticles were well dispersed and presented an average size of about 6 nm except for several sporadic large nanoparticles (Figure 1c and Figure S4). Moreover, these nanoparticles revealed distinctive lattice fringes with interplanar spacings of 0.21 nm, which were attributed to the (111) plane of the NiCo alloy (Figure 1f). Besides, the HR-TEM images also showed that these NiCo nanoparticles were spatially separated and supported by the surrounding multilayer graphitic carbon. Raman spectra with the G-band (1580 cm -1 ) and D-band (1350 cm -1 ) peaks further demonstrated the existence of carbon in the HC NiCo/C (Figure S6)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,4,P20_p4_032,"The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images demonstrated the elements of Ni, Co, and C were evenly distributed in the HC NiCo/C (Figure S7). In addition, almost no carbon nanotubes were observed on the surface of HC NiCo/C, thus the possibility that the supported nanoparticles were a mixture of Co and NiCo alloy could be ruled out. Furthermore, the deriving process of HC NiCo/C structure could be proposed that the hollow cage carbon skeleton was converted from the organic ligands in the retained ZIF-67 core and the NiCo alloy nanoparticles were derived from the melting Ni/Co in NiCo-LDH and ZIF-67."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,4,5,P20_p4_033,"Compared with the pure NiCo alloy, the carbon skeleton was soft and stable to maintain the HC morphology, which could effectively expose the catalytic active sites and thus improve the catalytic performance. Figure 3. XANES spectra and Fourier transforms of (a, b) Co K-edge and (c, d) Ni K-edge for Co foil, Ni foil, NiCo, and HC NiCo/C. Schematic illustration of different compositions of the surface defect sites for the proposed alloy crystal structures: (e) both Ni 0 and Co 0 atoms composed in HC NiCo/C surface, and (f) only Ni 0 atoms composed in NiCo surface. The hexagon represented the surface atom layer of alloy structures."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,5,5,P20_p5_034,"The chemical composition and electronic structures of HC NiCo/C were further revealed. The XRD pattern of HC NiCo/C and NiCo showed that the characteristic peaks at 2θ = 44.3°, 51.6°, and 76.1° were corresponding to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of NiCo alloy (Figure 2a), and the HC Co/C gave a broadened peak at 2θ = 44.3° that was assigned to the (111) plane of metallic Co (Figure S8). The peaks of impurities like metallic oxides were not observed in XRD suggesting that the crystalline phase was mainly composed of NiCo alloy. The fact was further confirmed by the result that the HC NiCo/C exhibited magnetic behaviors due to the ferromagnetic properties of NiCo alloy (Figure S9)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,5,5,P20_p5_035,"Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis indicated that the atomic ratio of Ni:Co in HC NiCo/C and NiCo were 29:71 and 72:28, respectively. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to investigate the surface chemical composition and electronic structures of the HC NiCo/C sample. The survey spectrum of HC NiCo/C indicated the presence of C, O, Ni, and Co (Figure S10a). In the C 1s spectra (Figure 2b and Figure S10b), five peaks of HC NiCo/ C at 289.2, 286.7, 285.7, 285.1, and 284.5 eV corresponded to OC-O, CO, C-O, C-C sp 3 , and C-C sp 2 , respectively."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,5,6,P20_p5_036,"HC NiCo/C showed a stronger C-C sp 3 peak but a weaker C-C sp 2 peak compared with HC Co/C, which was caused by the difference in the carbonization degree for graphitic carbon and carbon nanotubes. As shown in Figure 2c, the Ni 2p spectra of HC NiCo/C could be fitted into two shakeup satellites (indicated as ""Sat."") and two spin-orbit doublets, where Ni x+ corresponded to 874.1 and 855.9 eV and Ni 0 corresponded to 871.7 and 853.1 eV. The positive shift for the peak of Ni 0 was observed for HC NiCo/C compared with NiCo, indicating that partial electrons were transferred fromNi elements to Co elements or the carbon cage. The Co 2p b"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_037,Figure 4. HER performance of HC NiCo/C and NiCo in 1 M KOH: (a) LSV curves. (b) Tafel plots. (c) Nyquist plots. (d) Determination of the double-layer capacitance Cdl.
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_038,"spectra were applied to further understand the electronic structure of HC NiCo/C. In the Co 2p spectra of HC NiCo/C (Figure 2d), the peak at around 778.7 eV was corresponding to Co 0 , and the two peaks at 797.7 and 782.4 eV can be ascribed to Co x+ . The existence of Co x+ and Ni x+ originated from the surface oxygen adhesion of metallic Co and Ni caused by exposure to air. Compared with the Co in bulk NiCo alloy and the single metal Co in HC Co/C, the NiCo alloy in HC NiCo/ C showed a positive shift (0.9 eV) and a negative shift (0.3 eV), respectively (Figure S10c)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_039,These implied that the carbon cage and the Ni elements in HC NiCo/C worked as electron donors and the Co elements worked as electron acceptors. Both the metal-support interaction and alloying effect influenced the electronic structure of Co and Ni in HC NiCo/C.
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_040,"To further disclose the local structure information on Co and Ni elements in the as-prepared catalysts, X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectroscopy was performed (Figure 3a, c). Co K-edge and Ni K-edge XANES spectra in both HC NiCo/C and NiCo showed a similar pre-edge feature at 7712 eV with the reference metallic Co foil and Ni foil, suggesting that both Co and Ni were transformed from cationic states to metallic form. As shown in Figure 3c, the absorption edge of Ni K-edge XANES in NiCo and HC NiCo/ C were shifted to the lower energy compared with the Ni foil sample, indicating the electron transfer to the Ni atoms, which resulted in a lower valence state than Ni 0 ."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_041,"Meanwhile, compared with the white-line peak of foil references, the NiCo showed a similar behavior while the HC NiCo/C presented an increased peak intensity. The stronger absorption peak verified the strong metal-support interaction between the metallic species and carbon supports in HC NiCo/C. The Ni and Co K-edge k 3 χ (k) oscillation curves of the NiCo and HC NiCo/C showed weaker amplitudes of k values as compared to the Ni and Co foils, implying a different coordination environment in these catalysts."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_042,"The corresponding Fourier transform of extended X-ray adsorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra was further used to study the detailed coordination structures of Co and Ni atoms (Figure 3b, d). In the R-space spectra of NiCo and HC NiCo/C, the main peak at 2.17 Å was ascribed to the Co-Ni or Ni-Co coordination shell, which was consistent with the foil samples, revealing the formation of NiCo alloy. The Co-M or Ni-M shell (M = Ni, Co) in HC NiCo/C exhibited a decreased intensity of peak compared with that of NiCo, and the coordination number (Table S1) of the Co-M shell was determined to be 6.3 in HC NiCo/C, significantly smaller than the coordination number in NiCo (12)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_043,"These results demonstrated the existence of metal defects in the HC NiCo/C catalysts. In this case, the defects were attributed to the unsaturated coordinated atoms located at the edges, surfaces, or surface steps of nanocrystals. Based on the results of R-space spectra, the coordination of Co atoms was saturated and Ni atoms were unsaturated in NiCo, suggesting that almost all Co atoms located inside of the crystal and surface defects in NiCo were composed of pure Ni atoms (Figure 3e). For the HC NiCo/C, both the Ni and Co showed unsaturated coordination, inferring that the surface of alloy nanoparticles in HC NiCo/C consisted of the unsaturated Co 0 and Ni 0 atoms (Figure 3f)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,6,6,P20_p6_044,"The different surface composition was due to the different reduction potential and molar ratio of Co and Ni species. As for the NiCo-LDH precursor with a low Co:Ni ratio, the small amount of Co ions was quickly reduced, while the Ni ions were slowly reduced. Thus, only the Ni ions remained when forming the surface layer of the bulk NiCo, leading to a pure Ni surface. As for the ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH precursor with a high Co:Ni ratio, Co and Ni ions coexisted when forming the surface layer, resulting in an alloy surface composition of HC NiCo/C. Moreover, the small size effect of NiCo alloy particles in HC NiCo/C also promoted the surface exposure of Ni 0 and Co 0 ."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_045,"Figure 5. Free-energy diagram of HER on (a) (100) facets and (d) (111) facets for the HC NiCo/C and NiCo materials, simulated with models of their active surface Ni29Co71 and pure Ni. Blue, pink, and white spheres indicate Ni, Co, and H atoms, respectively. Charge density difference of the adsorbed H* on the (b) Ni29Co71(100) facets, (e) Ni29Co71(111) facets at top views. The plane-averaged charge density difference Δρ (Z) along the Z-direction normal to the (c) Ni29Co71(100) facets and (f) Ni29Co71(111) facets, the insets in (c) and (f) are the side views of Ni29Co71(100)- H* and Ni29Co71(111)-H*."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_046,"The HER performance of the HC NiCo/C, NiCo, and HC Co/C was evaluated in 1 M KOH aqueous solution using a typical three-electrode system. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) showed all corresponding composites exhibited excellent electrocatalytic HER performance. As shown in the polarization curves after iR-compensation (Figure 4a), the HC NiCo/C exhibited the best performance with a low overpotential of 99 mV at a current density j = 10 mA cm -2 , which was a better performance than most nonprecious-metal catalysts (Table S2). In contrast, other composites showed a relatively higher overpotential of 198 mV for NiCo and 196 mV for HC Co/C (Figure S12a)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_047,"Tafel slope was an important parameter to describe the effect of potential or overpotential on steady-state current density and could gain further insights into the kinetics of the reaction. As shown in Figure 4b, the HC NiCo/C afforded the lowest Tafel slope of 96 mV/dec, which was lower than the NiCo (127 mV/dec) and HC Co/C (151 mV/dec), suggesting that the hydrogen evolution process for HC NiCo/C followed the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism and the rate-determining step was the hydrogen generation (Figure S12b)."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_048,The excellent kinetic advantage and validated favorable HER activity of HC NiCo/C may attribute to the presence of unsaturated coordination of Ni or Co sites to provide more accessible H adsorption sites. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out to further reveal the catalytic behavior and internal resistance of the composite electrodes (Figure 4c and Figure S12c). The Nyquist plots indicated the expected semicircular features associated with charge-transfer resistances in the HER process.
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_049,"The HC NiCo/C displayed a modest charge-transfer resistance (Rct) compared with the other two catalysts, demonstrating that both the graphitic carbon shell and the alloying effect promoted a faster electron transfer process."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_050,"To illustrate the effect of hollow morphology and alloy site on the electrocatalytic activity, the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was evaluated by electrochemical double layer capacitance (Cdl). The Cdl was obtained by CV scanning in the appropriate range at different speeds (Figure S13). As shown in Figure 4d, the HC Co/C showed a 26.96 mF cm -2 Cdl, which was ca. 13 times than the collapsed NiCo with 2.13 mF cm -2 Cdl. This indicated that the presence of a hollow carbon skeleton well separated the NiCo alloy nanoparticles and exposed more active sites, which was conducive to the improvement of HER activity."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_051,"It was interesting that the Cdl of HC Co/C (31.06 mF cm -2 ) was slightly higher than that of HC NiCo/C, whereas the activity of HC Co/C was much lower than that of HC NiCo/C (Figure S12d). This contradiction indicated that the large surface area was not the only influencing factor, the alloying effect also had a contribution to the activity. Moreover, to prove the durability of the catalysts, the stability test was carried out at a constant voltage of -1.167 V (vs SCE) for 20 h (Figure S14). All the electrocatalysts exhibited excellent durability."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,7,7,P20_p7_052,"The LSV curves of HC NiCo/C recorded before and after cycling 20 h were almost overlap, indicating that the HC NiCo/C composite was stable in an alkaline solution."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_053,"For the purpose of understanding the alloying effect of the HC NiCo/C on HER performance, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were used to calculate the H* absorption free energy (ΔGH*) of different models of Ni29Co71, Ni, and Co which were constructed to simulate the HC NiCo/C, NiCo, and HC Co/C materials. Two crystal facets of HC NiCo/C including (100) and (111) were considered according to the HR-TEM and XRD analysis (Figure 1f and Figure 2a). The entire HER pathway consists of an initial state (H + + e - ), an intermediate state (adsorbed H*), and a final state (1/2H2), and the intermediate state ΔGH* represents the catalytic activity for HER."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_054,"For the hydrogen generation reaction, the |ΔGH*| of the catalyst should be close to 0 to be optimal, which represents a fast proton/electron-transfer step as well as a fast hydrogen release process. On (100) facets (Figure 5a and Figure S15a), the ΔGH* values of -0.22, and 0.28 eV were obtained for NiCo, and HC Co/C, respectively, suggesting that Ni had better adsorption properties of hydrogen than HC Co/C. The HC NiCo/C exhibited the smallest absolute ΔGH* (-0.12 eV), indicating that the Ni atoms in HC NiCo/C enhanced the adsorbability of hydrogen for Co atoms, therefore improving HER performance."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_055,"On the (111) facets (Figure 5d and Figure S15b), both Ni and Co had negative ΔGH* values (-0.25 eV vs. -0.66 eV), implying an energetically favorable interaction with hydrogen. The HC NiCo/C showed the weakest interaction with hydrogen among the three models, as indicated by the exothermic ΔGH* of -0.13 eV, which facilitated hydrogen release. Therefore, the modified electronic structure of Ni in the HC NiCo/C catalyst enables the Co site to achieve an optimum Co-H binding value, which might lead to rapid hydrogen adsorption and H2 release process, ultimately accelerating the HER process."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_056,"The charge density differences of two adsorption structures of Ni29Co71(100)-H* and Ni29Co71(111)-H* were also calculated. As illustrated in Figure 5b, e, the electron transfer can be more clearly visualized in the differential charge density of the adsorption structures. The electron accumulation on H* and the electron depletion on metal atoms indicated that the enriched electrons were transferred from metal atoms to H*. The enriched electrons around H* were beneficial to forming an H-H bond, thereby promoting H2 production. The Zdirection planar-averaged charge density difference (Δρ) was employed for further quantitative analysis of charge accumulation/depletion."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_057,"As depicted in Figure 5c, f, the positive and negative peaks represented gaining electrons and losing electrons, respectively. The electrons were almost exclusively transferred from the first layer of metal atoms on the surface of the Ni29Co71(100) or (111) facets to the H*, which suggested that the surface metal atoms determined the HER activity. The Ni29Co71(111)-H* system showed two main peaks (Δρ = 0.0039 e/Å and -0.0024 e/Å), which were higher than that of the Ni29Co71(100)-H* system (Δρ = 0.0022 e/Å and -0.0013 e/Å). The higher peaks demonstrated that more charge transfer occurred in the Ni29Co71(111)-H system."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_058,"Therefore, the exposed (111) facet of HC NiCo/C was more catalytically active than the (100) facets toward HER. 4. CONCLUSIONS"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_059,"In summary, with carefully regulating the yolk-shell structured ZIF-67@NiCo-LDH precursors, we successfully fabricated the HC NiCo/C, in which the NiCo alloy nanoparticles were well dispersed onto the hollow carbon cage support. The NiCo alloys nanoparticles showed an average 6 nm size with clearly observed metallic crystal lattice fringes. For comparison, heavily aggregated NiCo bulk alloys were obtained by applying the NiCo-LDHs as precursors. Both the Ni 0 and Co 0 were indicated in the XPS spectra of the HC NiCo/C. The Co 0 accepted electrons from Ni 0 and carbon support due to the alloying effect and metal-support interaction."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_060,"Moreover, the EXAFS spectra suggested that among the three metal structures, only the HC NiCo/C possessed the alloy surface composed of the unsaturated Co 0 and Ni 0 atoms simultaneously. As a noble-metal free electrocatalyst, the HC NiCo/C exhibited high reactivity and stability toward HER with a low overpotential of 99 mV together with a Tafel slope of 96 mV dec -1 at 10 mA cm -2 . The good HER performance may be attributed to the hollow structure bringing large surface area, enhanced electrical conductivity, active alloy surface composition, and electronic structure variation of Ni and Co."
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_062,"General Methods, synthesis, electrochemical measurement, computational details, characterization results and performance test (PDF) ■ AUTHOR INFORMATION Corresponding Authors Jin-Feng Chu - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China; orcid.org/0000-0003-28183630; Email: chujf@mail.buct.edu.cn Yu-Fei Song - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China; orcid.org/0000-0003-13090626; Phone: +86 10 64431832; Email: songyf@ mail.buct.edu.cn Authors"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,8,P20_p8_063,"Bo Qi - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China; orcid.org/0000-0002-0381534X Wen Chang - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China Qixin Xu - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China"
P20,am3c00385,am3c00385.pdf,8,9,P20_p8_064,"Luran Jiang - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China Sai An - State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, P. R. China Complete contact information is available at: https://pubs.acs.org/10.1021/acsami.3c00385 Author Contributions † B.Q. and W.C. contributed equally. Notes The authors declare no competing financial interest. ■ ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This research was supported by the National Nature Science Foundation of China (22101017, 22178019, 22288102) and the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities (XK1802-6, XK1803-05, XK1902)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_001,"1 Key Laboratory for Special Functional Materials of Ministry of Education, National & Local Joint Engineering Research Center for High-efficiency Display and Lighting Technology, School of Nanoscience andMaterials Engineering, Henan University, Zhengzhou, China 2 School of Materials Science and Engineering, Shaanxi University of Science and Technology, Xi'an, China 3 Institute of EnergyMaterials Science, University of Shanghai for Science and Technology, Shanghai, China 4 Institute for Superconducting and ElectronicMaterials, University of Wollongong, Wollongong, New SouthWales, Australia"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_002,Correspondence: JunMei (meijun@henu.edu.cn) Jing Shang (shangjing@sust.edu.cn) Shixue Dou (shi@uow.edu.au) Received: 29 August 2026 Revised: 6 October 2026 Accepted: 15 October 2026 Keywords: alloy | hydrogen | nickel | reconstruction | spillover ABSTRACT
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_003,"Hydrogen spillover can decouple elementary steps in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), yet conventional bimetallic catalysts often suffer from discontinuous interfaces that block spillover pathways. Herein, an interfacial hydrogen spillover mechanism is engineered in a Pd-PdNi-Ni catalyst via a partial interface alloying strategy for pH-universal hydrogen evolution. The graded PdNi interface serves as a recombination sink integrating two synergistic pathways: primary spillover, in which strongly adsorbed H* generated on Ni spontaneously migrates across the interface, and secondary spillover, in which H* produced on Pd also diffuses to the same interfacial sites."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_004,"The dual spillover is driven by a favorable hydrogen intermediate gradient resulting from Ni-to-Pd electron transfer and an optimized d-band center. The optimal catalyst delivers ultralow overpotentials of 60 mV (alkaline) and 37 mV (acidic) at 10 mA cm-2 , with robust durability, and the corresponding anion exchange membrane electrolyzer exhibits 1.73 V at 0.5 A cm-2 and 452 h stability in simulated seawater. Operando Raman spectroscopy detects Pd-H and Ni-H vibrations, providing direct evidence for hydrogen migration from both domains to the interface, and theoretical calculations confirm the electronic origin and spillover pathways."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_005,This work demonstrates a rational design for high-performance HER catalysts by harnessing dual hydrogen spillover. 1 Introduction
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_006,"The growing demand for sustainable hydrogen production via water splitting has positioned hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) as one of crucial research topics in electrocatalysis. While platinum-based metals remains the benchmark catalyst, its scarcity motivates the exploration for earth-abundant alternatives. Nickel (Ni) is a promising candidate, yet its intrinsic HER activity is greatly limited by excessively strong hydrogen adsorption, which could be well buffered in the presence of hydrogen spillover [1]. Hydrogen spillover refers to the surface-mediated migration of dissociated hydrogen atoms from a primary active metal onto an adjacent secondary metal or support."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_007,"In a bimetallic HER system, spillover decouples the elementary reaction steps: one component (e.g., Pd) optimizes the Volmer step (proton reduction or water dissociation), while the adjacent component (e.g., Ni) facilitates the Tafel or Heyrovsky recombination step [2]. Without this synergy, monometallic Ni suffers from rapid surface saturation with adsorbed hydrogen (H*), increasing the activation energy for H─H bond formation [3]. Thus, engineering efficient hydrogen spillover is not merely an enhancement but a critical strategy to break traditional scaling relations, enabling high activity at reduced noble-metal loading and ensuring rapid gas desorption."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,1,1,P21_p1_008,"All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence technologies or similar technologies. How to cite: Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2026, e4353715 doi.org/10.1002/anie.4353715"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_009,"Despite major progress, practical spillover-enhanced HER catalysts face critical bottlenecks. First, controlling interface architecture remains challenging. An ideal spillover system requires a contiguous and well-defined pathway from the hydrogen source to the hydrogen sink. However, disconnected noble-metal and base-metal domains always exist in the conventional bimetallic system, rendering the spillover effect negligible. Second, the dynamic surface reconstruction of bimetallic catalysts under operating conditions remains poorly understood [4]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_010,"For example, in alkaline media, the Ni center is prone to reversible hydroxyl (OH*) adsorption at moderate overpotentials, which can physically block spillover pathways; in acidic media, surface oxidation or metal leaching alters the interfacial electronic structure over time [5, 6]. These dynamic changes are rarely captured by ex situ characterization, leading to a significant gap between the pristine structure and the actual state during HER [7]. Third, the pH-dependence of spillover kinetics is a largely unexplored field. The solvation environment, the nature of the proton donor (H3O+ in acid vs."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_011,"H2O in base), and the surface charge state could modulate the energetics of hydrogen adsorption, diffusion, and recombination. A spillover architecture optimized for acidic conditions may completely fail in alkaline electrolytes because the rate-determining step (RDS) shifts from the recombination step to the initial water-dissociation step. Consequently, a systematic study is highly required on the construction of a bimetallic architecture with tunable chemical states and robust interfaces, and meanwhile decouples the pH-dependent spillover behavior, electronic structure evolution, and interfacial reconstruction [8, 9]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_012,"Motivated by these considerations, a partial interface alloying strategy is proposed to obtain a series of Pd-PdNi-Ni bimetallic catalyst systems consisting of Pd and Ni centers in the presence of PdNi alloy interface via a combination of controllable micelleassisted co-assembly synthesis with thermal reduction. From a mechanistic viewpoint, the rational design on the spilloverintensified HER activity depends on a favorable Gibbs free energy gradient for hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH) across the bimetallic interface. For a monometallic Ni center, ΔGH is moderately negative, which often causes too strong binding that slows recombination."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_013,"If a partial interface alloying is employed to form an unique Pd-PdNi-Ni architecture, Pd-Ni interface exhibits near-optimal ΔGH* and high hydrogen diffusivity. Therefore, this work provide a design principle that the alloying degree should optimize the gradient in H* chemical potential, offer a kinetic model for efficient hydrogen production via spillover design. 2 Results andDiscussion"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_014,"As illustrated in Figure 1, a triblock copolymer P123 (EO20PO70EO20) was employed as a soft template to synthesize Pd-PdNi-Ni bimetallic materials through a controllable micelleassisted co-assembly synthesis accompanied by a thermal reduction. This amphiphilic block copolymer self-assembles into spherical micelles in a water-isopropanol mixed solvent, with the hydrophilic poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO) blocks forming the corona and the hydrophobic poly(propylene oxide) (PPO) blocks constituting the core. Hence, this well-defined micellar architecture provides a confined nanoreactor environment essential for achieving spatial separation of metal precursors and controlling nucleation kinetics."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_015,"In a typical synthesis, an appropriate amount of P123 polymer was first dissolved in a water-isopropanol mixed solvent, and then nickel chloride tetrahydrate (NiCl2 4H2O), palladium chloride (PdCl2), and sodium acetate trihydrate (C2H3O2Na 3H2O) were added under stirring. Here, sodium acetate acts as a pH buffer to prevent premature Ni 2+ hydrolysis, which provides acetate ligands to modulate reduction kinetics, and stabilizes the colloidal system. Subsequently, ethylene glycol was introduced as both a mild reducing agent and a co-solvent to swell the micellar corona and facilitate mass transport."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_016,"After the mixture was treated at 180◦C for 2 h and annealed at 500◦C for 2 h under an Ar/H2 atmosphere, the polymeric template was removed and the partial alloying reaction between Pd and Ni atoms occurred, yielding a graded PdNi alloy structure intimately coupled with a metallic Ni substrate, which is a partial interface alloying architecture that enables efficient hydrogen spillover."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_017,"To obtain a series of Pd-PdNi-Ni bimetallic catalysts, the molar ratio of Pd 2+ to Ni 2+ was precisely controlled within a defined range from 0:1 to 1:5, in which the Pd ratios in Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2, and PdPdNi-Ni-3 catalysts, are 11.57%, 12.73%, and 12.99%, respectively, as confirmed by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) analysis (Table S1)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_018,"It is expected that this design on a Pd-Ni heterostructured electrocatalyst for promoting dual hydrogen spillover mechanism during HER. Specifically, at the Ni center, water molecules preferentially undergo Volmer dissociation to generate abundant adsorbed hydrogen (*H), yet the strong hydrogen adsorption on Ni inhibits direct H2 evolution, driving spontaneous primary spillover of adsorbed H from the Ni domain across the Pd-Ni interfacial boundary. Meanwhile, the Pd site also activates water splitting to produce surface-bound *H, which migrates toward the Pd-Ni interface via secondary spillover."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_019,"Hence, the Pd-Ni heterointerface serves as the optimal active sink to accumulate migrated *H species, where adsorbed hydrogen proceeds through either Tafel or Heyrovsky pathways to recombine and desorb as molecular H2, completing the overall alkaline HER cycle. The divergent hydrogen adsorption strength between Pd and Ni creates a thermodynamic driving force for directional *H migration, and the synergistic spillover effect substantially lowers the energy barrier for hydrogen production at the Pd-Ni interface."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_020,"As confirmed by the x-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S1), during the solvothermal stage, Pd species are preferentially reduced to metallic Pd particles, whereas Ni 2+ tends to form nickel hydroxide species under the same conditions. This kinetic selectivity is the critical first step in constructing the desired partial interface alloying architecture. After thermal reduction process, as shown in Figure 2a, the phase composition of PdPdNi-Ni catalysts varies systematically with different Pd-to-Ni ratios."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_021,"Specifically, in the absence of Pd center, three distinct diffraction peaks appear at 44.5◦ , 51.9◦ , and 76.3◦ , which can be well indexed to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of face-centered cubic (fcc) metallic Ni (PDF #04-0850), indicating the successful formation of crystalline Ni species [10]. Upon the introduction of Pd center, all Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts exhibit three additional diffraction peaks at approximately 41.5◦ , 48.3◦ , and 70.8◦ , corresponding to the PdNi alloy phase (PDF #65-9444), which confirms the partial interface alloying between Pd and Ni [11]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_022,"Moreover, with increasing Pd ratio from Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 to PdPdNi-Ni-3, the (111) diffraction peak gradually shifts toward lower angles, from∼42.8◦ for Pd-PdNi-Ni-1 to ∼40.7◦ for Pd-PdNi-Ni3, accompanied by a progressive increase in peak intensity. As the Pd content in the alloy phase increases, more Ni lattice sites are replaced by Pd, leading to a systematic expansion of the unit cell (Figure 2b)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_023,"To quantitatively resolve the phase proportion variations between the PdNi alloy phase and the metallic Ni phase in samples with different Pd contents, XRD Rietveld refinement analysis was further conducted (Figure S2), which reveal that with increasing Pd incorporation, the proportion of the PdNi alloy phase progressively increases, with the PdNi:Ni ratio evolving from an initial value of 39:61 to 43:57 and finally to 73:27. In contrast, these typical peaks associated with metallic Ni center exhibit negligible shift, and their intensities decrease as the Pd ratio increases, reflecting the gradual consumption of free Ni to form the alloy phase."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_024,"These observations indicate that the fraction of the alloy phase increases with Pd incorporation and gradually becomes the dominant phase, while the content of unalloyed Ni correspondingly diminishes. This compositional evolution is precisely the desired outcome for a partial interface alloying strategy: the alloy phase provides the electronic modulation and spillover source-sink gradient, while the residual metallic Ni substrate maintains structural integrity and serves as the primary hydrogen recombination sink."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,2,P21_p2_025,"To quantitatively evaluate the variation of heterointerfaces among the three catalysts, the volumetric heterointerface density (ρv) was approximately estimated by considering both the phase fractions and characteristic grain size. The heterointerface density was estimated according to (ρv = 2f1f2/d), where (f1) and (f2) represent the volume fractions of metallic Ni and PdNi phases obtained from Rietveld refinement, respectively, and (d) denotes the average grain size determined from statistical SEM analysis. Based on these parameters, the calculated (ρv) values for Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2, and PdPdNi-Ni-3 are 0.00864, 0.01137, and 0.00842 nm-1 , respectively (Table S2)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,2,3,P21_p2_026,"Notably, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 exhibits the highest heterointerface density, whereas further increasing Pd content leads to a decrease in heterointerface density. FIGURE 1 Schematic illustration of the synthesis of the Pd-PdNi-Ni bimetallic catalyst system, and the possible hydrogen spillover pathways over Pd-Ni interface."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,3,3,P21_p3_027,"To elucidate the microstructural features, lattice characteristics, and elemental distribution of the Pd-PdNi-Ni bimetallic catalysts, scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and corresponding energy-dispersive x-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping analyses were performed. Without Pd center, a smooth surface composed of numerous particles is identified after solvothermal reaction (Figure S3), which is changed into a rough one after thermal reduction (Figure S4)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,3,3,P21_p3_028,"A comparison on the Pd-PdNi-Ni-1 (Figure S5), Pd-PdNi-Ni2 (Figure S6), and Pd-PdNi-Ni-3 (Figure S7) catalysts reveals that all are composed of a large number of uniformly dispersed nanoparticles with particle sizes predominantly in the range of 30-50 nm. Particularly, for the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst (Figures 2c and S8), the specific particle size is around 40 nm (Figure 2d). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) analysis (Figure 2e) further verifies the presence of two types of well-resolved lattice fringes with the interplanar spacing of 0.220 nm (Figure 2f) and 0.165 nm (Figure 2g), which can be indexed into PdNi (111) and Ni (200), respectively [12]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,3,3,P21_p3_029,"Also, the selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure 2h) confirms the presence of the (111) and (200) planes of Pd-Ni alloying structure as well as the (200) plane of Ni center [13]. High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) image (Figure 2i) and the EDS mapping patterns of Ni (Figure 2j) and Pd (Figure 2k) suggests a much higher Ni signal intensity than that of Pd, which can be attributed to the formation or dispersion of alloy nanoparticles on a Ni-rich matrix."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,4,4,P21_p4_030,"FIGURE 2 Morphological and structural characterizations of Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts. (a) XRD patterns and (b) Structural model of the presence of Pd-Ni interface. (c and d) SEM image and particle size distribution plot, (e-g) HRTEM image, (h) SAED pattern, and (i-k) elemental mapping images of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst. To gain insight into the surface chemical states, electronic structure, and local coordination environment of Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts, x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and synchrotron xray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) were systematically employed."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,4,4,P21_p4_031,"As presented in Figure 3a, in all Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts, two sets of doublets are clearly observed at the binding energies of approximately 335.5 eV (Pd 3d5/2)/340.8 eV (Pd 3d3/2) and 336.5 eV (Pd 3d5/2)/342.0 eV (Pd 3d3/2) in the Pd 3d XPS spectra (Table S3), which can be assigned to metallic Pd 0 and mildly oxidized Pd-O surface, respectively [12]. With Pd ratio increases from Pd-PdNiNi-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 to Pd-PdNi-Ni-3, the relative proportion of Pd 0 progressively increases, accompanied by a discernible shift of the Pd 3d peaks toward lower binding energies."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,4,4,P21_p4_032,"This trend indicates that higher Pd loading promotes the formation of larger or more continuous metallic Pd domains within the alloy, reducing the fraction of Pd atoms at the surface that are susceptible to oxidation. In the PdNi alloy system, Ni possesses a lower electronegativity (1.91) than Pd (2.20), leading to preferential electron transfer from Ni to Pd upon alloy formation. As a result, Pd atoms gain partial excess electron density, increasing the local electron cloud density and lowering the binding energy of the Pd core levels."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,4,5,P21_p4_033,"This negative binding-energy shift confirms the strong electronic coupling between Pd and Ni interfaces and the formation of an alloy phase rather than a physical mixture [14]. Then, in the Ni 2p XPS spectra (Figure 3b), the main peak at approximately 852.6 eV corresponds to metallic 1.2 FIGURE 3 Surface states and surface characterizations of Pd-PdNi-Ni catalyst system. (a) Pd 3d and (b) Ni 2p XPS spectra of the Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts. (c) Pd K-edge XANES spectra of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst. (d and e) EXAFS fitting curves (f and g) EXAFS fitting curves, and (h and i) WT-EXAFS of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst and Pd foil."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_034,"Ni 0 (Ni 2p3/2), whereas the intense feature at around 856.3 eV, together with the associated satellite peaks, is characteristic of NiO surface [15]. With increasing Pd content from Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 to Pd-PdNi-Ni-3, the relative fraction of metallic Ni 0 decreases, accompanied by a positive shift in binding energy of approximately 0.25 eV, indicating a reduction in the local electron density of Ni, which further corroborates the interfacial electron redistribution induced by Pd-Ni electronic coupling."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_035,"The O 1s XPS spectra were deconvoluted into three peaks at ∼529.6 eV (lattice oxygen, OL), ∼531.1 eV (oxygen vacancies, Ov), and ∼532.4 eV (adsorbed oxygen, Oad) to quantify their relative contents (Figure S9) [16]. Among them, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst shows the highest Ov proportion (5.3%), indicating a higher oxygen defect concentration that favors reactant adsorption and interfacial charge transfer [17]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_036,"To obtain in-depth information on the local coordination environment of the Pd-PdNi-Ni catalyst, x-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and extended x-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) were analyzed. Taking the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst for example, as shown in the normalized Pd K-edge XANES spectra (Figure 3c), it is revealed that the peak positions of the PdPdNi-Ni-2 catalysts are different compared to the metallic Pd foil, indicating the partial formation of PdNi alloy."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_037,"Notably, compared with the reference PdO, the absorption edge of the Pd-PdNi-Ni alloy shifts distinctly toward lower photon energies, which is fully consistent with the XPS results and confirms the electron-rich nature of Pd in the alloy [18]. Figure 3d displays the Fourier-transformed Pd K-edge EXAFS spectra of the PdPdNi-Ni-2 catalysts. A dominant peak located at approximately 2.1 Å corresponds to the scattering path between Pd atoms and their first-shell neighboring Ni atoms."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_038,"Compared with the Pd─Pd metallic bond, this peak shifts toward shorter radial distances and exhibits noticeable broadening, suggesting that a portion of Pd nearest neighbors is substituted by smaller-radius Ni atoms, leading to the formation of Pd─Ni metallic bonds and increased structural disorder. In addition, a secondary scattering feature at around 2.5 Å can be assigned to Pd-Pd coordination, indicating that not all Pd atoms are fully isolated by Ni atoms [19]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_039,"Relative to Pd foil (Figure 3e), the Pd-Pd peak in the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst shifts toward a longer radial distance by approximately 0.03 Å, which likely originates from lattice distortion induced by alloying when combined with charge transfer from Ni to Pd, results in partial expansion of the local electron cloud surrounding Pd atoms and contributes to the observed bond-length elongation."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,5,5,P21_p5_040,"Furthermore, quantitative structural parameters, including coordination numbers and bond distances (Table S4) with the corresponding k-space fitting results of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst (Figure 3f) and Pd foil (Figure 3g) and wavelet transform EXAFS (WT-EXAFS) analysis on Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst (Figure 3h) and Pd foil (Figure 3i) indicate that the dominate existence of Pd-Ni bonding in the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst [20]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_041,"Based on the above-mentioned results, the Pd-PdNi-Ni catalyst features a metallic PdNi alloy with electron-rich Pd centers, a Nidepleted electronic environment, and a locally distorted lattice in the presence of Pd-Pd bonding. These electronic and structural characteristics are expected to induce a favorable ΔGH* gradient for efficient hydrogen spillover."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_042,"To systematically evaluate electrochemical performance of the asobtained Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2, and Pd-PdNi-Ni-3 catalysts for HER, electrochemical measurements were conducted in both alkaline (1 M KOH) and acidic (0.5 M H2SO4) solutions. Based on the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves presented in Figure 4a, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst exhibits the highest catalytic activity under alkaline conditions, in which an overpotential (η10) of only 60 mV is required to achieve a current density of 10 mA cm-2 (Figure 4b), outperforming other Pd-PdNi-Ni catalysts (167 mV for Pd-PdNi-Ni-1 and 148 mV for Pd-PdNiNi-3) and the pristine Ni (371 mV)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_043,"Under acidic condition, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst also maintains excellent activity with an η10 value of 37 mV, which is much lower than that of Pd-PdNiNi-1 (62 mV) and Pd-PdNi-Ni-3 (54 mV). Even at a higher density of 50 mA cm-2 , the overpotential (η50) is only 90 mV for the PdPdNi-Ni-2 catalyst in the acidic solution (Table S5). When loaded onto nickel foam (NF) and denoted as Pd-PdNi-Ni-X@NF (X = 1, 2, or 3), the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst delivers a low η10 value of 32 mV in alkaline media, and a low η50 value of 56 mV in acidic solution (Figure S10)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_044,"To verify the advantage of the partial interfacial alloying structure, the electrocatalytic performance of the pure PdNi alloy and the physically mixed Pd + Ni catalyst was compared. In 1 M KOH, the overpotentials of Pd + Ni and PdNi alloy at 10 mA cm-2 were 307 and 223 mV, respectively, substantially higher than that of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 (60 mV) (Figure S11). In 0.5 M H2SO4, this trend became more pronounced, with overpotentials of 197, 104, and 37 mV for Pd + Ni, PdNi alloy, and Pd-PdNi-Ni-2, respectively (Figure S12). These results demonstrate that the physical mixture, lacking a continuous Pd-Ni alloy interface, cannot establish effective hydrogen spillover pathways."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_045,"Meanwhile, the pure PdNi alloy, despite moderate activity, suffers from the absence of metallic Ni as a hydrogen source and structural support, resulting in inferior performance compared to Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 with a continuous alloy interface. As summarized in Figure S13, the PdPdNi-Ni-2 catalyst exhibits the highest heterointerface density, the fastest H2 desorption kinetics, and the best HER performance, thus revealing a clear correlation between interfacial structure, hydrogen-transfer kinetics, and catalytic activity."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_046,"To assess the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA), the double-layer capacitance (Cdl) was estimated from CV curves (Figure S14). As compared in Figure 4c, in alkaline medium, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst exhibits a Cdl value of 58.3 mF cm-2 , approximately 2.4 times that of the pristine Ni. In acidic electrolyte, its Cdl further increases to 140.4 mF cm-2 , nearly 2.8 times higher than that of the pristine Ni. These results suggest that the introduction of Pd center and the partial interface alloying not only modulates the electronic structure but also substantially increases the exposure of electrochemically accessible active sites [21]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_047,"Then, reaction kinetics were analyzed using Tafel plots (Figure 4d). In alkaline electrolyte, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst exhibits a Tafel slope of 50.9 mV dec-1 . Compared to other catalysts (131.9 mV dec-1 for Pd-PdNi-Ni-1 and 128.6 mV dec-1 for Pd-PdNi-Ni-3), a more efficient water dissociation and/or hydrogen desorption kinetic is confirmed for the Pd-PdNi-Ni2 catalyst [22]. In acidic electrolyte, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst shows the lowest Tafel slope of 33.8 mV dec-1 , indicating a characteristic of Tafel-controlled mechanisms, and implying rapid hydrogen recombination and desorption on the catalyst surface [23]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_048,"Also, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements (Figure S15) reveal that the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst possesses the smallest charge-transfer resistance (Rct) in both alkaline (10 ohm) and acidic (2.5 ohm) media. In addition, as displayed in Figure 4e, corrosion polarization tests on the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF, Ni/NF, and bare NF electrodes demonstrate that the Pd-containing catalyst exhibits the highest corrosion potential (0.59 V in 1 M KOH and -0.02 V in 0.5 M H2SO4) and the lowest corrosion current density (-4.3 mA cm-2 in 1 M KOH and -3.8 mA cm-2 in 0.5 M H2SO4) under different pH environment (Table S6), indicative of enhanced chemical and structural stability."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_049,"The long-term operational stability of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalysts was evaluated by chronopotentiometric measurements. In the alkaline solution, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst shows negligible potential increase during continuous operation at 40 mA cm-2 for 180 h (Figure 4f). Also, stable performance is maintained for 26 h at 10 mA cm-2 in acidic medium (Figure 4g). Moreover, a comparison on the LSV curves recorded before and after durability tests (Figure 4h) indicates no noticeable degradation, further confirming the excellent electrochemical robustness of the resulting Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_050,"Besides, compared with these previously reported catalysts, as presented in Figure 4i, the obtained Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF electrode is superior to many noble-metal-based catalysts, such as Pd NPs-Bis-24 h [24], PdxSy/1T-MoS2 [25], PdH@Ru [20], and PtCoNiFeCu-HEA catalysts [26], in terms of overpotential and Tafel slope across different electrolytes (Table S7)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_051,"To evidence the overall performance and operational durability of the as-fabricated catalyst in practical water electrolysis systems, a two-electrode configuration was assembled using the PdPdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst as the cathode and the commercialized RuO2@NF as the anode. Electrochemical measurements were conducted in both alkaline (1 M KOH) and acidic (0.5 M H2SO4) electrolytes. Under the alkaline conditions (Figure 5a), the electrolyzer required only 1.41 V to deliver a current density of 10 mA cm-2 . In the acidic electrolyte, a slightly higher voltage of 1.44 V was needed to reach the same current density."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,6,6,P21_p6_052,"Long-term stability was further assessed by chronoamperometric measurements at a constant current density of 10 mA cm-2 for 100 h (Figure 5b). In the alkaline electrolyte, the Pd-PdNi-Ni2@NF-based electrolyzer exhibits a stable current output without noticeable decay. Even at a higher density of 100 mA cm-2 (Figure S16), the current remains remarkably stable for 40 h. After the long-life stability test, the measured LSV curve of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst is almost overlapped with the initial one, indicating negligible degradation in catalytic activity. Even in the corrosive acidic system, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalystbased two-electrode system maintains stable performance for"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,7,7,P21_p7_053,"FIGURE 4 Electrochemical activityevaluationof thePd-PdNi-Ni catalysts inpH-dependent solutions. (a)LSVcurves, (b) overpotentials at current densities of 10, 50, and 100 mA cm-2 , (c) fitted plots for calculating Cdl values, (d) Tafel plots, and (e) corrosion polarization curves of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-1, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2, and Pd-PdNi-Ni-3 catalysts. (f and g) Long-term stability test of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2 catalyst in alkaline and acidic solutions, and (h) the corresponding LSV curves before and after stability tests. (i) Comparison of HER performance with previous reported catalysts."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,7,7,P21_p7_054,"28 h. Finally, Faradaic efficiencies were determined by the water displacement method in alkaline (Figure S17) and acidic (Figure S18) electrolytes, in which the calculated values are close to 100%, indicating highly efficient charge utilization and excellent reaction selectivity."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,7,8,P21_p7_055,"Moreover, the practical applicability of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst was assessed in a device-level configuration, in which the catalyst was integrated with a commercial RuO2@NF anode to construct an anion exchange membrane (AEM) electrolyzer (Figure 5c), in alkaline electrolyte (1 M KOH) and simulated seawater electrolyte (1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl). Considering the temperature influence, the catalytic activities were monitored under different temperatures under both alkaline and simulated 100"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,8,8,P21_p8_056,"FIGURE 5 Overall water-splitting performance using the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst. (a) LSV curves, and (b) stability test of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NFbased two-electrode system before and after cycling. (c) Schematic illustration of the anion exchange membrane (AEM) system using Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst, (d) LSV curves measured at different temperatures, (e) long-term stability test under alkaline conditions, and (f) long-term stability test in the simulated seawater. (g) Performance comparison with previously reported systems."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,8,8,P21_p8_057,"seawater conditions, As displayed in Figure 5d, the performance systematically improved with increasing temperature. In the alkaline electrolyte, the device voltages are only 1.73 and 1.86 V at 70◦C to achieve the densities of 0.5 and 1 A cm-2 , respectively. In the simulated seawater electrolyte, these voltages further decrease to 1.68 and 1.80 V to reach the same densities. Notably, the electrolyzer operated stably at 1 A cm-2 for 110 h in alkaline electrolyte, with a low degradation rate of 1.64 mV h-1 (Figure 5e)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,8,8,P21_p8_058,"In simulated seawater, it sustained 0.5 A cm-2 for 452 h with negligible performance decay, corresponding to an exceptionally low degradation rate of 0.14 mV h-1 (Figure 5f). To elucidate the origin of the enhanced device performance, the control AEM electrolyzers were constructed using a physically mixed Pd + Ni cathode and a PdNi alloy cathode, with RuO2 as the anode (Figure S19). At 0.2 A cm-2 , the PdNi@NF//RuO2@NF and Pd + Ni@NF//RuO2@NF cells required 1.72 and 1.97 V, respectively, both substantially higher than that of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF//RuO2@NF cell."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,8,8,P21_p8_059,"Moreover, both control cells exhibited pronounced performance degradation during prolonged operation, highlighting the critical role of the partialinterface-alloy architecture rather than Pd incorporation alone. Compared with representative noble-metal-based AEM electrolyzers, including Pt@SNiFe LDH//S-NiFe LDH [27] and Ru/NdOCl//IrO2 [28], the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF electrolyzer achieves lower cell voltages at comparable current densities (Figure 5g and Table S8), demonstrating its competitive electrolysis performance."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,8,8,P21_p8_060,"Nevertheless, direct comparison of AEM electrolyzer voltages should be made with caution because reported values are strongly influenced by operating temperature, catalyst loading, membrane type, electrolyte flow rate, and current density. Therefore, we further compared systems under closely matched conditions (1 M KOH, 70◦C, and 0.5 or 1.0 A cm-2 ; Figure S20). Under these conditions, Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF exhibits competitive cell voltages relative to reported systems tested under comparable conditions."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_061,"To gain deep insight into the origin of the HER performance and the spillover mechanism of the resultant Pd-PdNi-Ni catalyst, the hydrogen adsorption/desorption behavior on its surface was systematically investigated through electrochemical kinetic analysis, theoretical calculation, and operando characterizations. First, as shown in the CV curves of these catalysts with different Pd ratios from PdNi-Ni-1@NF, PdNi-Ni-2@NF, to PdNi-Ni-3@NF (Figure S21), quantitative analysis of the hydrogen desorption region reveals that the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF exhibits the largest hydrogen desorption peak area, indicating the strongest hydrogen desorption capability and fastest desorption kinetics [29]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_062,"Furthermore, the contour plots constructed from desorption peaks recorded at different scan rates (Figure 6a) show a slight shift in the hydrogen desorption peak position as the rate increases. By systematically extracting and fitting the relationship between the desorption peak potential (Ep) and scan rate (v) (Figure 6b), the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst is found to possess a significantly smaller peak-shift slope than the reference catalysts, which suggests a lower hydrogen desorption energy barrier and accelerated desorption kinetics, which significantly enhance the surface hydrogen release rate and the intrinsic catalytic activity [30, 31]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_063,"Then, density functional theory (DFT) analysis further reveals that the catalyst maintains efficient hydrogen spillover mechanisms. Figure 6c displays the Gibbs free energy profiles for atomic hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH*), a key descriptor for HER activity, over Pd, Ni, and Pd-Ni interface. The free energy of adsorbed H* on the Ni surface reaches -0.55 eV, representing excessively strong hydrogen binding that tends to trap H* intermediates and hinder subsequent H2 desorption. In contrast, the Pd site delivers a moderate H adsorption free energy of -0.42 eV."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_064,"Benefiting from interfacial electronic modulation between Pd and Ni, the Pd-Ni heterointerface presents an intermediate value of -0.44 eV, which lies between those of Ni and Pd. This thermodynamically balanced hydrogen adsorption strength not only facilitates spontaneous hydrogen spillover from the H-rich Ni domain toward the Pd-Ni boundary but also optimizes the kinetics of H* consumption for H2 generation, confirming that the Pd-Ni heterointerfacial structure tunes hydrogen adsorption to a near-optimal level and endows superior intrinsic alkaline HER activity [32, 33]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_065,"Figure 6d compares the HER thermodynamics over monometallic Pd, monometallic Ni, and heterostructured Pd-Ni catalyst along the Volmer-Heyrovsky pathway and the Volmer-Tafel pathway. For all three samples, the reaction proceeds sequentially through water dissociation, adsorbed hydrogen intermediate formation, co-adsorption of two hydrogen species, paired hydrogen adsorption (*H+*H), and final generation of H2 with co-adsorbed hydroxyl (*H2 + *OH). It is concluded that the energy barrier for the RDS is calculated to be 1.27 eV for Ni, 0.84 eV for Pd, and 0.97 eV for the Pd-Ni interface in the Volmer-Heyrovsky route."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_066,"Meanwhile, in the Volmer-Tafel pathway, the overall energy barrier follows the order Ni (1.23 eV) > Pd (0.87 eV) > Pd-Ni (0.83 eV). To further validate the effectiveness of this proposed strategy, the integrated Pd-PdNi-Ni model was further constructed, and three different regions, including Ni sites (Figure S22), Pd sites (Figure S23), and PdNi alloy sites (Figure S24), was examined to systematically calculate the HER thermodynamic energy barriers along the Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel pathways."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_067,"As presented in Figure S25, it is revealed that the RDS barriers are approximately 0.66 eV for Ni sites, 1.05 eV for Pd sites, and 1.27 eV for PdNi alloy sites along the Volmer-Heyrovsky pathway. In the Volmer-Tafel pathway, the overall energy barriers rank in ascending order as Pd sites (0.441 eV) < PdNi alloy sites (0.48 eV) < Ni sites (1.19 eV), indicating the dominate spillover from Ni sites to alloy sites during HER. Besides, the d-band center theory is applied to interpret the electronic modulation and interfacial charge transfer of the Pd-Ni heterointerface. The d-band center of Pd is calculated to be -1.763 eV (Figure S26), while that of Ni is -1.302 eV (Figure S27)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_068,"In the Pd-Ni heterointerface, the d-band center of the Pd-Ni interface shifts to -1.481 eV (Figure S28), falling between the values of Pd and Ni, which originates from spontaneous interfacial electron transfer. Moreover, in the integrated Pd-PdNi-Ni model, the d-band center further downshifts to -1.541 eV (Figure S29), which remains stable between Pd sites and Ni sites. At low coverage, the binding on Pd, alloy, and Ni sites creates a driving force for promoting spillover, and the alloy site serves as an optimal sink with intermediate binding that avoids both weak adsorption on Pd and strong trapping on Ni (Figure S30)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_069,"Moreover, a migration barrier of only 0.22 eV ensures that H* transport is far faster (Figure S31), confirming that the spillover pathways are thermodynamically and kinetically viable. As confirmed by the charge transfer analysis, it is revealed that approximately 2.56 electrons transfer from Ni atoms to adjacent Pd atoms across the Pd-Ni boundary (Figure 6e). This electronic reconfiguration provides the intrinsic driving force for directional hydrogen spillover between Ni and Pd sites, optimizes the Gibbs free energy of adsorbed H*, and accelerates the overall kinetics of alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction at the Pd-Ni heterointerfaces [34]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_070,"To further verify the presence of hydrogen spillover from an interfacial perspective, operando EIS measurements were conducted at different overpotentials for a series of catalysts (Figure S32), in which hydrogen adsorption pseudocapacitance (Cφ) and adsorption resistance (Ri) are indicative of surface hydrogen coverage and adsorption kinetics, respectively. As depicted in Figure S33, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-1@NF catalyst exhibits only a marginal increase in Cφ compared to Ni@NF, indicating limited hydrogen spillover [35]. In contrast, the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst shows a much higher Cφ than that of the Pd-PdNi-Ni3@NF catalyst, demonstrating that the most favorable spillover behaviors."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_071,"This optimized interface enables the accumulation of a high concentration of readily utilizable H* species on the Ni substrate, providing a continuous supply of key intermediates that accelerate the overall HER kinetics [36]. By comparison, the lower Cφ value of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-3@NF catalyst suggests insufficient interfacial hydrogen spillover, thus limiting surface hydrogen turnover and the rate of H2 generation."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,9,9,P21_p9_072,"Then, based on the H2 temperature-programmed desorption (H2-TPD) analysis (Figure S34), compared with reference Ni sample, the Pd-PdNiNi-2 catalyst exhibits significantly enhanced H2 desorption peak intensity and a markedly lower peak temperature, verifying the promoting effect of the hydrogen spillover mechanism on H2 desorption kinetics. To elucidate the kinetic nature of the hydrogen desorption process, the log-log plots of desorption peak current versus scan rate were constructed (Figure S35), in which the fitted slope (b) values range from 0.87 to 0.91, indicating that hydrogen desorption is predominantly surface-controlled and exhibits pronounced pseudocapacitive behavior [37]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,10,10,P21_p10_073,"FIGURE 6 Hydrogen spillover mechanism investigation. (a) Contour map of hydrogen desorption of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF at different scan rates. (b) Relationship between hydrogen desorption peak potential and scan rate for catalysts with different Pd contents. (c) Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔG) at Pd, Ni, Pd-Ni interface site. (d) Energy profiles of Pd, Ni, Pd-Ni interface for HER via Volmer-Tafel and Volmer-Heyrovsky pathways. (e) Electronic density of Pd-PdNi-Ni framework. (f) Operando Raman measurement of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst during HER."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,10,10,P21_p10_074,"Finally, operando Raman spectroscopy reveals that the Pd-PdNiNi-2 catalyst follows a surface reconstruction and hydrogen spillover mechanism in the alkaline electrolyte. As shown in Figure 6f, when HER proceeds, a surface reconstruction into NiOOH was identified, as confirmed by the appearance of the characteristic Ni-OH vibration at ∼385 cm-1 and the Ni─O bands at ∼458 and 793 cm-1 [38, 39]. As cathodic polarization potential progressively increased, these typical peaks were gradually reduced, indicating the electrochemical reduction into metallic Ni and the further exposure of additional electrochemically active sites."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,10,10,P21_p10_075,"Within the HER potential region, the catalyst interface remains under strongly reducing conditions, in which Pd centers thermodynamically favor the metallic or low-valence state without the obvious formation of substantial amounts of stable Pd 2+ species or Pd─O bonds. Notably, the vibration signals assigned to Pd-H (∼2322 cm-1 ) and Ni-H (∼989 cm-1 ) provides direct spectroscopic evidence for a hydrogen spillover process, indicating that hydrogen intermediates are initially generated on Ni or Pd sites through the Volmer step and subsequently migrate across the Pd-Ni interface for gas generation [40, 41]."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,10,10,P21_p10_076,"Simultaneously, the continuous enhancement of the O-H vibration band at ∼1067 cm-1 suggests the accelerated interfacial water activation for providing sufficient proton source for sustaining HER."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_077,"Combined with the aforementioned characterization and kinetic analysis results, it is elucidated that a dual hydrogen spillover mechanism is identified at the Pd-Ni heterointerface for accelerated HER. Owing to spontaneous transfer from Ni to Pd at the boundary, the Pd-Ni interface exhibits an intermediate dband center between Pd and Ni, which precisely modulates the H* adsorption free energy. Specifically, Ni binds H* too strongly to release H2, Pd delivers relatively weak H* adsorption, while the Pd-Ni interface possesses thermodynamically balanced H* affinity ideal for accepting migrating H* and facilitating H2 desorption."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_078,"Therefore, such an adsorption energy gradient drives the primary spillover: abundant H* generated from water dissociation on the H-enriched Ni domain spontaneously migrates across the heterointerface toward the Pd-Ni boundary. Meanwhile, adsorbed H* produced from water activation over Pd sites undergoes secondary spillover, diffusing toward the same interfacial active sink. Moreover, these accumulated interfacial H* originating from dual spillover subsequently undergo Tafel recombination to produce gaseous H2."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_079,"In brief, interfacial electron redistribution establishes the thermodynamic foundation for bidirectional hydrogen spillover, synergistically optimizing hydrogen adsorption-desorption behaviors and substantially boosting the intrinsic activity of the Pd-Ni interface."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_080,"To examine interface reconstruction mechanisms, structural changes on the surface of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst during HER were tracked in acidic and alkaline solution, As displayed in Figure 7a, SEM images acquired after cycling in alkaline electrolyte reveal the distribution of Pd and Ni species and the presence of aggregated Pd clusters deposition on the surface. TEM analysis (Figure 7b) shows a Ni-rich surface and the expanded lattice spacing (0.25 nm) with a defective state, confirming the loss of Pd centers and the formation of vacancies."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_081,"Furthermore, the particle-size statistics based on TEM image (Figure S36) reveal that, after long-term cycling, the catalyst particles are predominantly distributed around 48 nm, with a slight increase in size compared to the pristine catalyst, indicating that it still maintains good structural stability under practical high-current operation in the AEM device. In contrast, in acidic electrolyte, SEM (Figure 7c) and TEM (Figure 7d) images obtained after cycling indicates a Pd-rich surface in the presence of defective sites, verifying removal of Ni centers in the acid and the stability of Pd sites."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_082,"To further reveal the contribution of the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst for the enhanced HER performance, XPS technique was employed to systematically track the dynamic evolution of Ni (Figure 7e) and Pd (Figure 7f) sites under different overpotentials in both acidic and alkaline electrolytes. In the alkaline electrolyte (Figure S37), the binding energy on Ni 2p for the reconstructed Ni-O bonding exhibits a systematic negative shift with increasing overpotential, and the metallic Ni 0 becomes obvious at a high reduction overpotential such as 70 mV (Table S9). This formation or exposure of metallic Ni 0 significantly enhances electronic conductivity and facilitates rapid charge transfer."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_083,"Meanwhile, the negative shift in Ni 2p binding energy indicates increased electron density around Ni atoms, which promotes the polarization and dissociation of the O─H bond in H2O molecules, thus accelerating the Volmer step [42]. ICP analysis of the alkaline electrolyte after long-term cycling revealed trace Ni leaching (825 μg L-1 ).In contrast, the Pd 3d binding energy and valence state remain essentially stable during HER, indicating that Pd maintains high structural robustness in alkaline media. In the acidic electrolyte (Figure S38), the surface evolution pathway differs from that in alkaline media (Table S10)."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_084,"Upon the increased potentials, the intensity of the Ni 2p signal significantly decreases, indicating selective dissolution of Ni under acidic conditions. To verify this phenomenon, ICP analysis was conducted on the acidic electrolyte after long-term cycling. A substantial Ni signal (110,360 μg L-1 ) was detected, whereas the Pd signal remained extremely weak (14 μg L-1 ), further confirming the preferential dissolution of Ni in acidic operating environments, while the Pd host structure maintains excellent chemical stability."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_085,"Also, the metallic Ni◦ can be observed at lower overpotentials in acidic electrolyte, in which the surface oxide layers are effectively buffered and the underlying Ni regions are exposed that can strongly couple with Pd centers. Meanwhile, the binding energy of the residual Ni species positively shifts, suggesting an electron-deficient electronic state for optimizing the hydrogen adsorption energy and facilitating rapid hydrogen migration and desorption. Similar to the alkaline case, the Pd 3d spectra remain relatively stable in acidic media, confirming the electrochemical robustness of Pd centers."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_086,"After long-term HER operation, as shown in Figure 7g, the Pd 0 peaks shift toward a lower binding energy (335.4 eV) compared to the pristine sample (335.7 eV), with a more pronounced shift observed in the acidic medium than in the alkaline one (Table S11), which is attributed to the higher proton concentration under acidic conditions for enhancing hydrogen adsorption on the Pd surface. Meanwhile, the selective dissolution of Ni in the acidic environment forms a porous Pd-rich layer, further exposing active sites and promoting hydrogen enrichment. Besides, in terms of Ni 2p XPS spectra, Ni 2+ species are dominate without any obvious metallic Ni 0 signal under alkaline conditions."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,11,P21_p11_087,"In contrast, only 3.4% of Ni◦ from the initial 38.9% remains under acidic conditions after longterm stability measurements (Figure S39). This suggests that a dense reconstructed of Ni(OH)2 or NiOOH layer readily forms on the Ni surface in alkaline media, preventing further dissolution of Ni sites by maintaining an oxidized state. Simultaneously, Pd undergoes periodic lattice expansion and contraction due to repeated hydrogen absorption and desorption, generating substantial internal stress that leads to the detachment of surface atoms."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,11,12,P21_p11_088,"In acidic media, a typical dealloying process occurs, wherein Ni is selectively leached and dissolved into the solution as Ni 2+ , leaving behind a porous Pd-rich surface layer. A trace (e)"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,12,12,P21_p12_089,"FIGURE 7 Post-characterizationof thePd-PdNi-Ni-2@NFcatalyst after long-termstability. (a andb) SEMandTEMimages, andelementmapping pattern of Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst after HER in alkaline solution. (c and d) SEM and TEM images, and element mapping pattern of Pd-PdNi-Ni2@NF catalyst after HER in acid solution. (e and f ) Potential-dependent XPS spectra on Pd 3d and Ni 2p during HER at an overpotential range of 0-90 mV. (g) Comparison on percentages of different species after stability tests in different electrolytes."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,12,12,P21_p12_090,"amount of Ni 0 may be physically encapsulated within the porous Pd framework and thus remains. Owing to the porous structure and high hydrogen coverage, Pd exhibits a more pronounced electron enrichment effect."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,12,12,P21_p12_091,"Hence, these results demonstrate that the Pd-PdNi-Ni-2@NF catalyst possesses different interface reconstruction mechanisms under varied pH conditions. Specifically, the interface reconstruction under the alkaline environment is highly associated with hydrogen absorption and lattice expansion effects. Pd centers absorb hydrogen under negative potential to form hydrides (e.g., PdH), leading to significant lattice expansion of Pd. After repeated hydrogen absorption/desorption processes, the internal stress become obvious, making surface Pd atoms unstable, thus causing them to detach from the electrode surface in the form of Pd clusters."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,12,12,P21_p12_092,"As for Ni centers, the Ni-enriched hydroxide layer on the surface could partially inhibit the dissolution of Ni centers. In contrast, the interface reconstruction in the acidic solution is primarily due to the dissolution of transition metals. The standard electrode potential of Ni is much lower than the hydrogen evolution potential, and the local microenvironment and potential fluctuations leads to the generation of Ni 2+ and its further dissolution into the solution. Under acidic HER conditions, Pd itself is relatively stable without obvious dissolution."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,12,13,P21_p12_093,"Therefore, the reconstruction preferentially proceeds through the dissolution of Ni species for the formation of a typical dealloying phenomenon, in which Ni is selectively leached out and a Pd-rich surface layer is produced. 3 Conclusion"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,13,13,P21_p13_094,"In summary, a partial interface alloying strategy was employed to fabricate a Pd-PdNi-Ni heterostructure that enables an interfacial hydrogen spillover mechanism for high-performance pHuniversal HER. The engineered PdNi alloy interface, through spontaneous electron redistribution from Ni to Pd, generates an intermediate d-band center and an optimized hydrogen adsorption free energy gradient. The combined electrochemical, spectroscopic, and theoretical results support the involvement of both Ni- and Pd-containing domains in interfacial hydrogen transfer, while the PdNi region provides a favorable environment for hydrogen redistribution and subsequent H2 formation."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,13,13,P21_p13_095,"The catalyst with an optimal Pd/Ni ratio exhibits exceptional activity (60 mV in 1 M KOH, 37 mV in 0.5 M H2SO4) and outstanding long-term stability. Crucially, operando and post-reaction characterizations reveal pH-governed interfacial reconstruction dynamics: in alkaline electrolyte, hydrogen absorption-induced lattice expansion causes Pd cluster detachment, while a surface Ni(OH)2 layer suppresses Ni dissolution; in acidic electrolyte, selective Ni dealloying creates a porous Pd-rich surface that exposes additional active sites and enhances electron enrichment. These observations highlight the dynamic nature of the active interface under different HER conditions."
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,13,13,P21_p13_096,"Overall, these results demonstrate that partial interface alloying can effectively regulate hydrogen-binding energetics and interfacial hydrogen transfer, providing a general strategy for constructing efficient HER catalysts with spatially differentiated reaction functions. Author Contributions"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,13,13,P21_p13_097,"Di Wang: data curation, investigation, validation, formal analysis, visualization, writing - original draft. Jun Mei: conceptualization, methodology, software, investigation, formal analysis, supervision, funding acquisition, visualization, project administration, resources, writing - review and editing. Qianqian Yao: data curation, investigation, formal analysis, resources. Jing Shang: software, resources, formal analysis. Yahui Wang: data curation, validation, formal analysis. Shixue Dou: formal analysis, resources, supervision. Acknowledgments"
P21,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen,Angew Chem Int Ed - 2026 - Wang - Interfacial Hydrogen Spillover in Partially Alloyed PdNi Electrocatalysts for Hydrogen.pdf,13,13,P21_p13_098,"This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (52402112), the Natural Science Foundation of Henan Province (252300421102), the Joint Fund of Science and Technology R&D Plan of Henan Province (245200810061), and the Postdoctoral Science Foundation of China (2024M760781). Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_000,"Journal of Materials Chemistry A COMMUNICATION Cite this: J. Mater. Chem. A, 2018, 6, 1376 Received 11th November 2017 Accepted 21st December 2017 DOI: 10.1039/c7ta09939h rsc.li/materials-a"
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_001,"Rational design and synthesis of low-cost and robust electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is critical for the development of water electrolysis technology. In this work, low-rutheniumcontent NiRu alloy nanoparticles encapsulated in nitrogen-doped carbon (NiRu@N-C) have been prepared through doping ruthenium species into nickel-based metal-organic frameworks followed by pyrolysis treatment. Benefiting from their unique structural advantages and synergic effect of the components, the as-obtained NiRu@N-C nanohybrids exhibited excellent electrocatalytic activity and good stability towards the HER in acidic, neutral, and alkaline solutions."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_002,"Hydrogen is a promising energy carrier for a sustainable energy economy, and electrocatalytic water-splitting is considered as one of the convenient routes for large-scale hydrogen generation in high purity. 1-4 One of the key problems of this method is the need of highly active, low-cost, and durable electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). 5-7 So far, platinum (Pt) has remained the state-of-the-art H2-evolving electrocatalyst with nearly zero overpotential and excellent kinetics for actuating the HER. However, multiple fatal disadvantages of Ptbased catalysts, such as high cost and scarce supply, largely limit their practical applications."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_003,"Therefore, to fulll the demands in practical use, there is currently a dire need to develop cost-effective alternatives to the precious and less abundant Pt materials for the HER. In this regard, plenty of non-noble-metal materials, especially transition metal compounds (e.g., chalcogenides, 8-17 phosphides, 18-27 carbides, 28-31 and nitrides 32,33 ), metal-free carbon-based materials, 34-36 and their hybrids 37-44 with various structures and architectures, have been prepared and examined as effective HER catalysts in a series of recent studies. Nevertheless, in most"
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_004,"College of Chemical Engineering, Zhejiang University of Technology, Hangzhou 310014, Zhejiang, P. R. China. E-mail: hjw@zjut.edu.cn; wangliang@zjut.edu.cn † Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available: Experimental section and supplementary gures and tables. See DOI: 10.1039/c7ta09939h Published on 21 December 2017 Licence and permissions Low-ruthenium-content NiRu nanoalloys encapsulated in nitrogen-doped carbon as highly efficient and pH-universal electrocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction† You Xu, Shuli Yin, Chunjie Li, Kai Deng, Hairong Xue, Xiaonian Li, Hongjing Wang * and Liang Wang *"
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_005,"of these cases, their catalytic activity and stability still need to be improved to compete with Pt-based electrocatalysts."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_006,"Recent advanced studies highlighted that nanocomposite or nanohybrid core-shell structures composed of 3d transition metals (or their alloys) and N-doped carbon materials (M@N-C) hold great promise as substitutes for noble metal electrocatalysts for the HER and (or) the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) due to the interaction and synergy of the metallic core and carbon shell. 45-51 Such kind of catalysts can have particular advantages in electrocatalytic applications. Firstly, the carbon shell in such a M@N-C core-shell structure could effectively protect the metallic cores from corrosion and aggregation during electrocatalytic processes."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_007,"Secondly, the doped nitrogen atoms in the carbon matrix could modulate the electronic properties of the adjacent carbon atoms by intramolecular charge transfer, which is favourable for improving the electrocatalytic performance. Thirdly, and more importantly, the interaction and synergy of the metallic core and N-doped carbon shell could create sufficient localized reactive sites by modifying the charge distribution on the carbon layer surface via the promoted electron transfer effect, which changes the local work function for related reactive species adsorption and consequently facilitates the HER or OER."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_008,"It is reported that alloying Co with a less noble metal (Ru) could remarkably enhance the catalytic performance of this kind of M@N-C electrocatalysts. 45 This points to the possibility to synthesize new and efficient HER catalysts by combining Ru-based alloys and carbon materials. Despite these advances, most of the reported M@N-C electrocatalysts could function only in an acidic or basic electrolyte, which limits their applications in different water electrolysis technologies with different demands on the pH value of the electrolyte."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_009,"Therefore, it is highly desirable yet challenging to design and fabricate active H2-evolving electrocatalysts that exhibit high performance for the HER in a wide pH range. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a class of porous inorganic-organic hybrid crystals with a tunable chemical composition and structure, have been widely used as precursors/sacricial templates for controllable fabrication of many kinds of carboncontaining composite micro/nano-structures. 52-57 In most early studies, conventional MOF materials have been commonly used as precursors for conversion into inorganic functional materials via pyrolysis or chemical reaction with desirable reagents."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,1,P22_p1_010,"In addition to using simple MOFs as precursors, postsynthetic modication or functionalization of MOFs into novel multicompositional MOF-based analogues could be used to construct complex nanostructured materials with designable structures and compositions via further chemical transformation, which would offer a potential possibility to tailor their properties. 45,58,59 In the present work, we report the preparation of low-ruthenium-content NiRu alloy nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped carbon (denoted as NiRu@N-C) by coupling with Ru-doping and further pyrolysis treatment under an inert atmosphere using a Ni-based MOF as the starting material (Fig. 1)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,1,2,P22_p1_011,"Beneting from the structural advantages and component synergy, the as-obtained NiRu@N-C nanohybrids exhibited excellent electrocatalytic activity and good stability towards the HER in a wide pH range. Remarkably, the HER activities of the optimized NiRu@N-C sample (S-2) under acidic and alkaline conditions, featuring low overpotentials and small Tafel slopes, are comparable with those of commercially available Pt/C catalysts. Fig. 1 Schematic of the preparation of NiRu@N-C nanohybrids."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_012,"The Ni-based MOF (Ni2-(bdc)2ted) was rst synthesized according to the previous studies with some modications by reacting Ni(NO3)2$6H2O, bdc (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) and ted (triethylenediamine) in DMF solution at 130  C for 24 h (see the Experimental section for details; ESI†). 60,61 Fig. 2a presents the typical scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of the as-synthesized Ni-based MOF, in which many sheet-like structures with a lateral size of 300-800 nm and a thickness of 20-30 nm can be observed."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_013,"Aer further reacting with RuCl3 solution, the sheet-like morphology of the original Ni-based MOF was generally maintained except that the surface became slightly rough, as displayed in Fig. 2b. The as-obtained Rudoped Ni-based MOF analogues were used as the starting materials for further pyrolysis treatment at 800  C under a nitrogen ow (see the Experimental section for details). A series of NiRu@N-C samples with different Ru contents were obtained by using Ru-doped Ni-based MOF analogues with different doped concentrations as pyrolysis precursors. The asobtained products are denoted as S-0, S-1, S-2, and S-3, respectively, according to their Ru contents (Table S1, ESI†). Fig."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_014,"2c is a representative SEM image of the S-2 sample. It was found that numerous irregular particle-like nanostructures with diameters of 10-20 nm were obtained aer annealing treatment. Further transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis indicates that these irregular particle-like nanostructures consist of NiRu alloy nanoparticle cores that are coated with ultrathin carbon shells (Fig. 2d and e). The high resolution TEM (HRTEM) image shows clear crystal planes with a d-spacing of 0.21 nm, which can be readily indexed to the (111) crystal plane of the face centred cubic (fcc) Ni phase (Fig. 2f)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_015,"From the HRTEM image, no lattice fringe spaces of metallic Ru were found, implying the formation of the NiRu alloy which retained the crystal structure of metallic Ni. Moreover, we can clearly observe that the inner alloy nanoparticles were surrounded by 2-5 layers of graphitized carbon shells with an interlayer distance of 0.34 nm (Fig. 2f). The S-0, S-1, and S-3 samples possessed a similar morphology and structure to those of S-2 (Fig. S1, ESI†). Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra show the presence of Ni, Ru, C, and N in these annealed products (Fig. S2, ESI†)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_016,"The composition of the nanoparticles was further examined by scanning TEM-energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (STEM-EDX), which revealed a homogeneous distribution of Ni and Ru elements in the inner particles, further conrming the formation of bimetallic NiRu alloy nanoparticles (Fig. 2g-i). The Ru content in S-2 was detected to be 1.86 wt% by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES) (Table S2, ESI†)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,2,2,P22_p2_017,"Fig. 2 (a) SEM image of Ni-based MOFs. (b) SEM image of Ru-doped Ni-based MOF analogues. (c) SEM image of typical NiRu@N-C (S-2 sample). (d and e) TEM images, (f) HRTEM image and (g-i) HAADFSTEM images and the corresponding EDX mappings of Ni (h) and Ru (i) of the S-2 sample."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_018,"The corresponding X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of the S-2 sample is shown in Fig. 3a. The very weak and broad C (002) peak conrmed that graphitized carbon shells with an ultrathin thickness had been formed, which was in agreement with the results of the HRTEM analysis. Besides, the XRD pattern of the S-2 sample showed no additional reections except a series of Bragg reections corresponding to the diffractions from the NiRu alloy (Fig. 3a). From the Raman spectrum of the S-2 sample, two characteristic peaks of graphene with intensity maxima at about 1350 (D band) and 1580 cm ␀1 (G band) were observed, further conrming the presence of the graphitized carbon shell (see the inset in Fig."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_019,"3a). Further information on the composition was obtained by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The XPS survey spectrum indicated the presence of Ni, Ru, C and N elements (Fig. S3, ESI†). The Ni 2p XPS spectrum revealed that the Ni in the S-2 sample maintained its metallic state, which was consistent with the XRD and HRTEM results (Fig. 3b). High-resolution XPS spectra in the C 1s and Ru 3d regions are displayed in Fig. 3c. The C 1s peak can be deconvoluted into two peaks centered at 284.7 and 285.9 eV, corresponding to the C-C and C-N bonds, respectively. 62,63 The Ru 3d peaks at 280.2 and 284.4 eV were assigned to Ru 3d 5/2 and Ru 3d 3/2 for Ru 0 . 5,64 Furthermore, as shown in Fig."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_020,"3d, the three peaks in the N 1s spectrum of the S-2 sample are assigned to pyridinic N (398.7 eV), pyrrolic N (400.5 eV), and graphitic N (402.3 eV), respectively. 62,63 These results suggested that NiRu@N-C composed of bimetallic NiRu nanoalloy cores and ultrathin N-doped carbon shells has been successfully synthesized through doping ruthenium species into Ni-based MOFs followed by pyrolysis treatment."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_021,"During the pyrolysis treatment process under a nitrogen atmosphere, Ni 2+ and Ru 3+ cations from the Ru-doped Ni-MOF analogues were in situ reduced to form bimetallic RuNi alloy nanocrystals. Meanwhile, the organic components (i.e., bdc, ted) in the precursor could provide carbon and nitrogen sources for the formation of nitrogen-doped graphitized carbon layers surrounding the RuNi alloy nanocrystals, which is able to efficiently avoid the agglomeration of the inner alloy nanoparticles."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_022,"In such a process, the presence of in situ formed metal particles could help greatly improve the crystallinity and electrical conductivity of nitrogen-doped graphitized carbon layers by catalytic graphitization, which in turn function to efficiently avoid the agglomeration of the inner alloy nanoparticles. Fig. 3 Structural analysis of the typical RuNi@N-C nanohybrid (S-2 sample). (a) XRD. The inset of (a) is the Raman spectrum. (b) XPS spectrum of Ni 2p. (c) XPS spectrum of C 1s and Ru 3d. (d) XPS spectrum of N 1s."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_023,"Inspired by the unique alloy-carbon core-shell structure of these as-fabricated NiRu@N-C nanohybrids and the attractive electrochemical properties of other reported M@N-C analogues, we studied their electrocatalytic activity towards the HER at various pH values by loading them on a polished glassy carbon (GC) electrode and using a three-electrode electrochemical cell system. The HER electrocatalytic activity of the series of NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts was rst investigated in acidic media (0.5 M H2SO4, pH ¼ 0). The catalytic performance of commercially available Pt/C (20 wt%, Johnson Matthey) was also examined for comparison. Fig."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_024,"4 shows the electrocatalytic activity of various electrocatalysts in 0.5 M H2SO4 electrolyte. As expected, the Pt/C catalyst-decorated GC electrode exhibited high HER catalytic activity with negligible overpotential and a low Tafel slope of about 31 mV dec ␀1 , which is consistent with the reported values in the literature. 5,18 It was found that the undoped S-0 sample, which is Ni@N-C obtained by direct pyrolysis of the Ni-based MOF, exhibited a poor HER activity with an overpotential of about 450 mV to yield a current density of 10 mA cm ␀2 , which are far inferior to those of the doped counterparts of the NiRu alloy encapsulated in N-doped graphitized carbon shells (Fig. 4a)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_025,"These results suggested that the introduction of a suitable amount of Ru into Ni could boost the electrocatalytic activity in such a material system, mainly due to the higher efficiency of electron transfer from the alloy core to the graphitized carbon shell and the enhanced carbon-hydrogen bond in such an alloy-carbon core-shell structure. 45 Among the series of NiRu@N-C catalysts, the S-2 sample exhibited the best activity among various NiRu@N-C samples, which is much better than those of undoped S-0 and other two doped samples (S-1 and S-3)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_026,"In view of these NiRu@N-C samples varying in their Ru content (Table S2, ESI†), the best performance of the S-2 catalyst may be contributed to its stronger electronic coupling of Ni and Ru or interatomic charge polarization in it, as explained in the literature. 65 Moreover, the HER activity of the S-2 catalyst is also superior to that of its NiRu@C counterpart which has a similar alloycarbon core-shell structure but no N-doping atoms in the carbon shell (Fig. S5a-c, ESI†). The required overpotential to achieve a 10 mA cm ␀2 current density was 50 mV with a Tafel slope of 36 mV dec ␀1 (Fig. 4a and b), which is close to that of the commercially available Pt/C catalyst."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_027,"Notably, these values of the optimized S-2 samples were better than those of many other reported Ni-based HER electrocatalysts in acidic electrolyte, such as Ni0.85Se (240 mV, 49.3 mV dec ␀1 ), 12 Ni2P (120 mV, 46 mV dec ␀1 ), 23 CoNi@NC (142 mV, 104 mV dec ␀1 ), 48 MoxCNi@NCV (75 mV, 45 mV dec ␀1 ), 42 and Fe-Ni3C (178 mV, 36.5 mV dec ␀1 ). 31 Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was employed to study the interfacial electron transfer dynamics of these NiRu@N-C catalysts. As shown in Fig. S6a (ESI†), compared to S-0 and other NiRu@N-C catalysts (S-1 and S-3), the S-2 catalyst has the lowest charge-transfer resistance, indicating its superior charge transport kinetics."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_028,"Moreover, it was found that the S-2 catalyst also exhibited highly efficient HER activities in acidic media when using a graphite rod as the counter electrode (Fig. S7, ESI†). These results reveal that the enhanced HER performance is attributed to the intrinsic excellent catalytic activity of the S-2 catalyst."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,3,3,P22_p3_029,"Fig. 4 Electrochemical evaluation of the as-obtained series of NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts in 0.5 M H2SO4. (a) Polarization curves of various NiRu@N-C and commercial 20 wt% Pt/C catalysts. (b) Tafel plots of various NiRu@N-C and commercial 20 wt% Pt/C catalysts. (c) Polarization curves of the S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode before and after 1000 CV cycles. (d) Chronopotentiometry test of the S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode held at a constant current density of 10 mA cm ␀2 for 10 h. The polarization curves in (a) and (c) were iRcorrected. S-0 represents Ni@N-C catalysts; S-1, S-2 and S-3 represent NiRu@N-C catalysts with various Ru contents."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_030,"Durability and stability are two other important criteria to evaluate the performance of an electrocatalyst especially for practical applications. We chose the optimal S-2 sample as the model catalyst to evaluate the durability and stability. The durability of the catalytic response was evaluated by cycling the S-2 decorated GC electrode between +0.1 and ␀0.09 V (vs. the RHE) continuously for 1000 cyclic voltammetry (CV) cycles. Aer 1000 cycles, the polarization curve of the S-2 sample decorated GC electrode nearly overlaps with the initial one, indicating a superior durability (Fig. 4c)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_031,"To assess the long-term stability, we carried out a chronopotentiometry test at a constant current density of 10 mA cm ␀2 for 10 h. As shown in Fig. 4d, the overpotential of the S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode remained almost constant during the testing, revealing an excellent catalytic stability. It is believed that the superior catalytic stability and durability were due to the structure and morphology of these NiRu@N-C catalysts, as analyzed by TEM (Fig. S4, ESI†)"
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_032,"If the electrocatalytic H2 evolution has to be made applicable for large scale applications, it is of signicance to develop electrocatalysts with high performance and good stability in a wide pH range of aqueous media. We further evaluated the HER performance of the NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts in an alkaline electrolyte (1.0 M KOH, pH ¼ 14). As shown in Fig. 5a, the S-2 sample exhibited the best electrocatalytic activity among the various NiRu@N-C samples. Moreover, the HER activity of the S-2 catalyst was also better than that of the NiRu@C catalyst in alkaline media (Fig. S5d, ESI†), indicating that doping N atoms in the carbon shell is favourable for improving the HER activity."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_033,"The HER polarization curves of the S-2 sample showed a 10 mA cm ␀2 current density at a low overpotential of 32 mV, which was almost as low as that of the Pt/C catalyst. This overpotential was also lower than the values of the currently most promising Ni-based HER electrocatalysts in alkaline medium, such as Ni-0.2NH3 (52 mV), 60 Ni0.95Co0.05PS3 (77 mV), 66 NiCu@C (74 mV), 67 and Ni2P (290 mV). 24 The corresponding Tafel slope of the S-2 decorated GC electrode was measured to be 64 mV dec ␀1 (Fig. 5b). Electrochemical impedance characterization revealed a decreased transport resistance of the S-2 catalyst compared to S-0, S-1 and S-3 catalysts (Fig. S6b, ESI†). As shown in Fig."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_034,"5c, in the durability testing of the S-2 electrocatalyst, negligible differences can be observed even aer 1000 CV cycles. The excellent long-term catalytic stability was also conrmed by the chronopotentiometry testing at a 10 mA cm ␀2 current density for 10 h (Fig. 5d)."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_035,"Inspired by the high catalytic activity of these NiRu@N-C catalysts under acidic and alkaline conditions, we further studied their electrocatalytic response towards the HER in 1.0 M phosphate buffer solution (PBS) (pH ¼ 7). As observed from the polarization curves, to achieve current densities of 1 and 10 mA cm ␀2 , the best S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode required overpotentials of 35 and 482 mV, respectively (Fig. S8a, ESI†). The HER proceeding in neutral media sustains slower kinetics as compared to that under alkaline and acidic conditions Time (h)"
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,4,4,P22_p4_036,"Fig. 5 Electrochemical evaluation of the as-obtained series of NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts in 1.0 M KOH. (a) Polarization curves of various NiRu@N-C and commercial 20 wt% Pt/C catalysts. (b) Tafel plots of various NiRu@N-C and commercial 20 wt% Pt/C catalysts. (c) Polarization curves of the S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode before and after 1000 CV cycles. (d) Chronopotentiometry test of the S-2 catalyst decorated GC electrode held at a constant current density of 10 mA cm ␀2 for 10 h. The polarization curves in (a) and (c) were iRcorrected. S-0 represents Ni@N-C catalysts; S-1, S-2 and S-3 represent NiRu@N-C catalysts with various Ru contents."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,5,5,P22_p5_037,"mainly because of a larger ohmic loss, slower ion migration rate and lower proton concentration. 41 Also, the good durability and stability under neutral conditions are conrmed by cycling the S-2 catalyst-decorated GC electrode continuously for 1000 CV cycles and chronoamperometric measurement at 10 mA cm ␀2 for 40 h (Fig. S8b, ESI†). These above results indicated that, by optimizing the structure and composition parameters, these asprepared series of NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts could exhibit a robust catalytic activity towards the HER in aqueous reaction media over a wide pH range."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,5,5,P22_p5_038,"As for the origin of the markedly enhanced HER activity of the optimized NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts, it is believed that the structural advantages and component synergy have an important effect in improving the reactivity towards the HER. First, the carbon shell in such a NiRu@N-C core-shell structure could effectively protect the inner NiRu alloy cores from corrosion and aggregation during long-term electrocatalytic measurement. Secondly, the ultrathin carbon shell facilitates the electrons transferring from the inner alloy core to the surface of the carbon shell for the catalytic reactions."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,5,5,P22_p5_039,"Thirdly, the interaction and synergy of the bimetallic alloy core and N-doped carbon shell could create sufficient localized reactive sites by modifying the charge distribution on the carbon layer surface, thus change the local work function for related reactive species adsorption and consequently facilitate the HER."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,5,5,P22_p5_040,"In summary, we have successfully fabricated low-rutheniumcontent NiRu@N-C nanohybrids by coupling with Ru-doping treatment and further pyrolysis under an inert atmosphere using a Ni-based MOF as the starting material, and further investigated them as a HER electrocatalyst. Beneting from their structural advantages and component synergy, the NiRu@N-C electrocatalysts show high HER catalytic activity and superior stability in a wide pH range. Remarkably, the HER activities of the optimized S-2 sample under acidic and alkaline conditions are comparable with those of commercially available Pt/C catalysts."
P22,c7ta09939h,c7ta09939h.pdf,5,5,P22_p5_041,"The required overpotentials to achieve a current density of 10 mA cm ␀2 over S-2 catalysts were only 50 (in 0.5 M H2SO4) and 32 mV (in 1.0 M KOH), corresponding to Tafel slopes of 36 (in 0.5 M H2SO4) and 64 mV dec ␀1 (in 1.0 M KOH), respectively. The results here might offer a new avenue for the rational design and synthesis of highly efficient and cost-effective electrocatalysts for water splitting and other electrochemical applications. Conflicts of interest There are no conicts to declare. Acknowledgements"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_000,"www.chemcatchem.org AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual-Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and Electrocatalytic Hydrogen Generation Ramani Hazarika, [a] Deepshikha Roy, [a] and Kalyanjyoti Deori* [a]"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_001,"In this work, we have developed a simple solvothermal protocol for designing spherically arranged AgNi alloy NPs with uniform sizes and tunable compositions. This as-synthesized heterogeneous catalyst demonstrates excellent catalytic activity for nitrile hydration in water without any additives, achieving up to 99% product yield at 80 °C within just 5 h. Our assynthesized catalyst is magnetically separable and stable under catalytic reaction conditions. A broad variety of nitriles was efficiently transformed into their respective amides using this catalyst with high selectivity."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_002,"This study introduces a sustainable and cost-effective approach for the eco-friendly synthesis of amides via nitrile hydration. In addition, the as-synthesized AgNi alloy exhibited excellent hydrogen evolution activity. Ag5Ni1 synthesized through precise control over the stoichiometry could attain a current density of 10 mA/cm 2 at an overpotential of 248 mV (versus RHE) which is the lowest among all the synthesized alloy samples. Ag5Ni1 alloy sample could also attain an industrial scale current density of 1000 mA/cm 2 at a record low overpotential of 858 mV (versus RHE)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_003,"The Tafel slopes of AgNi, Ag1Ni5, and Ag5Ni1 are found to be 135.6, 96.3, and 80.7 mV dec -1 , respectively, which is comparable to benchmark commercial catalysts. Such low values of Tafel slope values supports the outstanding potential of our as-synthesized alloy to be used as a highly efficient electrocatalyst for HER. The present work showcases the excellent catalytic efficacy of AgNi NPs as a heterogeneous catalyst in the promising field of selective organic transformations as well as electrochemical energy applications. 1. Introduction"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_004,"Alloy nanoparticles (NPs) with flexible and adjustable properties based on their size, shape, and composition have attracted significant research interest due to their broad applicability in catalysis, organic transformations, fuel cells, sensing, and other advanced technologies. Alloy NPs have demonstrated superior performance compared to their monometallic counterparts, primarily due to synergistic effects between the constituent metals and efficient charge redistribution across different atomic sites."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_005,"[1,2] These unique characteristics have been effectively harnessed to achieve a variety of high-efficiency organic transformations, such as hydrogenation, CO oxidation and O2 reduction, oxidation reactions, cross coupling reactions, dye degradation processes etc. [3-6] AgNi alloy NPs have gained significant interest due to their enhanced catalytic efficiency, structural robustness, and synergistic effects, making them highly applicable across various fields. An easily recoverable and reusable catalyst, Fe3O4@CS_AgNi (with chitosan as the support), has been developed for efficient reduction reactions of 4-nitrophenol. AgNi alloy-decorated ZnO nanocomposites have been devel-"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_006,"KD's NAME (NanoMat&Energy) Lab, Department of Chemistry, Dibrugarh University, Dibrugarh, Assam 786004, India E-mail: kalchemdu@dibru.ac.in kalchemdu@gmail.com oped as efficient hybrid catalysts for the degradation of harmful organic pollutants. [7] Although silver and nickel nanoparticles individually possess valuable properties, their combination faces challenges such as lattice mismatch and differences in reduction potentials. Despite these obstacles, advancements in synthesis techniques, including chemical reduction and physical methods like vapor quenching, γ -radiation and gas condensation, have facilitated the development of AgNi nanoparticles with improved phase stability."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_007,"Recent studies have explored their potential in catalysis, photocatalysis, magnetic applications, and optical limiting, demonstrating their versatility and effectiveness in emerging technologies. [8-12]"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_008,"The development of efficient and multifunctional catalysts is of great interest in modern research, particularly those that can serve dual purposes in both organic synthesis and energyrelated applications. Amide bond formation is a fundamental transformation in organic chemistry; [13-20] however, conventional methods such as the Beckmann rearrangement, [21] Wolff reaction, [22] Ugi reaction, [23] Staudinger reaction, [24] Schmidt reaction, [25] and Ritter reactions [26] are often associated with major drawbacks including the use of toxic reagents, high costs, excessive waste generation, and low overall efficiency."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_009,"To overcome these limitations, various catalytic methods have been introduced, such as Pd-catalyzed N-acylation, [27] Co-mediated alkene-amine coupling, [28] and Fe-promoted amidation, [29] but these approaches continue to suffer from challenges related to limited substrate scope, harsh reaction conditions, and environmental concerns. In this context, nitrile hydration has emerged as a promising alternative due to its high atom economy and direct route to amides; [13,30-32] nevertheless, it remains constrained by difficulties in controlling selectivity, as reactions frequently proceed further to carboxylic acids."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,1,1,P23_p1_010,"[33] Although enzymatic systems such as nitrile hydratase offer excellent selectivity, they are limited by narrow substrate compatibility. Homogeneous catalytic systems, while effective, face issues of difficult separation, low recyclability, and poor long-term stability, while most of the reported heterogeneous catalysts still demand high temperatures and lack easy recovery or reusability. [34-39] These persistent drawbacks highlight the need for the development of robust, efficient, and recyclable catalytic systems capable of achieving selective nitrile hydration under mild, sustainable, and environmentally benign conditions."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_011,"At the same time, with growing global energy demands and heightened environmental concerns, there is increasing emphasis on sustainable and green energy technologies. Hydrogen energy, in particular, has gained significant attention as a clean and renewable alternative to fossil fuels due to its potential to reduce carbon emissions and mitigate urban pollution. Among various strategies for hydrogen production, water splitting, especially the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), offers an efficient route for large-scale hydrogen generation."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_012,"[40-42] Silver-based electrocatalysts have gained significant attention in energy applications due to their high catalytic activity, stability, and cost-effectiveness compared to noble metal alternatives. Recent studies highlight their effectiveness in electrochemical CO2 reduction and hydrogen evolution reactions (HER), with nanostructured Ag catalysts demonstrating enhanced selectivity and efficiency. The development of Ag-based alloys and nanostructured materials further improves their performance, making them promising candidates for sustainable energy conversion technologies."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_013,"[43-45] A recent study reports the development of N-doped porous carbon-supported AgNi binary alloys derived from Ni-ABDC precursors, demonstrating excellent HER activity and durability in both acidic and alkaline media, highlighting a promising strategy for designing efficient electrocatalysts for hydrogen energy applications. [46] But, the reliance on a complex synthesis route and the use of a carbon-based support, making it difficult to isolate the intrinsic activity of the AgNi alloy."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_014,"This reflects a broader challenge in this field, as most energyrelated catalysts still depend on support materials, underscoring the need for more efficient, stable and scalable systems with minimized reliance on complex supports. In this context, designing a single catalyst capable of efficiently promoting both nitrile hydration and HER not only maximizes material utility but also aligns with the goals of green chemistry and energy sustainability."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_015,"In this study, AgNi binary alloy NPs were synthesized via a simple solvothermal method, where tuning the Ag-to-Ni ratio was found to directly influence their catalytic function. A 1:1 molar ratio proved most effective for the hydration of benzonitrile to benzamide, attributed to the synergistic interaction between Ag and Ni active sites, while an Ag-rich 5:1 ratio delivered superior electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) activity due to enhanced surface availability and charge transfer properties."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_016,"These findings demonstrate that adjusting the metal composition enables selective optimization for different Societies Publishing catalytic applications, highlighting the versatile and multifunctional nature of AgNi alloys in both organic transformations and energy conversion processes. 2. Results and Discussion"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_017,"A simple solvothermal procedure was followed to synthesize AgNi alloy NPs as shown in Figure 1 (see the Experimental Section for details). The crystallographic structure, phase purity and composition of the as-synthesized nanoparticles were examined using PXRD pattern. Figure 2 displays the XRD patterns of standard metallic Ag and Ni, and the as-synthesized AgNi NPs. Ag NPs typically show four distinct peaks at 2θ values of 38.20°, 44.34°, 64.47°, and 77.42°, which correspond to the (111), (200), (220), and (311) planes of a face-centered cubic (fcc) lattice (JCPDS PDF#04-0783)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_018,"Similarly, Ni NPs exhibit three characteristic peaks at 2θ angles of 44.52°, 51.93°, and 76.42°, corresponding to the (111), (200), and (311) planes of fcc lattice of Ni (JCPDS PDF#040850). In the AgNi NPs pattern, the four peaks appeared at 38.04°, 44.25°, 64.47°, and 77.41°, matching the (111), (200), (220), and (311) planes of Ag. Notably, the distinct peak at 2θ angle of 52.15°, associated with the (200) plane of fcc Ni, was notably missing, suggesting that Ni NPs did not form as a separate phase and also revealed the formation of an alloy."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_019,"These findings indicate the formation of an AgNi solid solution, where Ni atoms are incorporated into the Ag crystal lattice, thereby confirming the coexistence of Ag and Ni in the resulting nanoparticles. The absence of any additional peaks associated with NiO, ZnO, or any other impurities, confirms the phase purity of the synthesized material. X-ray diffraction analysis also revealed a systematic shift of diffraction peaks toward higher 2θ values with decreasing Ag content (Figure 2b), indicating lattice contraction due to the substitution of larger Ag atoms by smaller Ni atoms within the alloy matrix."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_020,"XPS measurements were employed to verify the elemental composition and valence states of Ag and Ni in the prepared sample (AgNi). The C 1s XPS spectra (Figure 3a) were calibrated using a reference binding energy of 284.7 eV. Figure 3b displays the wide survey scan, which shows the presence of Ag and Ni in the sample. Although small peaks for C and O are also observed, likely originating from residual CTAB or surface oxidation. The XPS spectrum of Ag 3d displays relatively broad 3d3/2 and 3d5/2 peaks, centered at 374.2 and 368.2 eV, respectively, which are characteristic of metallic Ag, as illustrated in Figure 3(c)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_021,"The Ni 2p XPS spectra, recorded in the energy range of 890 to 850 eV, display two broad peaks corresponding to the 2p1/2 and 2p3/2 states, centered at 872.1 and 854.1 eV, respectively, for Ni 0 state. Additionally, two satellite peaks appear at 879.2.15 and 861 eV further indicate the presence of Ni 2+ , likely due to surface oxidation (Figure 3(d))."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_022,"The structural characteristics, including size, morphology, and crystallinity of the as-synthesized alloy nanoparticles, were further analyzed using FESEM and TEM imaging, as shown in Figure 4. FESEM images of the AgNi sample (Figure 4a,b) predominantly reveal spherical-shaped particles. Figure 4c-e display TEM and HRTEM images, which confirm the presence of spherical nanostructures with an average diameter of approximately 56 nm. Additionally, from Figure 4g-i, the measured d-spacing from the lattice fringes between the adjacent planes were found to be 0.204 nm and 0.234 corresponding well to the (200) and (111) crystallographic planes of the AgNi alloy NPs."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_023,"To further validate the crystallographic orientation of the Ag and Ni planes, a single particle area was examined at higher magnification, focusing on a defect-free region (Figure 4k). Lattice spacings of 0.202 and 0.24 nm were observed, which closely correspond to the (200) and (111) plane spacing of bulk Ag (0.236 nm), respectively, as shown in Figure 4j-l . Minor deviations in the measured (111) fringe spacing for Ag are attributed to slight crystal tilts, a commonly observed phenomenon. The 2D fast Fourier transform (2D-FFT) analysis derived from the selected region (shown in the inset Figure 4i,l), further confirming the presence of the (111) crystallographic plane in the nanoparticles."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,2,P23_p2_024,"Additionally, the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the AgNi alloy NPs, shown in Figure 3f, demonstrates distinct diffraction Societies Publishing Societies Publishing rings corresponding to the (111), (200), (220), and (311) planes, indicating a well-defined face-centered cubic (fcc) structure. These findings are in good agreement with the XRD results, affirming the crystalline nature and phase structure of the prepared sample. In addition, the Ag5Ni1 sample was further examined using TEM to investigate its morphological and structural features (See Supporting Information, Figure S1)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,2,3,P23_p2_025,"The TEM and HRTEM images reveal uniformly distributed spherical nanoparticles, and the 2DFFT pattern obtained from the selected region (See Supporting Information, Figure S1(b) and (c)) shows a lattice fringe spacing of 0.238 nm, corresponding to the (111) crystallographic plane of the Ag5Ni1 NPs. Figure 1. Schematic representation of the synthesis of AgNi. Figure 2. PXRD pattern of (a) as-synthesized AgNi, standard Ag and Ni (b) as-synthesized Ag1Ni5, Ag5Ni1, AgNi, standard Ag and Ni. Figure 3. (a) XPS spectra of C 1 s and (b) wide survey scan and high-resolution XPS spectra of (c) Ag 3d and (d) Ni 2p of AgNi alloy NPs."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,4,5,P23_p4_026,"Figure 4. (a) and (b) FESEM, (c)-(e)TEM, (f ) SAED pattern, (g)-(l) HR-TEM [inset: corresponding 2D-FFT], (m)-(o) elemental mapping of constituents Ag and Ni of as-synthesized AgNi alloy NPs. Figure 5. (a) and (b) M versus H Hysteresis loop of as-synthesized AgNi alloy NPs at room temperature."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,5,5,P23_p5_027,"The magnetic characteristics of the synthesized AgNi alloy NPs were investigated at room temperature (25 °C) using a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM). The variation of magnetization (M) with respect to the applied magnetic field (H) was analyzed to evaluate the magnetic response of the samples. The AgNi alloy NPs exhibited weak ferromagnetic behavior, which can be attributed to the incorporation of Ni atoms within the Ag lattice. The magnetic hysteresis loop of AgNi alloy NPs (Figure 5) exhibited a saturation magnetization (MS) of 1.17 emu/g, coercivity (HC) of 272.2 Oe and a remanent magnetization (Mr) of 0.34 emu/g."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,5,5,P23_p5_028,"Figure 6. Catalytic activity of Ni NPs, Ag1Ni5, AgNi, and Ag5Ni1. Reactions conditions: benzonitrile (1 mmol), catalyst (15 mg), water (3 mL), 5 h, and 80 °C (temperature)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,5,5,P23_p5_029,"A series of catalysts, designated as AgNi, Ni, Ag1Ni5, Ag5Ni1 were synthesized with varying Ag and Ni content and evaluated for their catalytic activity in the hydration of benzonitrile (Table 1) (Figure 6) and for electrochemical HER activity. The catalytic behavior of AgNi alloys reveals a pronounced dependence on composition, underscoring the importance of metal ratio in tailoring activity for specific reactions. The Ag:Ni molar ratio of 1:1 was found to be most effective for promoting the targeted benzamide formation, likely due to a balanced electronic structure and synergistic interaction between Ag and Ni active sites that facilitate the reaction pathway."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,5,5,P23_p5_030,"In contrast, Ag-rich alloy with a 5:1 molar ratio exhibited significantly enhanced electrocatalytic performance, which may be attributed to the increased availability of Ag surface sites and modified electronic properties that favor charge transfer processes. These findings highlight the tunable nature of AgNi alloys, where adjusting the Ag-to-Ni ratio enables Societies Publishing Societies Publishing optimization for distinct catalytic applications, offering a versatile platform for reaction-specific catalyst design."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_031,"Figure 7. (a) Arrhenius plot for hydration of benzonitrile. Reaction conditions: benzonitrile (1 mmol), catalyst (15 mg), water (3 mL), 5 h, 80 °C (temperature). (b) Illustration of the activation energy barriers involved in the hydration of nitriles catalyzed by H2SO4, Fe0.7Cu0.3Ox-300, Ru(OH)x/Al2O3, and AgNi."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_032,"The as-synthesized catalysts, AgNi, Ni, Ag1Ni5, and Ag5Ni1 were evaluated for their catalytic activity in the hydration of benzonitrile (See Supporting Information, Table S1). Notably, no benzamide product was detected in the absence of a catalyst (Table 1, entry 2), confirming that catalytic intervention is essential for the reaction. Pure Ni and Ag NPs exhibited negligible activity for benzamide formation (Table 1, entry 3 and 4). In contrast, Ag1Ni5, Ag5Ni1, and AgNi achieved 34%, 56%, and 99% conversion of benzonitrile, respectively (Table 1, entries 5-7), highlighting the synergistic contribution of both Ag and Ni toward enhanced catalytic performance."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_033,"Among these, AgNi with a 1:1 molar ratio demonstrated the highest activity, achieving 99% conversion with 100% selectivity toward benzamide. Therefore, AgNi alloy NPs were selected as the most efficient catalyst for the hydration of nitriles, and the reaction conditions using AgNi catalyst were subsequently optimized, as summarized in Table 1."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_034,"Among various organic solvents tested (Table 1, entries 8-14), water proved to be the most effective medium (Table 1, entry 1), likely due to its role as both a solvent and a reactant. Reaction temperature was found to be a key parameter, with catalytic efficiency improving as the temperature increased (Table 1, entries 1, 15). However, the difference in activity between 80 and 100 °C was minimal (Table 1, entries 1, 16-18). In line with green chemistry principles, 80 °C was selected as the optimal temperature for the nitrile hydration process. Additionally, a 5-h reaction time was found to be optimal, as it led to a 99% yield of benzamide (Table 1, entry 1, 19, and 20)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_035,"Optimization studies determined that a catalyst loading of 15 mg (Table S3), using water as the reaction medium at 80 °C for 5 h, provided the most efficient reaction conditions. Kinetic experiments have been conducted to elucidate the basic mechanism of nitrile hydration using our developed protocol. By analyzing the Arrhenius plot (Figure 7a), we have determined the activation energy as 51.74 kJmol -1 which is notably lower than some previously reported literatures (Figure 7b), suggesting our method is more efficient. Figure 8. Gram scale synthesis."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_036,"For potential industrial implementation, critical parameters such as scalability, substrate versatility, and catalyst reusability must be thoroughly assessed. Accordingly, three scale-up reactions using benzonitrile as the substrate were performed with AgNi under optimized conditions. Accordingly, three scale-up reactions using benzonitrile as the substrate were performed with AgNi under optimized conditions. These reactions were carried out with different substrate quantities, along with corresponding AgNi catalyst loadings, using water as the reaction medium (Figure 8). Remarkably, the corresponding product, benzamide, was obtained in excellent yields of 92%, 88%, and 82%, respectively."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_037,"The findings highlight the economic feasibility and promising potential of the AgNi NPs as a catalyst for industrialscale conversion of aromatic nitriles to their corresponding amides. To confirm the heterogeneous nature and assess metal leaching, a hot filtration test was performed. The model reaction was allowed to proceed for 2 h, after which the catalyst was"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_038,"18673899, 2025, 22, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/cctc.202501369 by Zhejiang University, Wiley Online Library on [27/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License removed by hot filtration. The filtrate was then stirred for an additional 3 h under identical conditions, during which no further product formation was detected, indicating the absence of catalytically active species in the filtrate (Figure 9a)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,6,P23_p6_039,"The reusability of the catalyst was assessed over six consecutive reaction cycles (Figure 9b). For this, the catalyst was magnetically separated from the reaction mixture after completion of the reaction (Figure 9c), washed with solvent and dried before reuse. A small decrease in catalytic activity was observed in consecutive cycles, and an overall decrease of 10% occurred in benzamide yield occurred only after the sixth cycle, which can be attributed to catalyst mass loss during recovery and partial blockage of active sites due to strong substrate-catalyst interactions."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,6,7,P23_p6_040,"Moreover, powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) and TEM analysis conducted before and after catalytic cycles revealed consistent diffraction patterns, confirming the structural integrity and phase stability of the catalyst throughout repeated use (See Supporting Information, Figures S2 and S3). Figure 9. (a) Hot filtration test (b) recyclability test for AgNi and (c) magnetic separation of the catalyst (AgNi) from the reaction mixture."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,7,7,P23_p7_041,"The catalytic versatility of AgNi was examined across a range of substituted benzonitriles, as summarized in Table 2. The catalyst exhibited consistently high activity toward substrates bearing both electron-donating and electron-withdrawing groups, with minimal influence from electronic substituent effects on overall catalytic performance. In conclusion, our as-prepared AgNi alloy NPs efficiently catalyzed the hydration of aromatic nitriles bearing various substituents in aqueous medium, demonstrating broad substrate compatibility."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,7,7,P23_p7_042,"Comparative analysis with previously reported catalysts (Table 3) showed that AgNi exhibited catalytic efficiency comparable to that of homogeneous system, delivering excellent yield of benzamide. Moreover, this catalyst operates under milder conditions than most reported heterogeneous catalysts, which typically require high temperatures for the reaction or complex synthetic procedures for the catalyst. 3. Electrochemical HER Performance"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,7,7,P23_p7_043,"The as-synthesized AgNi catalyst displayed an intriguing dual functionality, being active both in nitrile hydration and in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). However, rather than all samples behaving similarly across both reactions, a distinct and unusual trend was observed. Specifically, the Ag5Ni1 composition exhibited superior HER activity compared to other stoichiometric ratios, while the AgNi (with 1:1 molar ratio) sample performed more effectively in nitrile hydration leading to amide formation. This contrasting selectivity toward different catalytic processes is uncommon and highlights the composition-dependent catalytic behavior of AgNi alloys."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,7,7,P23_p7_044,"Ni possess high electronic conductivity, excellent redox property, and highly tunable electronic structure. But possible formation of Ni hydride during HER often impacts their stability due to which alloying of Ni with other metallic species is considered one of the best strategies for enhancing its stability and maintaining an optimum activity for HER. Optimum adsorption of hydrogen on the catalyst surface is highly dependent on their exposed facets. Ag (111) facets have the potential of strongly adsorbing H due to low adsorption energy requirement at a more positive potential."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,7,9,P23_p7_045,"[53-55] So, our stoichiometric-controlled synthesis of AgNi binary alloy offers a large number of highly exposed (111) planes of Ag which acts as active sites for hydrogen adsorption while also benefitting from the high electronic con- Societies Publishing Societies Publishing Table 2. Hydration of different nitriles by AgNi. 1000 Societies Publishing"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_046,"Figure 10. (a) HER polarization curve (LSV) of Ag5Ni1 (blue), Ag1Ni5 (green), AgNi (red), and Ni (pink) recorded at 2 mV/s scan rate. (b) Tafel plots of Ag5Ni1 (blue), Ag1Ni5 (green), and AgNi (red). (c) Nyquist plots of Ag5Ni1 (blue), Ag1Ni5 (green), AgNi (red), and Ni (black). (d) CV of Ag5Ni1 and (e) a plot of double layer current versus scan rate recorded in non-faradaic region at different scan rates (5-100 mV/s). (f ) Chronoamperometric stability test for Ag5Ni1 conducted for 14,000 s at a constant current density of 10 mA/cm 2 ."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_047,"ductivity of Ni. The electrochemical HER activity of all the three as-synthesized samples (AgNi, Ag5Ni1, and Ag1Ni5) was studied by using a conventional three-electrode configuration dipped in 1 M KOH solution as an electrolyte. The LSV plots of all the compositions of the alloy, bare glassy carbon electrode, and Ni nanoparticles are depicted in Figure 10a. All the LSV plots are 100% iR-compensated. The LSV plots revealed that alloying Ag with Ni increased the maximum current density up to 1000 mA/cm 2 at a overpotential of 1.43 V (versus RHE) with AgNi sample. Such high current density value is magnificently larger than that achieved for Ni nanoparticles and bare glassy carbon electrode."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_048,"This escalation in current density with the introduction of Ag signifies the crucial role of Ag for enhancing the electrochemical HER activity of the alloy as compared to the pristine Ni. Nevertheless, it is observed that the overpotential can be further reduced by adopting stoichiometric control over the composition of the as-synthesized alloy. The sample Ag5Ni1 could attain a current density as high as 1000 mA/cm 2 at a record low overpotential of 858 mV (versus RHE). Moreover, the overpotential required by our Ag5Ni1 alloy sample at 10 mA/cm 2 is comparable with many of the composite based electrodes, as shown in Table S1."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_049,"Our simple synthetic procedure and precise stoichiometric control over the as-synthesized alloy successfully outweighed many of the previously prepared Ni-based electrodes in terms of maximum current density achieved, Tafel slopes, and charge transfer resistance. Thus, our as-synthesized electrode has the potential for outstanding performance as a HER electrocatalyst and also provides further scope for its modulation. The kinetics and reaction mechanism of HER is further investigated by using Tafel plots, as shown in Figure 10b. The HER mechanism can either follow- Volmer-Heyrovsky or Volmer-Tafel reaction pathway."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_050,"The Tafel slopes of AgNi, Ag1Ni5, and Ag5Ni1 are found to be 135.6, 96.3, and 80.7 mV dec -1 , respectively, which corresponds to Volmer-Heyrovsky reaction pathway. Such low values of Tafel slope supports comparatively faster reaction kinetics on the surface of our synthesized electrocatalyst than many of the composite based electrodes, as illustrated in Table S1. The lowest Tafel slope of Ag5Ni1 among all the compositions indicates its faster reaction kinetics which can be attributed to the synergistic effect of Ag and Ni and optimum concentration of Ag in the alloy."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_051,"The comparatively better electrochemical activity of Ag5Ni1 is also evident from EIS measurements of the samples conducted in the frequency range 0.01 Hz-100 KHz, as shown in Figure 10c. The charge transfer resistance of Ag5Ni1 is found to be 5.78 Ω, which is lower as compared to AgNi, Ag1Ni5, and Ni nanoparticles (13.73, 25.01, and 12 Ω, respectively). To understand the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of the catalysts, the electric double layer capacitance (EDLC) of the electrocatalysts were obtained by running CV in non-faradaic potential region (-0.1 to 0 V versus Ag/AgCl) at different scan rates (5100 mV/s), as shown in Figure 10d,e."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_052,"ECSA is then obtained from EDLC measurement, using equation S1. It gives information about the actual number of electrochemically active sites available on the surface of the catalyst. The ECSA of Ag5Ni1 is found to be 33.75 cm 2 which is significantly large as compared to AgNi, Ag1Ni5, and Ni nanoparticles (7.5, 1.80 and 0.49 cm 2 , respectively). Such huge increment in ECSA is observed because larger number of active sites becomes available for adsorption of hydrogen with increase in Ag content at a less negative potential."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_053,"The weak Ag─H bond at a more negative potential and its synergy with Ni accelerates the release of adsorbed hydrogen for their recombination H + from electrolyte, thus maintaining an optimized interaction with active sites. [54,56,57] A higher value of ECSA promises availability of larger electrochemically active area for reaction and better charge transport across electrodeelectrolyte interface. The non-faradaic CV and corresponding ECSA measurement plot of AgNi, Ag1Ni5, and Ni nanoparticles is depicted in Figure S4 of the supporting information."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,9,P23_p9_054,"The Roughness factor calculated from the ratio of ECSA and geometrical area of the electrode, as depicted in equation S2, provides information about the specific activity of the electrocatalysts. The roughness factors of AgNi, Ag1Ni5, Ag5Ni1, and Ni nanoparticles are found to be 106.2, 25.5, 478.04, and 6.9, respectively. A comparatively higher value of roughness factor of Ag5Ni1 arises because of the larger ECSA obtained with rise in Ag content. A significantly higher roughness factor (Rf) of Ag5Ni1 provides more surface irregularities which results into their greater number of surface-active sites for HER."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,9,10,P23_p9_055,"To check the stability of the as-synthesized Ag5Ni1 alloy, chronoamperometric I-t test was performed at 10 mA/cm 2 current density for 1400 s (Figure 10f). It is clearly evident from I-t test, Ag5Ni1 showed excellent stability without significant loss in activity over a period of 14,000 s. As summarized in Table S2, the electrocatalytic performance of our as-synthesized Ag5Ni1 alloy for HER is comparable to or exceeding that of several previously reported catalysts, underscoring its high efficiency. 4. Conclusions"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_056,"In summary, AgNi alloy NPs with spherical morphology and tunable Ag/Ni composition were successfully synthesized via a novel and simple solvothermal method, and the integration of both metals was confirmed by PXRD, XPS, TEM, and HR-TEM analyses. Among the synthesized compositions, AgNi alloy NPs with a 1:1 molar ratio showed superior catalytic performance for the hydration of a wide range of aromatic nitriles in aqueous medium without organic solvents or additives, enabling the synthesis of twelve different amides under mild reaction conditions with excellent scalability, recyclability, and magnetic separability, and no significant loss of activity even after more than six cycles."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_057,"In contrast, Ag5Ni1 alloy NPs demonstrated outstanding electrocatalytic performance for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in water splitting. It is observed that precise control over the stoichiometry of the as-synthesized alloys enabled Ag5Ni1 to achieve a current density of 10 mA/cm 2 at an overpotential of 248 mV (versus RHE) which is the lowest among all the three compositions of the samples (507.24 and 532.24 mV (versus RHE) for AgNi and Ag1Ni5, respectively). The Ag5Ni1 is also capable of achieving an industrial level current density of 1000 mA/cm 2 at an appreciably low overpotential of 858 mV (versus RHE)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_058,"The enhanced activity of Ag5Ni1 can be attributed to lower tafel slope value of 80.7 mV dec -1 which ensures faster reaction kinetics across its surface. Moreover, Ag5Ni1 showed excellent stability over a period of 14,000 s without significant loss in activ- Societies Publishing ity. These results highlight that the catalytic efficiency of AgNi alloy NPs is strongly composition-dependent, with the 1:1 ratio being optimal for organic transformations and the 5:1 ratio for energy applications, underscoring their potential as multifunctional catalysts and offering new design principles for bridging sustainable synthesis with renewable energy technologies. 5. Experimental Section"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_059,"5.1. Materials and Reagents Silver acetate (AgC2H3O2, SRL, 98%), Nickel(II) acetate tetrahydrate (Ni(CH3COO)2.4H2O, 99.9%), cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB, Spectrochem, 98%), hydrazine hydrate (N2H4.H2O, Spectrochem, 80%), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), ethanol (99.9%), KOH, nafion (10 wt.%), isopropanol (99.5%) and deionized water were used. Benzonitrile (>99.0%) and related substrates were purchased from TCI. All the chemicals were used as received, without any additional purification. 5.2. Synthesis Procedure of AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_060,"AgNi alloy nanoparticles (NPs) were synthesized via a simple solvothermal method. Initially, 0.5 mmol of silver acetate was dissolved in 10 mL of ethanol and stirred for 15 min. Separately, 0.5 mmol of nickel acetate and 0.2 mmol of CTAB were dissolved in 6 mL of ethanol in another beaker and stirred, after which this solution was added dropwise to the silver-containing mixture under continuous stirring. Once a homogeneous solution was obtained, a mixture containing 0.5 g of NaOH and 10 mL of hydrazine hydrate (N2H4.H2O) was added dropwise, followed by an additional 20 min of stirring. The resulting mixture was then transferred to a Teflonlined autoclave and heated at 120 °C for 4 h."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_061,"After cooling to room temperature, the product was collected using a magnet and washed four times with deionized water and ethanol. The obtained catalyst was dried at 60 °C and subsequently calcined at 100 °C for 1 h and the final product was labeled as AgNi. Similarly, samples with molar ratios of 5:1 and 1:5 (Ag:Ni) were synthesized and labeled as Ag5Ni1 and Ag1Ni 5, respectively. 5.3. The Catalytic Procedure for Hydration of Nitrile"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_062,"A mixture of 1 mmol of benzonitrile and 3 mL of water were taken in a round bottom flask. To the reaction mixture, 15 mg of the assynthesized AgNi alloy NPs was added as catalyst. The mixture was then stirred at 80 °C for 5 h. Once the reaction was complete, the reaction mixture was subjected to a work-up process. The organic layer was then dried over anhydrous sodium sulfate (Na2SO4). The catalyst was magnetically separated, washed, and dried for reuse in subsequent cycles. The obtained product was purified by column chromatography and characterized by 1 H and 13 C NMR spectroscopy. 5.4. Electrochemical Measurement"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_063,"The electrocatalyst ink was prepared by dispersing 5 mg of the as-synthesized catalyst into 100 μL of iso-propanol and sonicating it for 3 h to obtain a well-dispersed suspension. 20 μL of Nafion (10 wt.%) was then added into the suspension followed by sonication for another 15 min. Then, 10 μL of the above suspension was drop-casted onto the surface of glassy-carbon electrode and dried"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_064,"18673899, 2025, 22, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/cctc.202501369 by Zhejiang University, Wiley Online Library on [27/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License at room temperature overnight in a desiccator. All the electrochemical measurements were performed using CHI-760E electrochemical workstation consisting of a conventional three-electrode system dipped in 1 M KOH as an electrolyte."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,10,P23_p10_065,"Glassy carbon electrode, a platinum electrode, and a Ag/AgCl electrode were used as working, counter, and reference electrode, respectively. All the electrochemical measurements were done at room temperature (∼25°C). Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was carried out in the potential window 0 to -1.5 V (versus RHE) at 2 mV/s. Non-faradaic cyclic voltammetry (CV) was recorded in the potential window -0.1 to 0 V (versus Ag/AgCl) at varied scan rates (5-100 mV/s). The charge transfer resistances (Rct) were determined by recording electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) in the frequency range 0.01 Hz-100KHz using AC voltage with an amplitude of 5 mV at open circuit potential (OCP)."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,10,11,P23_p10_066,All the potentials are converted into RHE scale by using Equation 1. E (vsRHE) = EAg/AgCl+0.0591pH+0.197 ( pH = 13.6for1MKOH ) (1) 5.5. Characterization
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,11,11,P23_p11_067,"The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the synthesized alloy NPs was collected using a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer equipped with monochromatic Cu Kα radiation (λ = 1.54,056 Å). The morphology of the specimen was examined using a field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, ƩIGMA model, Carl ZEISS, Germany). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), including low-magnification TEM, high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM), selected area electron diffraction (SAED) and scanning transmission electron microscopy with high-angle annular dark-field (STEM-HAADF) imaging, was carried out on a JOEL JEM-F200 microscope operating at 200 kV."
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,11,11,P23_p11_068,"X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analyses were conducted using a state-of-the-art ESCALAB Xi + system developed by Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA. 1 H and 13 C nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra were recorded on a Bruker Ascend spectrometer at 500 and 125 MHz, respectively. Acknowledgments"
P23,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and,ChemCatChem - 2025 - Hazarika - AgNi Alloy Nanoparticles as Dual‐Function Catalysts for Sustainable Nitrile Hydration and.pdf,11,11,P23_p11_069,"R.H. is thankful to UGC, New Delhi for the fellowship. K.D. is grateful to ANRF-India, (grant: ANRF/IRG/2024/001383/CS) for their generous support and financial assistance. The authors sincerely thank DST, for financial support under DST-PURSE project (SR/PURSE/2022/143 (C)) and DST-FIST project (SR/FST/CSI/2020/152). The authors are grateful to STIC Cochin for XRD analyses, SAIF, CSIR-NEIST for XPS and FESEM, IISER Mohali for TEM, and CSIC Dibrugarh University for NMR and LC-MS facilities. Conflict of Interests The authors declare no conflict of interest. Data Availability Statement The supporting data for this study can be found in the Supporting Information provided with this article."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_000,"Journal of Materials Chemistry A PAPER Cite this: J. Mater. Chem. A, 2023, 11, 10720 Received 12th December 2022 Accepted 8th March 2023 DOI: 10.1039/d2ta09660a rsc.li/materials-a 1 Introduction"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_001,"Electrochemical water splitting (WS) for hydrogen production driven by green and renewable energy provides a promising path to mitigate the aggravated energy crisis and realize largescale storage of intermittent energy in chemical bonds. 1,2 Developing effective electrocatalysts with high activity and stability for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is crucial to hydrogen production during the electrochemical water splitting process. In general, platinum (Pt)-based electrocatalysts serve as the benchmark for the design of new catalysts. 3-6 However, Ptbased electrocatalysts were not conducive to large-scale commercial applications due to its extremely high price and low earth abundance."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_002,"Within this context, intensive attention has been drawn to highly active but less expensive transition metals with higher abundance. According to previous studies, the bond strength of Ru-H (about 65 kcal mol -1 ) was close to that of Pt-H, 7,8 indicating that Ru-based materials presumably possess electrocatalytic performance for the HER comparable to that of Pt. In addition, the price of Ru is merely 1/3 that of Pt. 9 However, relatively inappropriate hydrogen chemical School of Physical Science and Technology, ShanghaiTech University, Shanghai 201210, China. E-mail: linbl@shanghaitech.edu.cn"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_003,"† Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available. See DOI: https://doi.org/10.1039/d2ta09660a Published on 10 March 2023 Licensed under CC-BY-NC 4.0 The RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface optimizes the d-band center of the Ni-Ru catalyst for highperformance alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction† Yitian Zhou, Yifan Liu, Hehua Tang and Bo-Lin Lin *"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_004,"Controlling the binding energy of hydrogen by introducing a secondary metal is an effective approach to design Ru-based electrocatalysts for improving the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) performance. Here, we construct a series of multi-active site NiRu bimetallic materials with improved HER catalytic performance through a facile microwave synthesis method. Upon varying the Ni/Ru feeding ratio, the prepared Ni1Ru1/C sample was found to have the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface and showed the best HER performance. Only a 13 mV overpotential was observed at 10 mA cm -2 in 1.0 M KOH solution, which is comparable to that of commercial Pt/C."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_005,"Remarkably, lower overpotentials were achieved for the Ni1Ru1/C sample under industrially relevant current density conditions (higher than 500 mA cm -2 ) than commercial Pt/C. The HER activity for the Ni1Ru1/C sample can also be sustained at 400 mA cm -2 for over 80 h with negligible degradation. The characterization and electrochemical experiment results illustrate that the excellent HER catalytic performance might be attributable to the enhancement of electrochemically active surface areas and the formation of the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_006,"DFT calculations further reveal a downshifted d-band center due to the presence of the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface, which appropriately weakens the too-strong adsorption of H* and thus improves the HER performance. adsorption/desorption on Ru still limits the HER performance. 8 Thus, further development and designing the structures of Rubased catalysts to moderate the binding energy of Ru-H and improving HER catalytic performance are of great importance."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_007,"In order to optimize the catalytic activity of Ru-based catalysts for the HER, numerous studies have been carried out on the modication of Ru-based materials and further regulating the electronic structure to optimize hydrogen adsorption and desorption processes during the HER. 10-17 Among them, introducing a secondary metal is an attractive approach to realize modications of both electronic structures and morphology of the catalyst. 8,18-20 The introduction of Ni to Ru-based catalysts has been proved to be an effective way to facilitate the catalytic performance of the HER, as the modication of Ni could weaken the too-strong Ru-H bond strength."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,1,1,P24_p1_008,"1,21-26 In addition, adding cheaper Ni into Ru-based catalysts can reduce the amount of precious metals to lower the cost. The excellent HER activity of NiRu catalysts has been mainly attributed to the formation of heteroatomic bonds, the changes of lattice parameters, and the modications of charge distributions aer Ni incorporation. 8,23 In previous work, the existence of lattice strain induced by NiRu alloying was demonstrated by TEM and XRD characterization. 27 DFT calculations further demonstrate that the Ni-Ru alloying effect is able to tune the adsorption and desorption of H during the process of the HER under alkaline conditions, hence improving the HER performance. 8,18,28"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_009,"Herein, we reasoned that excessive amounts of a secondary element doping may lead to its accumulation and phase separation, affording abundant heterogeneous interfaces. 29,30 The strain regions arising from heterogeneous interfaces can regulate the conductivity, mechanical strength, plasticity, charge transfer properties and electron density of the catalysts, which could further affect the behaviors of electrochemical redox processes. 30 Based on this hypothesis, we fabricated a series of NiRu-based electrocatalysts with multi-active sites through a convenient and rapid microwave method."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_010,"Among them, the Ni1Ru1/C sample featuring RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interfaces showed outstanding catalytic performance for the HER. Only 13 mV overpotential at a current density of 10 mA cm -2 was observed using 1 M KOH aqueous solution as the electrolyte. In addition, the catalytic activity for the HER was even better than that of commercial Pt/C (40 wt%) at high current density. Remarkably, Ni1Ru1/C displayed negligible catalytic activity degradation of the HER for more than 80 h at a current density of 400 mA cm -2 ."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_011,"The measured double layer capacitance of the Ni1Ru1/C sample was 23.9 mF cm -2 and the high electrochemically active surface areas made an important contribution to the improved HER activity. Moreover, density functional theory (DFT) calculations suggested that the Gibbs free energy of adsorbed H (H*) formation could be regulated by the introduction of Ni, thus favoring hydrogen formation. More importantly, the Gibbs free energy of H* formation could be further optimized upon the introduction of the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface, rationalizing the superior catalytic performance for the HER on the Ni1Ru1/C sample. 2 Experimental details 2.1 Materials synthesis"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_012,"Synthesis and characterization of NixRuy/C: by varying the Ni/ Ru metal molar ratio, Ni1Ru0/C, Ni2Ru1/C, Ni1Ru1/C Ni1Ru2/ C and Ni0Ru1/C were synthesized at Ni/Ru ratios of 1 : 0, 2 : 1, 1 : 1, 1 : 2 and 0 : 1, respectively (Table S2†). As shown in Fig. 1, NixRuy/C samples were prepared through a two-step procedure (x and y represent the fed molar ratios of Ni and Ru metals, respectively). First of all, ball-milling was used to mix RuCl3$3H2O, Ni(acac)2 and carbon black well for 3 hours to get the powder precursors."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_013,"Secondly, 300.0 mg of the mixture should be placed in a microwave reactor and then heated for 3 minutes with a power of 900 W in air, aer which multi-active site NixRuy/C was successfully obtained. The HER property measurement showed that the optimal ratio of Ni/Ru is 1 : 1 in NixRuy/C (denoted as Ni1Ru1/C). The ink was prepared by ultrasonically mixing 2.0 mg of NixRuy/C, 0.1 mL of ethylene glycol and 15 mL Naon, which was then dropped onto carbon paper and dried overnight to get the NixRuy/C electrode for electrochemical measurements. The details of the main reagents and instruments are listed in Table S3.† Fig. 1 Schematic illustration of the preparation process of the RuO2/ NiRu."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_014,2.2 Characterization Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed on a JSMIT500HR/LA. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) measurements were performed on a JEM-F200 and JEM-1400plus. X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns were acquired using a D8 Advance. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted on an ESCALAB 250Xi. The contact angle analysis was performed with a KRUSS DSA25. ICP-OES was conducted on an ICP-OES Icap7400. 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_015,"All HER tests were performed at room temperature with a conventional three-electrode system which employed a Hg/ HgO reference electrode and a platinum net counter electrode, and an electrolyte of 1.0 M KOH. The NixRuy/C electrode was used as the working electrode with the area immersed in the electrolyte being 1 cm × 1 cm. The loading of the Ni1Ru1/C catalyst was 1.86 mg cm -2 . The potential in this work was referenced to a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale according to the Nernst equation in 1.0 M KOH (Evs. RHE = Evs. Hg/HgO + 0.098 + 0.05916 pH)."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_016,"Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with auto iR correction and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) with a frequency range from 100 kHz to 0.1 Hz were performed using a Gamry electrochemical workstation. Electrochemically active surface areas (ECSAs) of the NixRuy/C electrodes were measured using a CHI 660E electrochemical workstation and were determined using double-layer capacitance (Cdl), which can be determined by calculating the slope in the linear relationship of current density with a scan rate increase. 2.4 DFT calculations"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_017,"DFT calculations were performed with the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VSAP), with the projector augmented wave (PAW) potentials to describe the electron-ion interactions. The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with the PerdewBurke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional was used for the exchangecorrelation potentials. The cutoff energy for the plane-wave basis set was 450 eV. The long-range dispersion interactions between the adsorbates and catalyst surface were illustrated by the D3 correction method. 31 The Brillouin zone was sampled with 3 × 3 × 1 gamma-centered k-mesh for metal oxide slabs and 2 × 2 × 1 k-mesh for the alloy/oxide interface."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,2,2,P24_p2_018,Geometries of all the structures were optimized with the conjugate gradient algorithm and the convergence tolerances for residual energy and force were 10 -4 eV and 0.02 eV Å -1 .
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_019,"RuO2(110) and NiO(100) were modeled as a periodic fourlayer p(3 × 3) model with the 2 lower layers xed and 2 upper layers relaxed. NiRu(101) was modeled as the 2 × 3 supercell of c(O2 × O2) - 45. To simulate the RuO2/NiRu interface, the NiRu cluster containing 4 Ni and 4 Ru atoms was placed on RuO2(110). The simulation of the Ru/NiRu interface was the same as that of the RuO2/NiRu interface, except for replacing RuO2(110) with Ru(101). A vacuum space of 15 Å perpendicular to the surface was applied to separate the interaction between the neighboring slabs. The adsorption free energies of hydrogen were dened as:"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_020,"where EH*, E* and EH 2 are the total energies of the H adsorbed system, a clean substrate and molecular H2. The Gibbs free energy of the adsorbed state was computed as: where DZPE and DS represent the zero-point energy and entropy, respectively; temperature T was set to 298.15 K. DZPE and DS were derived from the calculated vibrational frequencies with the harmonic approximation. 32 The Ru 3d band center was dened as the weighted average energy of the projected density of 3d states of Ru relative to the Fermi level. 3 Results and discussion 3.1 Characterization of NixRuy/C"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_021,"As depicted in Fig. 1, the NixRuy/C samples were prepared through a facile microwave method; the carbon black substrate was mixed with Ni and Ru precursors with grinding treatment, followed by microwave irradiation, and then the targeted carbon supported NiRu composite material was obtained. The scanning electron microscope (SEM) image (Fig. 2a) indicated that the Ni1Ru1/C sample was composed of small nanoparticles anchored on the carbon substrate. Further transmission electron microscopy (TEM) characterization and size distribution analysis (Fig. 2b and c) suggested that the particle size mainly ranged from 1.8 to 20 nm and the average particle size was 6.98 nm."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_022,"The smaller nanoparticles should be more favorable for the exposure of active sites. 5,33,34 As depicted in Fig. 2e, a heterogeneous interface (denoted as RuO2/NiRu) was observed on the Ni1Ru1/C sample. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) showed lattice fringe distances of 0.228 and 0.206 nm, ascribed to the (200) facets of RuO2 and the (0002) facets of the NiRu alloy, respectively. Moreover, NiO, RuO2, and NiRu alloy species were observed on the Ni1Ru1/C sample from HRTEM (Fig. S3 and S4†), and the selected-area electron diffraction (SAED, Fig. S5†) pattern further conrmed the existence of the as-mentioned species. Fig."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_023,"2g-j display scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) images and the corresponding energy-dispersive spectrometry (EDS) mappings; the results suggested that Ni and Ru were homogeneously distributed on the nanoparticles supported by carbon black. These data conrmed that the multi-active site catalyst of Ni1Ru1/C with the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface was successfully fabricated. Fig. 2 (a) SEM images of Ni1Ru1/C. (b-e) TEM images of Ni1Ru1/C. (d and f) The enlarged images of the yellow part (d) and green part (f) in (e), respectively. (g-j) STEM/EDS image with elemental mapping images for C, Ni and Ru. 3.2 HER performance of NixRuy/C"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_024,"The effect of the metal ratio of Ni/Ru on the electrochemical performance of multi-active site NixRuy/C catalysts was studied. The electrochemical performance was evaluated in a N2-saturated 1 M KOH aqueous solution with a classical three-electrode system. From the linear sweep voltammetry polarization curves (LSV, Fig. 3a), we found that the HER catalytic performance was signicantly improved aer the introduction of Ni, indicating the importance of Ni in improving the catalytic performance of the HER. The catalytic performance could be further optimized through regulating the ratio of Ni/Ru, and the best HER catalytic performance was achieved when the Ni/Ru ratio was 1 : 1."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_025,"It was worth noting that the overpotential on the Ni1Ru1/C sample was only 13 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm -2 , which was close to that of commercial Pt/C (11 mV) and smaller than those on Ni2Ru1/C (16 mV) and Ni1Ru2/C (15 mV), respectively (Fig. 3c). Thus, the amounts of Ni in NixRuy/C composite catalysts had a great inuence on the catalytic performance of alkaline HER, and the Ni1Ru1/C sample possessed a more optimized Ni/Ru molar ratio to achieve a higher apparent current density than samples with other ratios with the same overpotential."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,3,3,P24_p3_026,"Surprisingly, the Ni1Ru1/C sample even exhibited better HER activity than the Pt/C sample at large current densities (greater than 500 mA cm -2 ). By converting the apparent current density into mass current density, the mass activity could be compared more intuitively (Fig. S14 and Table S6†). At an overpotential of 10 mV, the mass current density of Ni1Ru1 was about 22.7, 18.7 and 14.2 times higher than those of Ni2Ru1/C, Ni1Ru2/C and Pt/C, respectively, which indicated higher intrinsic activity of Ni1Ru1/C."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_027,"Fig. 3 (a) Polarization curves (with iR correction) for various electrodes recorded in 1.0 M KOH with a scan rate of 5 mV s -1 . (b) Tafel plots obtained from the corresponding polarization curves in (a). (c) Comparison of the required overpotentials at 10, 100 and 500 mA cm -2 for various electrodes. (d) Comparison of NixRuy/C and other NiRu catalysts reported in previous literature based on the potential at 10 mA cm -2 and Tafel slope."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_028,"The Tafel slope plots were extracted from the linear portions of the LSV curves in order to study the reaction kinetics of the HER process. As depicted in Fig. 3b, the Tafel slope of the Ni1Ru1/C sample was 33 mV dec -1 , which was almost the same as the 30 mV dec -1 of the Pt/C sample and smaller than 44 mV dec -1 of the Ni1Ru2/C sample and 56 mV dec -1 of the Ni2Ru1/C sample. The Tafel slope suggested that the HER on the Ni1Ru1/ C sample might be processed via the Volmer-Tafel mechanism."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_029,"28,35,36 The improvement of the kinetic process might originate from the synergic catalytic effect of Ni and Ru, 28 and the optimized molar ratio of Ni and Ru on the Ni1Ru1/C sample might further promote such a reaction process compared to Ni2Ru1/C or Ni1Ru2/C. From the perspective of overpotential at a current density of 10 mA cm -2 and the Tafel slope, our Ni1Ru1/C sample showed better catalytic performance than relevant NiRu-based HER catalysts reported in the literature previously (Fig. 3d and Table S1†)."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_030,"To further understand the origin of the excellent catalytic performance of the NixRuy/C composite samples, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) analyses of several multi-active site NixRuy/C samples were carried out. EIS was used to evaluate the charge transfer resistance of the electrodes during the electrocatalytic reaction process. The corresponding equivalent circuit is shown in the inset, which was composed of Rs (the ohmic resistance of the electrolyte), Rct (charge transfer resistance) and Y0 (constant phase elements). 18 The diameter of the half cycle shown in Fig. 4a of Ni1Ru1/C (0.651 ohm) was obviously smaller Time (h)"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_031,Fig. 4 (a) Fitting Nyquist plots for various electrodes measured at an overpotential of -74 mV. (b) CV curve tested at different scan rates (varying from 10 mV s -1 to 70 mV s -1 ) of Ni1Ru1/C. (c) Plots of the current density difference (Dj) against the scan rate for calculation of double-layer capacitance (Cdl). (d) Chronopotentiometric (V-t) curve of Ni1Ru1/C with a constant current density of -400 mA cm -2 (a certain amount of pure water was added at the point indicated by the arrow to compensate for the consumption of water due to electrolysis after 30 hours in experiments).
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_032,"than that of Ni0Ru1/C (1.938 ohm), Ni1Ru2/C (0.933 ohm), and Ni2Ru1/C (1.372 ohm), indicating that the Ni1Ru1/C sample should have a lower contact resistance and a faster electron transfer process during the HER than the control samples. 37 The Rct of catalysts was signicantly reduced due to the introduction of Ni, and thus the corresponding kinetic process of the HER was greatly promoted. 23 Fig. 4b and S7† present the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of NixRuy/C electrodes at different scan rates. As shown in Fig."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_033,"4c, the calculated double-layer capacitance (Cdl) of Ni1Ru1/C was 23.9 mF cm -2 , which was larger than 20.2 mF cm -2 of Ni2Ru1/C, 13.2 mF cm -2 of Ni0Ru1/C, 11.8 mF cm -2 of Ni1Ru2/C, and 6.5 mF cm -2 of Ni1Ru0/C, suggesting an exposure of more abundant active sites on Ni1Ru1/C than the control samples. When the Ni/Ru ratio is 1, the number of active sites might increase, which leads to the largest ECSA of the Ni1Ru1/C sample. And the higher ECSA of the Ni1Ru1/C was one of the important causes of enhanced HER activity."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_034,These results seem to support our initial hypothesis that the introduction of RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interfaces might serve as an effective strategy to improve the catalytic activity.
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,4,P24_p4_035,"In order to evaluate the stability and durability of the Ni1Ru1/C catalyst and its application potential in industrial water electrolysis, the Ni1Ru1/C electrode was tested in 1 M KOH electrolyte at a current density of 400 mA cm -2 for over 80 hours (Fig. 4d). Aer 80 hours of galvanostatic testing, the increase in overpotential appeared negligible, indicating that the Ni1Ru1/C electrode was able to maintain satisfactory durability in the alkaline HER catalysis process. Though there was a slightly reduced signal for the decreased amount of RuO2(211), no obvious Ru 0 signal was presented in the XRD spectrum shown in Fig."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,4,5,P24_p4_036,"S9.† The ratio of Ru 4+ to Ru 0 on the post-electrode surface did not change signicantly in XPS patterns (Fig. S13 and Table S5†). The XRD and XPS results indicated that most RuO2 had not been reduced. And the SEM images and contact angle results of the electrode surface before and aer the HER measurement showed that the morphology of the electrode surface remained basically unchanged (Fig. S8 and S11†). Therefore, the morphology of the electrode surface remained basically unchanged during the 80 h HER test, which suggested its application potential in industrial WS. 3.3 The study of local electron modulation for the RuO2/ NiRu heterogeneous interface"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,5,5,P24_p5_037,"In order to further study the localized electron modulation in the multi-active sites of Ni1Ru1/C and rationalize the improved alkaline HER activity, we carried out XRD and XPS characterization tests on the synthesized catalysts. As shown in the XRD pattern (Fig. 5a), Ni1Ru1/C was mainly composed of a NiRu alloy, RuO2 and NiO, which was consistent with XPS characterization. As shown in Fig. 5c, 280.6 and 281.3 eV in Ni0Ru1/C correspond to the Ru 0 of Ru metal and Ru 4+ of RuO2, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5d, Ni1Ru0/C shows two signals at 854.7 and 856.5 eV corresponding to Ni 0 of Ni metal and Ni 2+ of NiO, respectively."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,5,5,P24_p5_038,"When the corresponding peaks were compared with those of Ni1Ru1/C, it could be seen that when Ni was introduced to form the NiRu alloy, the binding energy of Ru 0 shied negatively while positive shis occurred on Ni 0 peaks. This indicated that electrons were transferred from Ni to Ru in NiRu alloys due to the smaller electronegativity of Ni relative to that of Ru, leading to the interatomic charge polarization in the NiRu alloys. 28 In addition, the results of XPS peak analysis indicated that feeding different metal ratios of Ni to Ru can change the ratio of Ru 0 to Ru 4+ on the surface of the synthesized material. Thus, the introduction of Ni can inuence the formation of the NiRu alloy."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,5,5,P24_p5_039,"The difference in the positional shi of the binding energy of Ni 0 in Ni2Ru1/C, Ni1Ru2/C and Ni1Ru1/C suggested a difference in the degree of electronic coupling between Ni and Ru. 38 Among them, the deviation degree of Ni 0 in Ni1Ru1/C was the largest, suggesting that there was a stronger local electronic modulation on the surface of the catalyst. Furthermore, compared with Ni2Ru1/C and Ni1Ru2/C, the peak of Ru 0 in Ni1Ru1/C had a positive shi of 0.1 eV and a negative shi of 0.1 eV also occurred to the Ru 4+ peak, which might be caused by electron transfer from NiRu to RuO2 at the interface of RuO2/NiRu."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,5,5,P24_p5_040,"Such an electron effect may also be one of the reasons for the aforementioned deviation enhancement of the Ni 0 peak. The results showed that there might be a stronger local electron modulation at the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface, thus speeding up the electron transfer and charge transfer in the catalytic reaction for alkaline HER. 3.4 The DFT calculation study for the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,5,5,P24_p5_041,"It was accepted that the alkaline HER followed the VolmerHeyrovsky or Volmer-Tafel mechanism. 39,40 The H-O-H bond of water molecules rst needed to break to form H* and OH - , and the Volmer step (eqn (1.1)) involved the adsorption and dissociation of water molecules on the catalyst surface. 41 Next, a hydrogen generation step occurred on the surface with the Heyrovsky step (eqn (1.2)) or Tafel step (eqn (1.3)). 36 Fig. 5 (a) XRD pattern of Ni1Ru1/C. (b) XPS survey of NixRuy/C. (c) C 1s + Ru 3d. (d) Ni 2p."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_042,"Though the Tafel slope could not be used to determine the specic mechanism of the HER, the value of the Tafel slope has been generally used to analyze the rate-determination steps in the reaction process according to the Bulter-Volmer equation. 28,35,36 Combined with research studies on Ru-based catalysts 1,9,42 and Tafel slope, 43,44 the HER on the Ni1Ru1/C might be processed via the Volmer-Tafel mechanism."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_043,"To probe the potential effect of the RuO2/NiRu interface, the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (DGH*), a good descriptor that may partially rationalize HER catalytic activity, was calculated using DFT. It indicated optimal hydrogen adsorption and release when DGH* was close to zero. 45,46 The RuO2/NiRu interface model was constructed for DFT calculations. Given the partial reduction of RuO2 of RuO2/NiRu aer the HER test, a Ru/NiRu model was also simulated. Fig. 6a shows the optimized NiO, RuO2, NiRu alloys, RuO2/NiRu interface and Ru/NiRu interface for the hydrogen atom adsorption model, respectively."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_044,"Our DFT calculation results showed that DGH* on the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface (-0.240 eV) was much closer to zero than those of NiO (1.047 eV), RuO2 (-0.772 eV) and NiRu alloy (-0.422 eV), indicating that the formation of the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface was able to modulate the hydrogen adsorption strength thereby improving the HER performance of Ni1Ru1/C (Fig. 6b). And the DGH* of RuO2/NiRu interface was also closer to zero than DGH* of the Ru/NiRu interface, suggesting that the former has higher HER activity than the latter."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_045,"Since no signicant increase in overpotential was observed in the 80 h CP test of Ni1Ru1/C, it should be reasonable to propose that RuO2/NiRu should be the most active component of Ni1Ru1/C. The analysis of PDOS Fig. 6 (a) Schematic illustrationof the adsorptionmodel. (b) Gibbs free energy diagram for hydrogen evolution at equilibrium (U = 0 V) in NiO, RuO2, NiRu, and RuO2/NiRu interface. (c) PDOS and d-band centers of RuO2, NiRu, and RuO2/NiRu interface. (d) Gibbs free energy changes of hydrogen adsorption as a function of d-band centers of RuO2, NiRu, and RuO2/NiRu interface."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_046,"illustrated that the RuO2/NiRu interface presented a downshied d-band center and a trend away from the Fermi level, which is consistent with the weakening of the H adsorption energy (Fig. 6c). As the Sabatier principle described, an active catalyst binds reaction intermediate(s) neither too strongly nor too weakly. 45,47 Thus, the computational results also support our original speculation that the formation of the RuO2/NiRu heterogeneous interface can weaken the too-strong adsorption of H*, 47 leading to the corresponding DGH* being close to a desirable value (Fig. 6d)."
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_047,"Additionally, RuO2 might play the important role of strengthening *OH adsorption, which was benecial for the promotion of the Volmer step. 48 By combining the weakened *H adsorption and enhanced *OH adsorption, 48,49 a reasonable hypothesis can be made that the interfacial synergy between NiRu and RuO2 might boost the HER activities. 4 Conclusions"
P24,d2ta09660a,d2ta09660a.pdf,6,6,P24_p6_048,"In summary, we prepared a series of multi-active site NiRu bimetallic materials through a facile microwave synthesis method. The introduction of Ni to form NiRu bimetallic electrocatalysts has led to signicantly improved HER catalytic performance; furthermore, the NiRu/RuO2 heterogeneous interface was successfully constructed on the Ni1Ru1/C sample to further improve the HER catalytic performance. The optimized Ni1Ru1/C sample exhibited an overpotential of only 13 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm -2 under alkaline conditions, outperforming state-of-the-art results reported in the previous literature; moreover, excellent stability for more than 80 h at 400 mA cm -2 was achieved."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_000,"Journal of Materials Chemistry A PAPER Cite this: J. Mater. Chem. A, 2025, 13, 24062 Received 7th March 2025 Accepted 21st June 2025 DOI: 10.1039/d5ta01877c rsc.li/materials-a 1 Introduction To achieve the goal of zero carbon emissions, reducing the use of fossil fuels is imperative. Hydrogen (H2) is considered one of the most suitable alternatives due to its high energy density, eco-friendliness, and abundancy. 1 In this context, electrochemical water splitting, powered by intermittent electricity from renewable energy sources, is a highly promising strategy for the efficient and clean production of ultra-pure H2. 2 Published on 23 June 2025 Licence and permissions"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_001,"Boosting the HER/OER bifunctional electrocatalytic activity of Ni-Cu alloy via watercontaining deep eutectic solvent system† Yi-Hung Liu, * a Fen-Yan Zeng, a Yen-Shen Kuo, a Yu Chen a and Cheng-Liang Hsu * b"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_002,"Green hydrogen produced from water electrolysis is regarded as a promising alternative to fossil fuel. To achieve this, non-noble metal-based electrocatalysts with high hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performances are crucial. Addressing this need, a simple one-step electrodeposition method using a water-containing choline chloride/ethylene glycol deep eutectic solvent (ChCl/EG DES) medium is proposed to create Ni-Cu alloy/carbon fiber felt (CF) electrodes for water splitting. By controlling the water content within the DES, an appropriate ionic conductivity and viscosity can be achieved without disrupting the DES molecular structures."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_003,"Importantly, the DES with 3 wt% water content provides an environment where formation of a moderate Ni(OH)2 colloid membrane can be achieved, resulting in a uniform deposition structure and an appropriate Ni/Cu ratio of Ni-Cu alloy over the CF substrate. Thanks to these specific features, the optimized Ni-Cu/CF electrode (NC(3)) demonstrates exceptional HER and OER performances with low overpotentials of 53 mV at 10 mA cm -2 and 390 mV at 50 mA cm -2 , respectively. The favorable bifunctional activity makes the NC(3) electrode-based electrolysis cell highly stable against water splitting, with a reduced cell voltage of 1.52 V at 10 mA cm -2 and and 1.86 V at 50 mA cm -2 ."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_004,"Specifically for the HER, the composite electrode exhibits a high faradaic efficiency of over 99%, resulting in H2 gas with a purity of approximately 99%. Furthermore, the improved electrode kinetics and stability of the NC(3) electrode are supported by its smaller Tafel slope, lower charge transfer impedance, increased electrochemical active surface area, and enhanced anti-corrosion capability. The promoted electronic effect between the Ni and Cu species of the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst may play a key role in this regard."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_005,"Electrochemical water splitting involves two half-reactions: anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and cathodic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). The efficiency of these reactions directly impacts the overall performance of water splitting. Both HER and OER require appropriate catalysts to enhance H2 production and minimize energy consumption. 3,4 At present, the most efficient catalysts for HER are based on precious metals such as Pt and Pd. However, the high cost and scarcity of these materials limit their application, particularly in large-scale water splitting systems."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_006,"Similarly, noble metal-based catalysts like RuO2 or IrO2, despite their excellent OER activity, are not preferred due to the same cost and scarcity issues. Fortunately, a new group of catalytic materials, known as transition metals, has emerged, offering several advantages over noble metal-based catalysts in terms of lower cost and greater abundance. 5 Among them, alloy-type catalysts have attracted researchers' attention due to the synergistic effects between transition metals, which enhance catalytic activity. The hydrothermal method, combined with high-temperature thermal treatment, has been employed to prepare alloy-type catalysts."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_007,"6,7 Other methods, such as bath sonication followed by carbonization, 8 and electrospinning combined with thermal treatment, 9 are also effective. However, these synthesis approaches are oen complex, involving multiple steps and high-temperature processes, which are time-consuming and energy-intensive. To address this, more straightforward catalyst synthesis methods with lower energy requirements have been proposed. For instances, an Fe-Mo alloy prepared via microwave-assisted electrodeposition has been reported to demonstrate good HER activity."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,1,1,P25_p1_008,"10 According to the ndings of Liu et al., 11 Fe-Co lm electrocatalysts made by electrodeposition exhibit bifunctional catalytic properties for water splitting, enhancing both HER and OER activities. Nevertheless, there is still room for improvement in bifunctional electrocatalysts to further enhance water splitting performance by reducing the overpotential for both HER and OER. In addition, while electrocatalysts are typically prepared through electrodeposition in water-based solutions, this process can lead to non-uniform deposition due to simultaneous water electrolysis, resulting in poor catalytic activity."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_009,"Deep eutectic solvents (DESs) are a new type of green solvent that offer several advantages, including nontoxicity, low cost, biodegradability, nonvolatility, recyclability, and a wide electrochemical window. These properties have led to their recognition as green solvents, with applications in various elds such as metal recovery, surface modication, and functional material synthesis. 12 Generally, DES is a mixture composed of hydrogen bond donors and acceptors, which has a lower melting temperature than its individual components. Ethylene glycol (EG) and urea are typical hydrogen bond donors, while halide salts are commonly used as hydrogen bond acceptors."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_010,"In recent years, DES has gained considerable attention as a reaction medium for the synthesis of functional nanomaterials through electrodeposition. 12 Water-based media typically have a narrow electrochemical stability window (ESW) for the electrodeposition process, where water electrolysis easily occurs, producing H2 gas at the cathode surface and compromising material deposition. Conversely, DES-based electrodeposition systems can avoid these issues and can highly dissolve metal salts, which is benecial for the electrodeposition of metals such as Zn and Cu."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_011,"13 Additionally, DES holds particular promise for the electrodeposition of metals with low reduction potential, such as Ga, because of its wide ESW. Ni-based catalysts, including single metals, 14 alloys, 15 oxides, or their composites 16 have also been synthesized via DES-based electrodeposition systems and have proven effective for H2 evolution. The addition of a certain amount of water in DES-based electrodeposition systems can further enhance the surface properties of deposited materials."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_012,"For instance, a choline chloride (ChCl)/urea DES system with a 6 wt% water content was found to promote the formation of ne and uniform Ni nanoparticles within a deposition layer, a signicant departure from the dendrite Ni clusters formed by using a water-free DES system. 17 In the water-containing DES system, a Ni(OH)2 passivation layer was purported to form over the substrate surface, which can prevent the overgrowth of Ni deposits during electrodeposition. 18 Our previous study also conrmed the formation of a Ni(OH)2/Ni hierarchical deposit over the substrate surface aer electrodeposition using a watercontaining DES system."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_013,"16 However, knowledge about the availability and feasibility of water-containing systems for synthesizing other types of electrocatalysts, such as alloys, is still limited. Additionally, the strong H absorption energy of single elements such as Ni may lead to sluggish kinetics for H2 evolution. Forming alloys with other transition metals to compensate for the strong H absorption energy of catalysts is considered a promising strategy to enhance HER activity. 19 Therefore, the synthesis of alloy electrocatalyst via watercontaining DES electrodeposition is worth investigating and remains to be well-addressed in terms of material properties, catalytic activity, and formation mechanism."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_014,"In this study, a ChCl/EG DES system with a water content less than 5 wt% was established to synthesize Ni-Cu alloy catalysts on a carbon ber felt (CF) substrate via electrodeposition. The inuence of various water contents on DES and synthesized material properties was thoroughly investigated. By appropriately controlling the water content, Ni-Cu alloy deposits with a ne and uniform structure can be obtained, along with a relatively high Ni/Cu ratio. Based on these specic features, the optimal Ni-Cu/CF electrode exhibits prominent bifunctional activity toward HER and OER with reduced overpotentials, as well as anti-corrosion characteristics."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_015,"Additionally, the electrolytic cell composed of Ni-Cu/CF electrodes demonstrates a low operating voltage (1.52 V at 10 mA cm -2 ), high stability, and high current efficiency, which is favorable for water splitting. The formation mechanism of NiCu electrocatalyst in the DES electrodesposition system with various water contents was also elucidated. 2 Experimental procedures 2.1 Pretreatment of CF substrate"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_016,"First, a piece of CF was cut into a size of 40 × 15 × 1.5 mm. Next, the trimmed CF substrate was transferred to a high-temperature furnace with nitrogen gas and thermally treated at 800 °C for 2 h. Aerward, the heat-treated substrate was removed and immersed into an acidic solution (H2SO4 + HNO3) at 90 °C for 1.5 h, followed by rinsing with deionized (DI) water until the pH of the rinsed solution reached a neutral level. The pretreatment of the CF was completed by drying the CF substrate in an oven at 80 °C overnight. 2.2 Fabrication of Ni-Cu/CF composite electrodes"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_017,"Reagent-grade chemicals, including ChCl (HOC2H4N(CH3)Cl, 99%), EG ((CH2OH)2, 99%), nickel chloride hexahydrate, (NiCl2$6H2O, 98%) and copper chloride dihydrate (CuCl2$2H2O, 98%), were used to prepare the DES-based reaction solution without further purication. ChCl and EG were mixed in a molar ratio of 1 : 2 in a ask and stirred at 80 °C for 12 h until the mixture became a homogeneous, transparent DES. The DES was then supplemented with 0.6 M NiCl2 and 0.05 M CuCl2, followed by stirring until completely dissolved."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,2,P25_p2_018,"To prepare the water-containing DES, various amounts of distilled water (1, 3, and 5 wt%) were added to the ChCl/EG DES, denoted as DES(x) solutions where x = 0, 1, 3, and 5 represent added water content of 0, 1, 3, and 5 wt%, respectively. The pretreated CF (working electrode) was partially immersed in the water-decient or water-containing solutions in an electrolytic cell. A carbon rod and an Ag/AgCl (saturated KCl) electrode served as the counter and reference electrodes, respectively, to establish a threeelectrode system for electrodeposition."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,2,3,P25_p2_019,"The electrodeposition was conducted using an electrochemical workstation (SP-150, Biologic, France) in potentiostatic mode at -2 V and 80 °C for 5 min. Aerward, the prepared electrode was removed from the reaction solution, washed with DI water for 1 h to eliminate residual solution, and dried at 80 °C overnight. The catalyst electrodes fabricated using DES(0), DES(1), DES(3), and DES(5) solutions were denoted as NC(0), NC(1), NC(3), and NC(5). The catalyst loading of NC(0), NC(1), NC(3), and NC(5) electrodes were 1.99, 1.97, 1.97, and 2.33 mg cm -2 , respectively. 2.3 Material characterizations"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_020,"To examine the molecular structures of water-decient and water-containing ChCl/EG DES, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR, Spectrum One, PerkinElmer, USA) was employed. The ionic conductivities and viscosities of different DES solutions were measured using a conductivity meter (CM41X, DKK-TOA Corporation, Japan) and a rheometer (MCR302, Anton Paar, Austria), respectively. The static contact angles between DES droplets and various untreated or treated CFs were measured using a contact angle goniometer (Dataphysics DSA 10-MK2, KR¨USS, Germany) to assess their interfacial compatibility."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_021,"X-ray diffraction (XRD) was utilized to determine the crystal structures of Ni-Cu based electrocatalysts, carried out using an X-ray diffractometer (D8 Advance, Bruker, USA) using Cu Ka radiation (l = 1.54056 Å). The scan was operated at 2.6° min -1 , ranging from 10° to 90°. The metal content of the electrocatalysts was determined using inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS; Elan DRC II, PerkinElmer, USA). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Sigma Probe, Thermo VG-Scientic, UK) with a monochromatic Al Ka (1486.6 eV) X-ray source was performed to identify the surface elemental composition and valence states of the electrocatalysts."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_022,"Additionally, the morphologies and element distribution of the prepared electrodes were analyzed by eldemission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, S-4800, Hitachi, Japan) equipped with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). 2.4 Electrochemical analysis and water splitting"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_023,"A three-electrode reaction system was used to conduct all the electrochemical measurements. In this system, the as-prepared Ni-Cu/CF electrode, a carbon rod, and an Ag/AgCl (saturated KCl) electrode served as the working, counter, and reference electrodes, respectively. To avoid interference between the anodic and cathodic compartments, an ionic exchange membrane (Naon N117) was placed between the two compartments. The potentials obtained from the system for all the electrochemical experiments were converted to the reversible hydrogen electrode (ERHE) using the equation:"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_024,"Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was employed to study the catalytic activities of various Ni-Cu based electrocatalysts for HER and OER in a 1.0 M KOH solution. The LSV was conducted at a scan rate of 5 mV s -1 , within a potential range from 1.3 to -1 V (vs. RHE) for HER and from -0.7 to 3 V (vs. RHE) for OER. Each test run included 85% IR-drop compensation. By analyzing the polarization curves obtained from LSV measurements, Tafel plots can be established using the Tafel equation: where h is the overpotential, j is the current density, b is the Tafel slope, and a is the Tafel constant."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_025,"To obtain internal impedance information during HER and OER, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted in the frequency range of 150 kHz to 100 mHz with an amplitude of 20 mV. The applied potential for HER was -0.1 V vs. ERHE, while for OER it was 1.6 V vs. ERHE. To determine the electrochemical active surface areas (ECSAs) of the prepared electrodes, their double-layer capacitances (Cdl) were estimated from cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements in a nonfaradaic potential region. The scan rate ranged from 20 to 200 mV s -1 , with increments of 20 mV s -1 ."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,3,P25_p3_026,"The corrosion characteristics of various prepared electrocatalysts were evaluated using potentiodynamic polarization tests, specically Tafel curve analysis. The tests were performed in a 1.0 M KOH solution at a scan rate of 1 mV s -1 over a potential range from -1 to 2 V. The stability of the typical water splitting electrolytic system, comprising the optimal Ni-Cu/CF electrode, was examined by chronopotentiometry within a three-electrode setup at current densities of 10 and 50 mA cm -2 for 12 h without IR-drop compensation. H2 gas produced during electrolysis was collected using a drainage method."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,3,4,P25_p3_027,"The gas was further analyzed to calculate the current efficiency of the electrolytic system and examine the purity of H2 using a gas chromatograph (GC-2014AT, SHIMADZU, Japan). The analysis was performed at 100 °C with Ar gas as the carrier. A capillary column with an internal diameter of 0.53 mm, lm thickness of 1.50 mm, and length of 15 m (DB115M-STD) was used for the analysis. Fig. 1 (a) FTIR spectra of ChCl, EG, and ChCl/EGDES. (b) FTIR spectra of water-deficient (DES(0)) and water-containing ChCl/EG DESs (DES1), DES(3), and DES(5)). (c) Ionic conductivity and (d) viscosity change over temperature for water-deficient and water-containing DESs. 3 Results and discussion"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_028,"3.1 Properties of water-containing ChCl/EG DES FTIR analysis conrmed the formation and fundamental properties of ChCl/EG-based DES. Fig. 1a depicts the FTIR spectra of the water-decient DES, pure ChCl, and pure EG. The spectrum of the ChCl/EG DES exhibits characteristic peaks from both ChCl and EG. The broad peak at 3302 cm -1 corresponds to the stretching vibration of the O-H group, akin to the 3296 cm -1 peak observed in the EG spectrum. A slight shi of the DES peak toward a higher wavenumber signies that the presence of ChCl may weaken the hydrogen bonding among EG molecules."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_029,"20 Additionally, major characteristic peaks at 953, 1480, and 3027 cm -1 in the DES spectra correspond to the stretching vibration of C-N + , the bending of alkyl groups (CH2), and the stretching vibration of N-H, respectively, which are also evident in the ChCl spectra. Furthermore, the DES spectra include peaks at 2936 cm -1 and 2872 cm -1 , attributed to C-H stretching vibration, as well as peaks at 1084, 1036, and 882 cm -1 , indicating C-O stretching, asymmetric stretching of C-C-O, and symmetric stretching of C-C-O, respectively."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_030,"21 Based on these observations, it is evident that the formation of the ChCl/EG DES results from the nondestructive integration of individual ChCl and EG components. Fig. 1b displays the FTIR spectra of ChCl/EG-based DES with varying water content. The spectra shapes are similar for all four samples, denoting that water content below 5 wt% has negligible inuence on the molecular structures of the DES. However, there is a slight shi toward higher wavenumbers in the peaks around 30003500 cm -1 with increasing water content."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_031,"This shi may be attributed to interactions between water molecules and ChCl/ EG, which weaken the strong hydrogen bonds within the DES and facilitate a more uniform distribution of water molecules within the framework. 22 Conductivity and viscosity of both water-decient and water-containing DESs at various temperatures were measured as crucial factors in electrodeposition, shown in Fig. 1c and d. The conductivity increases with rising temperature across all DES systems (Fig. 1c). The outcome is expected because higher temperature increases the average kinetic energy of ions, thereby enhancing conductivity."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_032,"Importantly, the conductivity of DES signicantly increases with added water, and this increase generally correlates with the quantity of water added. This enhanced conductivity is primarily due to improved ionic dissociation of ChCl facilitated by water molecules. Conversely, the trend in viscosity differs from that of conductivity concerning temperature and water content changes (Fig. 1d). As expected, viscosity deceases with higher temperature due to the liquid nature of DES. Comparatively, water has lower viscosity than DES, directly reducing the viscosity of water-containing DES, especially notable in the DES(5) sample."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,4,P25_p4_033,"These ndings evidently demonstrate that added water has a signicant impact on the conductivity and viscosity of DES, which in turn may affect the electrodeposition process in the water-containing DES systems. Fig. 3 (a) XRD patterns of the composite electrodes fabricated using DES-based solutions with varying water content. SEM images of the composite electrodes fabricated using DES-based solutions with (b) 0 wt%, (c) 1 wt%, (d) 3 wt%, and (e) 5 wt% water content. Magnified SEM images of the composite electrodes fabricated using DES-based solutions with (f) 0 wt%, (g) 1 wt%, (h) 3 wt%, and (i) 5 wt% water content."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,4,5,P25_p4_034,"Fig. 2 Contact angle analysis between DES droplet and (a) pristine CF, (b) thermally treated CF, and (c) CF with heat and acid treatments. To ensure the compatibility between the CF substrate and DES-based reaction medium, contact angle analysis was carried out. As shown in Fig. 2a, a DES droplet partially embeds into the pristine CF substrate upon contact, forming a contact angle of approximately 138°. This indicates that the pristine CF has a hydrophobic surface, which is incompatible with the hydrophilic nature of DES. Likewise, the thermally treated CF exhibits hydrophobicity toward DES, with a contact angle of approximately 135° (Fig. 2b)."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_035,"However, aer further acid treatment, the CF substrate becomes hydrophilic, as evidenced by the complete wetting of the surface by the DES droplet within less than a second of contact (Fig. 2c). This enhanced compatibility between the CF substrate and DES is considered benecial for the electrodeposition of target catalysts in the DES-based systems. 3.2 Material characterization of Ni-Cu electrocatalyst"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_036,"Fig. 3a shows the XRD patterns for the electrocatalysts synthesized via both water-decient and water-containing DES systems. Three characteristic peaks at approximately 44°, 51°, and 76° are observed in all samples, corresponding to the (111), (200), and (220) crystal planes of the Ni-Cu alloy (JCPDS 471406), 23 conrming the successful synthesis of the Ni-Cu alloy in all DES systems. Notably, the primary peak at 44° shis toward higher angles as the water content increases from 0 wt% to 3 wt%, indicating an increase in the Ni composition relative to Cu in the Ni-Cu alloy."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_037,"This is further supported by ICP analysis, which shows that NC(3) has higher Ni/Cu ratio, followed by NC(1) and NC(0) (Table S1†). In addition, when the water content reaches a certain level, specically in NC(5), the primary peak splits into two peaks corresponding to Ni and Cu metals, along with the appearance of peaks associated with Ni(OH)2 (JCPDS 38-0715). 24 That is, excessive addition of water in DES promotes the formation of Ni(OH)2 but hinders the formation of the Ni-Cu alloy. Fig. 3b-i show the morphologies of Ni-Cu/CF electrodes fabricated using water-decient and water-containing DES systems."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_038,"In the water-decient sample (NC(0)), the deposited particles are loosely packed and unevenly spread over the CF substrate, tending to aggregate into isolated mountain range-like structure, as shown in the magnied SEM image in Fig. 3f. As water is added, the deposited particles distribute more uniformly, covering the CF substrate well (Fig. 3c). The magnied image conrms that the particles are compact yet less aggregated, forming an interconnected mountain range-like structure (Fig. 3g), unlike the NC(0) sample. With further increase in water content, particle distribution becomes more uniform and aggregation decreases (Fig."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_039,"3d), resulting in an even mountain range-like structure with good interconnection (Fig. 3h). However, excessive water addition leads to signicant particle aggregation, resulting in a thick, rough, and uneven deposit layer on the substrate (Fig. 3e and i). The irregular structure of the NC(5) sample may correspond to the formation of Ni(OH)2 as discussed in Fig. 3a. Fig. 4 (a) SEM image of the typical Ni-Cu/CF electrode NC(3) with (b) Ni and (c) Cu element mappings. (d) Ni 2p, (e) Cu 2p, and (f) O 1s XPS spectra of the typical Ni-Cu/CF electrode NC(3)."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_040,"The NC(3) sample, which has the most uniform deposit layer, was selected as the typical electrode for further investigation. Fig. 4a-c show the EDS element mapping results of the NC(3) electrode. The results indicate that Ni (Fig. 4b) and Cu (Fig. 4c) elements are uniformly distributed along the deposited particles on the CF substrate, demonstrating the uniform deposition of the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst. The typical NC(3) electrocatalyst was further examined by XPS, and the analysis results are shown in Fig. 4d-f. In the Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 4d), the peaks at binding energies (BEs) of 852.9 and 870.4 eV correspond to Ni 2p3/2 and Ni 2p1/2, indicating the presence of Ni 0 ."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_041,"The relative intensity ratio between the 2p3/2 and 2p1/2 peaks is approximately 2 : 1. The peaks observed at BE = 860.8 and 879.5 eV are shakeup satellites, generated due to multielectron excitations. In addition, a minor presence of Ni 2+ is evidenced by the Ni 2p3/2 and Ni 2p1/2 peaks at BE = 857.6 and 875.2 eV, possibly due to natural surface oxidation or the formation of hydroxides before XPS measurements. 9 In the Cu 2p spectrum (Fig. 4e), two peaks at BE = 932.3 and 952.4 eV correspond to Cu 2p3/2 and Cu 2p1/2, accompanied by two shakeup satellites at BE = 941.5 and 961.3 eV. These signals conrm the presence of Cu 0 ."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_042,"However, based on the deconvolution results, a minor presence of Cu 2+ is recognized, possibly due to partial oxidation of the sample before the measurement. 24 The unexpected oxidized metal species were also evidenced from the O 1s spectrum (Fig. 4f), presenting the signals of Ni(OH)2 and Cu(OH)2 at BE = 531.4 and 530.5 eV, respectively, as well as minor adsorbed H2O. Overall, these ndings reveal that the prepared electrocatalyst comprises a Ni-Cu alloy, albeit with minor hydroxide species on its surface likely formed during sampling. This is consistent with the XRD and EDS analysis results."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,5,5,P25_p5_043,"Based on the specic features of the Ni-Cu/CF electrodes, a formation mechanism for the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalysts on the CF substrate is proposed, as illustrated in Fig. 5. When a negative potential is applied to the CF substrate in the watercontaining DES system, the electrodeposition of the Ni-Cu alloy begins. Meanwhile, water molecules are conned by DES molecules (EG) due to hydrogen bonding (Fig. 5a). The initial metal deposit formed on CF then starts to catalyze the water molecules, causing them to decompose into H2 and OH - (Fig. 5b). Due to their negative polarity, OH - ions accumulate at a certain distance from the substrate surface, combining with Ni(OH){2"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_044,Fig. 5 Schematic illustration of the formation of Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst on the CF substrate in a water-containing DES system: (a) initial metal deposition; (b) water electrolysis; (c) formation of Ni(OH)2 colloidal membrane where selective metal ions transport occurs; (d) deposition of Ni-rich Ni-Cu alloy.
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_045,"Ni 2+ to form a Ni(OH)2 colloid membrane. Similar to the behavior in general aqueous solutions, the Ni(OH)2 membrane can effectively prevent Cu 2+ from transferring to the electrode surface (Fig. 5c), thereby reducing the Cu composition in the Ni-Cu alloy deposit. As the water content increases in the DES, the Ni(OH)2 membrane becomes denser due to the increased production of OH - ions, which favors the formation of a Ni-rich Ni-Cu alloy (Fig. 5d)."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_046,"This is likely the reason why the NC(3) electrocatalyst exhibits higher Ni content within the Ni-Cu alloy compared to NC(1) and NC(0), as shown in Table S1.† However, excessive production of OH - ions can hinder the formation of the Ni-Cu alloy and cause the precipitation of Ni(OH)2 due to the overproduction of Ni(OH)2 membrane, as evidenced by XRD results in Fig. 3a. To summarize, the electrodeposition process and the composition of the Ni-Cu alloy in a water-containing DES system can be controlled by adjusting the water content. The adjustment can affect the material properties and, consequently, the performance in water splitting. 3.3 Electrochemical performance of Ni-Cu/CF electrodes"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_047,"Polarization curves are used to examine the of HER performance of CF, Ni/CF, Cu/CF, and various Ni-Cu/CF electrodes, as summarized in Fig. 6a. Compared with the other electrodes, the pristine CF electrode exhibits much poorer HER performance, with a high overpotential of 588 mV at 10 mA cm -2 . Aer metal electrocatalysts are loaded onto the CF substrate, the HER overpotential is greatly reduced. The Ni-Cu alloy-based electrodes show lower overpotential than those of single Ni or single Cu metal-based electrodes, corroborating the synergistic effect of the alloy-type electrocatalyst in terms of moderate hydrogen adsorption energy."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_048,"19 Additionally, the electrodes prepared by a water-containing DES system outperform those made with a water-decient DES-based system in terms of lower overpotential. In particular, the NC(3) electrode exhibits the lowest overpotential of 53 mV at 10 mA cm -2 , while the NC(0) electrode requires 83 mV to attain the same current density. The appropriate addition (3 wt%) of water in DES is benecial for improving deposit uniformity and the Ni/Cu ratio, as discussed in Fig. 3 and 4, thereby enhancing the HER performance of the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_049,"This enhanced HER performance is superior to that of several previously reported noble metal and noble metal-free electrocatalysts in terms of lower overpotentials at 10 and 50 mA cm -2 for HER, as summarized in Table S2.† To further investigate the reaction kinetics of the electrodes, the electrode potentials obtained from polarization curves were plotted against the logarithm of current density to create Tafel plots, as displayed in Fig. 6b. According to the linear relationship between these two parameters, all the electrode reactions follow the Tafel equation, indicating that the reaction control region is dominant."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_050,"Notably, the NC(3) electrode shows the lowest Tafel slope of 66 mV dec -1 , indicating more favorable electrode kinetics toward HER compared to the others. Conversely, the single Ni or Cu electrode exhibit relative high Tafel slopes, signifying poor HER kinetics. The reaction mechanisms of HER in an alkaline medium involve three reaction steps: the Volmer, Heyrovsky, and Tafel steps (see eqn S1-S4†). The Volmer step dominates electrode kinetics when the Tafel slope is approximately 120 mV dec -1 , while the Heyrovsky or Tafel step becomes dominant when the Tafel slope approximates 40 or 30 mV dec -1 ."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_051,"Based on the above statements, the Heyrovsky step where adsorbed H atoms react with protons from H2O and electrons is considered the more dominant ratedetermining step for HER with the NC(3) electrode, rather than the Volmer step. 14 Importantly, the promoted electronic effect of Ni-Cu alloy, involving d electron transfer from Cu 0 to Ni 0 , could enhance H-atom adsorption and thus the Volmer step. 25 Furthermore, the Ni/Cu ratio within the NC(3) electrocatalyst may be more balanced in terms of H adsorption energy, being closer to zero in volcano plots, leading to the enhanced HER performance."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,6,P25_p6_052,"26 EIS measurements were conducted to further explore the reaction kinetics of the HER process using different composite electrodes, as shown in Fig. 6c. The obtained Nyquist plots were tted using a proposed equivalent circuit model (inset) to quantify the system's resistances, as summarized in Table S3.† In the model, Rs represents the ohmic resistance of the electrode and electrolyte, Rc is the contact resistance between the CF substrate and the Ni-Cu alloy catalyst, and Rct is the charge transfer resistance at the interface between the electrode and electrolyte. Two semicircles in the spectra are typical impedance features related to Rc and Rct."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,6,7,P25_p6_053,"While Rs and Rc among the electrodes are similar, Rct differs signicantly. In particular, the NC(3) electrode demonstrates an Rct of 0.4 U for HER, which is much lower than single Ni (3.0 U) and single Cu (3.4 U). This indicates that the Ni-Cu alloy of the NC(3) Fig. 6 Polarization curves for (a) HER and (d) OER with various electrodes. The corresponding Tafel plots for (b) HER and (e) OER with various electrodes. EIS spectra at (c) -0.1 V (V vs. RHE) for HER and (f) 1.6 V (V vs. RHE) for OER with various electrodes."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_054,"electrode facilitates charge transfer on the electrode surface during the HER process, corresponding to the enhanced electronic interaction between the Ni and Cu species within the alloy. This nding also supports the improved HER performance of the NC(3) electrode. The polarization curves for OER using the various electrodes are shown in Fig. 6d. At around 1.4 V (vs. RHE), an oxidation peak is observed for each Nicontaining electrode, indicating the valence state transition from Ni 2+ to Ni 3+ . 27 This is also supported by the Ni-free electrodes, for which no such oxidation peak is found in their curves. This peak is attributed to the formation of NiOOH, which is electroactive for OER."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_055,"28 Beyond 1.4 V, current densities of the various electrodes signicantly increase with different onset potentials, the start of OER. Similar to the results for HER performance, the pristine CF electrode exhibits poorer OER performance, presenting higher overpotential compared to the electrocatalyst-containing electrodes. Additionally, the overpotentials of the alloy-type electrodes are smaller than those of the single Ni or Cu electrodes, which is consistent with the ndings for HER."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_056,"Interestingly, the NC(3) electrode, which shows the strongest oxidation current density at approximately 1.4 V among the tested electrodes, demonstrates the lowest overpotential for OER (390 mV at 50 mA cm -2 ). This denotes that the presence of more electroactive NiOOH within the NC(3) electrode facilitates OER by reducing overpotential. Unlike its outstanding HER performance, the optimized Ni-Cu alloy demonstrates moderate OER performance compared to other previously reported electrocatalysts (Table S2†). It is inferred that the developed catalyst may favor HER more than OER in this regard."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_057,"This could be because the appropriate Cu content within Ni-Cu alloy is energetically more favorable for H2 desorption, (small Gibbs free energy change for adsorbed H atoms) than O2 desorption. 8 The electrode kinetics of the various electrodes for OER were investigated through Tafel plots, as shown in Fig. 6e. As expected from the polarization curves, the NC(3) electrode exhibits the best electrode kinetics toward OER, evidenced by the smallest Tafel slope among the electrodes. This is followed by the other alloy-type electrodes, the single-metal electrodes, and the pristine CF electrode."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_058,"The enhanced reaction kinetics of the NC(3) can be attributed to the formation of more NiOOH during OER, corresponding to the uniform deposition of the Ni-Cu electrocatalyst over the CF substrate and the promoted electronic effect between the Ni and Cu species. The EIS measurement results of the various electrodes offer additional insight into their electrode kinetics, as summarized in Fig. 6f. According to the tting results (Table S3†), the Ni-Cu alloy electrodes show lower Rct than the single Ni or Cu electrodes, with the NC(3) electrode presenting the lowest Rct of 0.9 U among the alloy-type electrodes."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_059,"This nding further supports the synergistic effect of the Ni-Cu alloy, particularly the promoted electronic effect between the Ni and Cu species in the NC(3) electrode, contributing the improved OER performance. Overall, thanks to the DES synthesis system with the appropriate addition of water, the NC(3) electrode reveals prominent bifunctional HER and OER activities."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_060,"Fig. 7a-f show the typical CV curves in the non-faradaic region for the various electrodes at different scan rates. All the electrodes demonstrate rectangular patterns in their CV curves, indicating capacitor-like behavior during the CV process. While the current densities of all the electrodes increase with the scan rates, the Ni-Cu alloy electrodes exhibit more signicant responses to changes in scan rate compared to single Ni and Cu electrodes. Particularly, the NC(3) electrode presents the highest average current density at each scan rate among the electrodes, leading to the greatest integrated area demarcated by its CV curves."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_061,"The relationship between the average current density and scan rate allows for the computation of Cdl, thus assessing the ECSA of each electrode. As shown in Fig. 7g, the Cdl of single Ni or Cu electrodes is relatively small, while the alloy-type electrodes demonstrate enhanced Cdl. Among them, the NC(3) exhibits the highest Cdl of 14.2 mF cm -2 , signifying more abundant electrochemically active sites within the electrode. This nding is consistent with SEM observations (Fig. 3b-i), which show the ne and uniform structure of the Ni-Cu alloy deposit formed using the DES system with appropriate water content."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,7,P25_p7_062,"Beneting from the high ECSA, the NC(3) electrode reveals high electrocatalytic activity and outperforms its counterparts in terms of HER and OER performance. To evaluate the corrosion resistance of the various electrocatalyst materials, potentiodynamic polarization for each electrode was conducted, and the Tafel plots are shown in Fig. 7h. By extrapolating the linear portion of cathodic and anodic polarization curves described by the Tafel equation, the corrosion potential (Ecorr) and corrosion current density (icorr) can be obtained. A more positive Ecorr denotes the material's thermodynamically inertness against corrosion, and a smaller icorr indicates a lower corrosion rate."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,7,8,P25_p7_063,"Based on this, the alloy-type electrodes exhibit higher (more positive) Ecorr and smaller icorr than the single Ni and Cu electrodes, suggesting the enhanced corrosion resistance of the Ni-Cu electrocatalysts. Among the alloy-type electrodes, the NC(3) electrode demonstrates the highest Ecorr of Fig. 7 CV curves of (a) Cu/CF, (b) Ni/CF, (c) NC(0)/CF, (d) NC(1)/CF, (e) NC(3)/CF, and (f) NC(5)/CF electrodes with different scan rates. (g) Plots of current density vs. scan rate based on the CV results. (h) Tafel plots of various electrodes."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,8,8,P25_p8_064,-0.26 V and the smallest icorr of 25 mA cm -2 . The improved anticorrosion properties can be attributed to its ne and uniform structure of the NC(3) Ni-Cu alloy. 29 This nding also reveals that the DES reaction system with appropriate water content not only enhances the bifunctional catalytic activities of the Ni-Cu/CF electrode toward HER and OER but also improves its corrosion resistance against the strong alkaline environment (1.0 M KOH(aq)).
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,8,8,P25_p8_065,"To assess the stability of the composite electrode (NC(3)/CF) for HER and OER, a 12-h chronopotentiomety analysis was conducted within a three-electrode system. In Fig. 8a, the initial overpotential for cathodic HER is measured as 53 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm -2 . Over time, this overpotential remains nearly stable, showing only a slight decrease to 55 mV aer 12 h, which conrms the electrode's high stability and low overpotential during the HER process employing the NC(3)/CF electrode. Moving to the OER aspect (Fig. 8b), the overpotential starts at 390 mV and experiences a slight increase as the OER proceeds, reaching 402 mV aer 12 h of operation."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,8,8,P25_p8_066,"The slight increase in overpotential is attributed to the higher operating current density of 50 mA cm -2 , which leads to increased accumulation of O2 gas on the electrode surface. 7 Despite this minor increase, the NC(3)/CF electrode maintains its stable nature with a low overpotential during the entire period of OER operation. Fig. 8c and d illustrate the cell stability during water-splitting using the NC(3)/CF electrode as both cathode and anode within the electrolytic cell. The cell voltage stabilizes at 1.52 V at 10 mA cm -2 over the 12-h operation period, demonstrating the low operational voltage and high durability of the electrolytic cell for water-splitting applications (Fig. 8c)."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,8,8,P25_p8_067,"Although the OER performance in terms of overpotential does not reach the highest reported levels, the low overall voltage for water splitting outperforms that of previously reported electrolytic cells composed of bifunctional electrocatalysts. 30-32 Even at an increased current density of 50"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,9,9,P25_p9_068,Fig. 8 Chronopotentiometric analysis of electrolytic cells usingNC(3) as the cathode andgraphite rod as the anode for (a) HER and (b) OER in 1.0 KOH solution. Chronopotentiometric analysis of electrolytic cells using NC(3) as both the cathode and anode at current densities of (c) 10 mA cm -2 and (d) 50 mA cm -2 . (e) Accumulation of H2 produced from the electrolytic cell composed of NC(3) as both the cathode and anode at 10 mA cm -2 . (f) Gas chromatogram of gases collected from the cathode compartment of the electrolytic cell composed of NC(3) as both the cathode and anode at 10 mA cm -2 .
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,9,9,P25_p9_069,"mA cm -2 , the cell voltage remains stable at approximately 1.86 V throughout the 12-h operation (Fig. 8d). The stability of the electrolysis process is further demonstrated by a 168-h operation at 50 mA cm -2 , during which the cell voltage remains consistently stable with only a minimal increase over time (Fig. S1†). Notably, the surface morphology and chemical state of the NC(3) electrocatalyst exhibit negligible changes aer the long-term operation (Fig. S2 and S3†), further conrming its robust stability during electrolysis. This low cell voltage and high durability underscore the promising bifunctional electrocatalytic activities of the optimized Ni-Cu alloy toward HER and OER."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,9,9,P25_p9_070,"Moreover, the Ni-Cu electrocatalyst and CF substrate demonstrate resilience under high pH electrolysis conditions, as inferred from the consistent performance observed. Fig. 8e presents the accumulation of H2 over time during the watersplitting process, where H2 gas collected from the electrolytic cell conrms the theoretical predictions based on Faraday's law of electrolysis, achieving a high faradaic efficiency of exceeding"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_071,"99%. The purity of the collected H2 from the electrolytic cell was veried using gas chromatography (Fig. 8f). The chromatogram shows a distinct peak at approximately 1.1 min, corresponding to H2, followed by a minor peak around 1.5 min representing O2. By comparing the integrated peak areas of these gases, the H2 purity is determined to be approximately 99%. These comprehensive ndings validate the exceptional bifunctional catalytic activities of the non-noble metal-based Ni-Cu/CF electrode toward both HER and OER, highlighting its signi- cant potential in practical water-splitting applications. 4 Conclusions"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_072,"Bifunctional Ni-Cu/CF catalyst electrode were successfully developed via one-step electrodeposition within a watercontaining ChCl/EG DES system. The ionic conductivity and viscosity of the DES medium are enhanced by the additional water content up to 5 wt% without compromising the structural integrity of the DES molecules. At a specic water content of 3 wt%, formation of a moderate Ni(OH)2 colloid membrane is achieved during electrodeposition. This leads to uniform and ne deposition of the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst onto the CF substrate, and an appropriate Ni/Cu ratio within the deposited alloy."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_073,"Due to the specic features, the optimized NC(3) electrode demonstrates exceptional catalytic activities toward HER and OER in a 1.0 M KOH solution, with an overpotential of 53 mV at 10 mA cm -2 and 390 mV at 50 mA cm -2 , respectively. The smaller Tafel slope, lower charge transfer impedance, and augmented ECSA of the NC(3) electrode support its favorable electrode kinetics toward HER and OER. Additionally, the promoted electronic effect between the Ni and Cu species of the Ni-Cu alloy electrocatalyst may play a crucial role in enhancing the catalytic activity, which renders the NC(3) alloy better HER and OER performance than single Ni or Cu electrocatalyst."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_074,"Furthermore, the appropriate Ni/Cu ratio of the NC(3) alloy electrocatalyst leads to an improved corrosion resistance against strong alkaline environment. The electrolytic cell, equipped with the optimized Ni-Cu/CF electrodes serving as both cathode and anode, demonstrates exceptional stability in water-splitting over 12 h. It maintains cell voltages of approximately 1.52 and 1.86 V at 10 and 50 mA cm -2 , respectively. Specically for HER, the electrode achieves a high faradaic efficiency of over 99%, resulting in collected H2 gas purity of approximately 99%."
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_075,"The improved HER and OER performance of the Ni-Cu/CF electrode validates the feasibility and potential of the facile one-step electrodeposition in a water-containing DES system for manufacturing non-noble metal-based electrodes for water-splitting. Data availability Data for this article, including FTIR, ionic conductivity, viscosity, contact angle analysis, XRD, SEM(EDS), XPS, LSV, EIS, CV, Tafel plots, CP, H2 accumulation, and GC analysis are available at Google Drive at [https://drive.google.com/drive/ folders/1GK3OKhoam7a6FY_OvasjXaXTI6dyYa7k? usp=drive_link]. Author contributions"
P25,d5ta01877c,d5ta01877c.pdf,10,10,P25_p10_076,"Yi-Hung Liu: conceptualization, methodology, resources, data curation, writing - original dra, writing - review & editing, visualization, supervision, project administration, funding acquisition. Fen-Yan Zeng: conceptualization, methodology, validation, investigation, data curation, visualization. Yen-Shen Kuo: writing - review & editing, visualization. Yu Chen: validation, investigation. Cheng-Liang Hsu: writing - review & editing, supervision. Conflicts of interest There are no conicts to declare. Acknowledgements"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_000,"pubs.acs.org/JPCC Article Tungsten-Nickel Alloy Boosts Alkaline Hydrogen Evolution Reaction Published as part of The Journal of Physical Chemistry virtual special issue ""Energy and Catalysis in China"". Nana Yang, § Zhigang Chen,* ,§ Ding Ding, Chengfeng Zhu, Xingxing Gan, and Yi Cui* Cite This: J. Phys. Chem. C 2021, 125, 27185-27191 ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations *sı Supporting Information"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_001,"ABSTRACT: The alkaline electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution can produce clean and high-purity hydrogen gas that meets sustainable demands. Metal oxides supported transition metal alloys (e.g., WNi4@WOx, MoNi4@MoOx) can work in synergy to alter the sluggish kinetics of alkaline HER electrocatalysis. However, to date the formation mechanism of the metal alloys extracted from nickel tungstate/molybdate precursors is unclear. Moreover, the synergistic effects of W/Mo and Ni atoms were solely based on the density functional theory calculations, and no sufficient practical evidence can support the results of the simulations. Here, we demonstrate the formation"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_002,mechanism of WNi4@W-WO2 using in situ variable temperature near ambient pressure X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (NAPXPS) measurements. The as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 heterostructures exhibited markedly high alkaline HER performance with a low overpotential (83 mV) at 10 mA/cm 2 and a small Tafel slope (83 mV/dec). The quasi in situ alkaline HER electrocatalysis determined that the enhanced mechanism of alkaline HER activity can be attributed to the synergistic effects of the neighboring W and Ni atoms. This work paves a feasible route for designing desired transition metal alloy catalysts toward highly efficient alkaline HER electrocatalysis and the elucidation of the catalytic mechanism.
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_003,"■ INTRODUCTION The electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction from water electrocatalysis has long been considered as one of the most economical and effective ways for producing high-purity hydrogen gas without the contamination of acid mist and can be combined with oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and chlor-alkali electrocatalysis for scale-up applications. 1-3 Unlike the acidic solutions, the reaction pathway of alkaline HER electrocatalysis involves an additional water dissociation step (Volmer step, H2O + e - → H* + OH - ) and the subsequent hydrogen desorption step (Heyrovsky step, H* + H2O + e - → H2 + OH - ; or Tafel step, 2H* → H2)."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_004,"The additional energy barrier in the cleavage of H-OH bonds makes the alkaline HER electrocatalysis more dynamic unfavorable than that in acidic medium. 4,5 As a benchmark HER catalyst, platinum group metals (PGM) can efficiently lower the kinetic HER overpotentials, but the high cost and scarcity of PGM have seriously impeded their widespread utilization. 6 Therefore, the exploration of earth-abundant alkaline HER catalysts with low overpotentials and high stability is very interesting and challenging."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_005,"Recently, the synergistic effect of first-row (3d) transition metal (i.e., Ni) and non-3d metal (i.e., W and Mo) anchored on metallic WO3-x and MoO3-x oxides have been reported for highly efficient alkaline HER electrocatalysis due to their wide regulation of the d-band electronic structure in associated with the hydrogen binding energy (ΔGH). 7-9 In addition, the metallic WO3-x/MoO3-x substrates can reduce the charge transfer resistance, thus boosting much facile electrode kinetics"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_006,"toward hydrogen evolution. 10-12 Usually, the binary metal alloy can be synthesized by the calcination of the corresponding NiWOx/NiMoOx precursors under an H2/Ar (v/v, 5/95) atmosphere, where the NiWOx/NiMoOx precursors were partially decomposed into the anchored WNix/MoNix nanocrystals and the underlying WO3-x/ MoO3-x substrates by controlling the outward diffusion of Ni atoms."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_007,"7,13 However, to date the formation mechanism of such state of the art catalysts is only predicted based on the feasible ex situ X-ray diffraction characterization of the asprepared powders at different temperatures, which will lead to the uncontrollability of the supported WNix/MoNix species, 13 hence resulting in different HER activities. Therefore, in situ detection technique to monitor the formation of the WNix/ MoNix active materials is crucial for the appealing W/Mo-Ni electronic structures toward highly efficient HER electrocatalysis."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,1,1,P26_p1_008,"Moreover, although numerous works have reported the highly efficient alkaline HER activity over the samples of WNix/MoNix anchored on WO3-x/MoO3-x substrates, the enhanced mechanism based on the synergistic effect of W/Mo Received: August 23, 2021 Revised: November 22, 2021 Published: December 7, 2021"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_009,Figure 1. (a) Schematic illustration of the synthesis of WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids using NiWO4 precursors via a calcination process. (b) XRD patterns of the NiWO4 precursors and the product of WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids. (c) Low- and (d) high-magnification TEM images of the asobtained WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids with the lattice fringes and the EDS-elemental spectrum inset in (d). (e) Typical EDS-elemental mapping of one nanopaticle.
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_010,"and Ni atoms was only determined by the theoretical models. For example, Yin et al, reported the construction of Ni-WO2@ C/NF heterostructures for HER electrocatalysis in alkaline medium, where the interface between Ni and WOx enriching the active sites and promoting the charge transfer were responsible for the highly efficient alkaline HER activity. 7 Feng et al."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_011,"synthesized the MoNi4/MoO2@Ni nanohybrids by treating the NiMoO4 precursors at 500 °C for 2 h under an Ar/H2 atmosphere, and the high alkaline HER activity can be attributed to the synergistic effect of Ni and Mo atoms 13 in which the 3d Ni atoms are expected to accelerate the water dissociation kinetics in Volmer step, whereas the non-3d W/ Mo counterparts serve as the active sites for the H + produced during alkaline HER electrocatalysis. Hu et al. constructed the NiMo solid solution nanowire arrays, and the electronic interactions of Mo-Ni atoms resulted in the downshift of Mo d-band center, thus facilitating the desorption of H* intermediates from the catalyst surface."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_012,"9 As can be seen, no consensus has been reached on the catalytic mechanism of the enhanced HER activity, because no reliable experiment evidence supplements the theoretical discussion in practice. Therefore, the detection of the subtle change on the electronic structures of Ni and W/Ni atoms during alkaline HER electrocatalysis using in situ characterization techniques is both intriguing and valuable."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_013,"Herein, we report the synthesis of WNi4 alloy anchored on the metallic tungsten species (denoted WNi4@W-WO2) via a calcination process, where the formation mechanism is monitored by the in situ variable temperature near ambient pressure photoelectron spectroscopy (NAP-XPS). Because of the strong electronic interactions of W and Ni atoms, the optimized Ni and Mo sites are beneficial for lowering the barrier energies in prior water dissociation and subsequent H* desorption."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_014,"Consequently, the as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids exhibited markedly high alkaline HER activity with a low overpotential of 83 mV at 10 mA/cm 2 and a small Tafel slope (83 mV/dec), which are much superior than those of bare Ni and WO2 counterparts. Moreover, the quasi in situ alkaline HER measurements performed in vacuum-connected NAP-XPS and glovebox system demonstrated that electrons were transferred from Ni sites to W counterparts at applied negative potentials, resulting in the formation of high-valence Ni and low-valence W sites within WNi alloy, which will work in synergy for the water dissociation step and the desorption of the adsorbed H* intermediates."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_015,"This work paves a feasible route for the controllable fabrication of the desired W-Ni alloy for highly efficient alkaline HER electrocatalysis, as well as the detection of the subtle change on the electronic structures of the active sites during electrocatalytic reactions. ■ RESULTS AND DISCUSSION"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_016,"The as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids were synthesized according to previously reported protocols. 7 Briefly, as illustrated in Figure 1a, sodium tungstate (Na2WO4·2H2O, 9.9 mg), nickel chloride (NiCl2·6H2O, 7.128 mg), and urea (H2NCONH2, 3.6 mg) were dissolved in the mixture of glycol (30 mL) and distilled water (30 mL) followed by stirring for 30 min. After that, the homogeneous solutions and pretreated Ni foam (1 × 3 cm) were put into a Teflon-lined autoclave and kept at 160 °C for 12 h. After cooling down to room temperature, the black products coating in the Ni foam were washed several times with ethanol and distilled water."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,2,2,P26_p2_017,"Finally, the WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids were obtained by pyrolyzing the NiWO4 precursors at 550 °C for 2 h under a 5% H2 and Ar atmosphere. For comparison, the bare Ni and WO2 samples were synthesized by the same method without the addition of Na2WO4·2H2O and NiCl2·6H2O, respectively. The composition and crystal phase of the as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 nanohybrids were verified by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure 1b). The product obtained in pure Ar atmosphere only exhibited the feature of NiWO4 composition (JCPDF No. 15-0755), while hybrid signals indicating the WNi4 alloy (JCPDF No. 65-2673), WO2"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_018,"Figure 2. In situ variable temperature NAP-XPS at temperatures of 25-620 °C. (a) W 4f, (b) Ni 2p, and (c) O 1s core level XPS spectra."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_019,"(JCPDF No. 32-1393) and metallic W (JCPDF No. 04-0806) were clearly observed in WNi4@W-WO2 sample, implying the vital role of H2 atmosphere in the synthesis of WNi4 alloy (Figure S1). The morphology of the as-obtained WNi4@WWO2 sample was then observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electronic microscopy (TEM). The SEM images confirmed that the Ni foam was uniformly coated by the as-obtained W-Ni nanohybrids with typical nanoparticle morphology (Figure S2). The low- and high-magnification TEM images and the corresponding EDX and STEM mapping can allow us to observe the closer morphology and composition in more detail."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_020,"As shown in Figure 1c, numerous nanoparticles with lateral dimension of approximately 50 nm were seen in the low-magnification TEM image of WNi4@W-WO2 sample. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image gives three types of lattice fringes of 3.45, 2.40, and 2.07 Å, which are attributed to the (011)WO2, (110)W, and (211)WNi4 facets, respectively (Figure 1d). The corresponding energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy and STEM-EDS mapping revealed the homogeneous distribution of the Ni, W, and O elements over the entire nanoparticle (inset in Figure 1d,e). In addition, the morphology of bare Ni and WO2 samples were also observed by TEM measurement."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_021,"One could see that the WO2 products exhibited typical nanoparticles with sizes less than 20 nm (Figure S3a), and the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (inset in Figure S3a) showed the polycrystalline nature of the nanopaticles with the presence of (011) and (101) facts of monoclinic WO2 phase, besides the marked d-spacing value of 0.35 nm in the HRTEM images is in good consistency with the (011) facet of WO2 (Figure S3b)."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_022,"However, a loosely packed network constructed by Ni nanosheets was seen in Ni products (Figure S3c), and the corresponding SAED pattern (inset in Figure S3c) and the d-spacing of lattice fringe (Figure S 3d) evidenced the formation of metallic Ni with facecentered cubic (fcc) structure."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_023,"To gain insight on the subtle change of the electronic structures in W and Ni atoms during the thermally induced outward diffusion of the Ni atoms, in situ variable-temperature NAP-XPS measurements were conducted on NiWO4/Ni foam under an H2 atmosphere (0.1 mbar), and the Ni 2p and W 4f core level XPS spectra were recorded at temperatures from 25 to 620 °C. One could see that the signals of W 0 (31.6 eV for W 4f7/2 and 33.8 eV for W 4f5/2) and Ni 0 (852.8 eV for Ni 2p3/2 and 870.1 eV for Ni 2p1/2) were detected at 410 °C in W 4f and Ni 2p XPS core level spectra, respectively (Figure 2a,b), indicating the outward diffusion of Ni atoms started to occur at approximately 400 °C."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_024,"After treatment at temperatures higher than 550 °C, the high valence states of W 6+ and Ni 2+ /Ni 3+ were almost absent, implying the outward diffusion of Ni atoms was over, and the metallic WNi4 was probably formed."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_025,"Correspondingly, the O 1s core level spectra can also give information on the evolution of the NiWO4 precursors during the calcination process (Figure 2c). One can see a predominant peak at 531.5 eV for the fresh sample, which is attributed to the lattice oxygen species. The intensities of the sample decreased after the temperatures were higher than 410 °C, indicating the reduction process by H2 gas. Remarkably, the sample exhibited a sharp decrease of the intensity at 550 °C, suggesting the formation of WNi4 alloy and the residual oxygen species probably originated from the WO2 substrates."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_026,"Also, a controlled NAP-XPS measurement of NiWO4 at pure Ar atmosphere has been provided in the Supporting Information. As shown in Figure S4, the valence state of tungsten species still centered at 6+ with the increase of the temperature, indicating the reducing atmosphere provided by H2 gas played a vital role in the formation of low-valence and metallic tungsten species."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_027,"However, we could observe that Ni 0 species began to appear when the heating the NiWO4 precursor up to 440 °C under pure Ar atmosphere, and the predominant Ni 0 species were detected in the NiWO4 precursor after treated at 620 °C, indicating the reducing atmosphere has no influence on the formation of metallic Ni 0 species. Therefore, combining the information on Ni 2p, W 4f, and O 2p core level XPS, we can conclude that WO2 substrates supporting W-Ni alloy have been achieved."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_028,"From our viewpoint, the construction of WNi4@W-WO2 heterostructures can be beneficial for improving the alkaline HER electrocatalysis for the following three reasons: (i) the WNi alloy can provide dual sites for the adsorption of the HOH bonds, which can efficiently reduce the sluggish kinetics in prior water dissociation step; 14 (ii) the Ni-dopant can optimize the electronic structures of W sites, which will accelerate the desorption behavior of the adsorbed H* intermediates; 15 and (iii) the metallic W-WO2 substrates can facilitate the charge transfer from the electrode to the adsorbed alkaline HER intermediates, thus boosting the facial electrode kinetics toward hydrogen evolution."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,3,P26_p3_029,"The HER performance of WNi4@W-WO2 was evaluated using a standard three-electrode system in 1.0 M KOH solutions along with the bare WO2, Ni, NiWO4 precursors, and the commercial Pt/C catalyst. As can be seen, bare WO2 and Ni exhibited very poor alkaline HER activities with overpotentials at 10 mA/cm 2 up to 293 and 277 mV, respectively, indicating that bare W and Ni species are not the major contributors of alkaline HER activities. Besides, the NiWO4 precursors also showed a large overpotential value (265 mV), implying the vital role of the W-Ni electronic interactions in the synergistic effect of the enhanced alkaline HER activity."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,3,4,P26_p3_030,"Encouragingly, the WNi4@W-WO2 sample exhibited a markedly high alkaline HER activity with a low overpotential at 10 mA/cm 2 (η10 = 83 mV), which is much more superior Time (h) 18 Figure 3. Evaluation of alkaline HER performance in 1.0 M KOH solutions. (a) Polarization curves and (b) Tafel plots of Ni, WO2, NiWO4, WNi4@W-WO2, and commercial Pt/C catalyst. (c) Comparison of η10 and Tafel slope of WNi4@W-WO2 in this work and previously reported alloy samples. (d) CdI values and (e) Nyquist plots of bare Ni, WO2, NiWO4 precursors, and WNi4@W-WO2. (f) Chronopotentiometry measurement of the as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 catalyst at a current density of 10 mA/cm 2 ."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_031,"than those of previously reported alloy catalysts, 16-31 and even being competitive to the commercial Pt/C catalyst (η10 = 48 mV) (Figure 3a,c). Besides, in order to eliminate the influence of the substrate, the polarization curves of WNi4@W-WO2 powder loading onto glassy carbon electrode (GCE) and carbon film were also collected in 1 M KOH electrolyte, respectively (electrode fabrication seen in experiment details). As can be seen, regardless of what substrates were used, the alkaline HER activities of WNi4@W-WO2 powder are still comparable to that collected on Ni foam (Figure S5), indicating the intrinsic good alkaline HER activity of the asobtained WNi4@W-WO2 catalyst."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_032,"Additional kinetic information (i.e., reaction pathway) could be extracted from the Tafel plots. The WNi4@W-WO2 sample exhibited a small Tafel slope of 83 mV/dec, which is much lower than those of WO2 (149.4 mV/dec), Ni (95.6 mV/dec), and NiWO4 precursors (179 mV/dec) (Figure 3b), indicating the reaction pathway of alkaline HER electrocatalysis over the WNi4@W-WO2 sample probably follows the Volmer-Heyrovsky step with the hydrogen desorption process as the rate-determining step."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_033,"32 The electrochemically active surface area was also measured to evaluate the electrocatalysts using cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements by extracting the double-layer capacitance (CdI) from 0.2 to 0.6 V versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) (Figure S6). It can be found that the value of CdI on WNi4@ W-WO2 is as high as 5.6 mF/cm 2 , which is 2.8-, 3.7-, and 2.2fold enhancement in comparison with WO2, Ni, and NiWO4, respectively (Figure 3d), indicating the WNi4@W-WO2 sample possesses a larger specific capacitance, which will provide more active sites for electrocatalysis, because the higher specific capacitance usually implies the larger electrochemical active area."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_034,"32,33 Accordingly, the superior alkaline HER activity of WNi4@W-WO2 is still remarkable after ECSA normalization, 34 demonstrating the substantial enhancement of the intrinsic alkaline HER activity by constructing such an alloy catalyst (Figure S7). The charge transfer resistance of the asobtained catalysts was examined by Nyquist plots. As shown in Figure 3e, the Nyquist plots reveal that the semicircular diameter of WNi4@W-WO2 is obviously smaller than those of WO2, Ni, and NiWO4, and even surpassing that of commercial Pt/C, suggesting the higher charge transfer kinetics on the WNi4@W-WO2 catalyst."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_035,"Long-term stability is another significant criteria of HER catalyst for practical application. As shown in the prolonged chronopotentiometry measurement (Figure 3f), the as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 catalyst exhibited a negligible activity loss of the overpotential for continuous electrocatalysis at a current density of 10 mA/cm 2 , highlighting the stable structure of the alloy catalyst in the alkaline HER process."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,4,4,P26_p4_036,"The morphology and elemental characterization of the spent WNi4@W-WO2 sample exhibited a negligible change of the composition and the structures, as determined by Figure 4a,b,c. Quasi in situ alkaline HER measurements were further conducted to probe the subtle change of the W and Ni atoms during the alkaline HER electrocatalysis via a vacuumconnected NAP-XPS and glovebox system. The W 4f and Ni 2p core level XPS spectra of the as-obtained WNi4@W-WO2 catalyst treated at different negative potentials were in situ"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,5,5,P26_p5_037,"Figure 4. Morphology characterization and surface bonding configurations of WNi4@W-WO2 after alkaline HER electrocatalysis. (a) Low- and (b) high-magnification TEM images. Inset: typical lattice fringes and EDS-elemental spectra. (c) EDS-STEM mapping of W, Ni, and O elements. (d) W 4f and (e) Ni 2p core level XPS spectra recorded at different overpotentials. (f) The adsorption modes of H2O, OH - , and H + over the synthesized WNi4@W-WO2, where the charge transfer from the metallic substrates to supported WNi4 is represented by white arrows, while the electrons transporting from Ni atoms to the neighboring W sites is indicated by the orange arrows."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,5,5,P26_p5_038,"recorded. As shown in Figure 4d, the sample only soaked in KOH solutions (initial state) exhibits three pairs of peaks, in which the first pair of peaks at lower binding energies (31.6 eV for W 4f7/2 and 33.8 eV for W 4f5/2) is attributed to the W-W metallic bonds, the second pair of peaks at middle binding energies (33.1 eV for W 4+ 4f7/2 and 35.28 eV for W 4+ 4f5/2) is assigned to low valence W 4+ in WO2 oxides, whereas the third pair of peaks at higher binding energies (35.6 eV for W 6+ 4f7/2 and 37.78 eV for W 6+ 4f5/2) originates from the high valence W 6+ in WO4 2- anionic groups."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,5,5,P26_p5_039,"As expected, the concentration of W 0 corresponding to metallic W enhanced with the increase in overpotentials from 0 to 180 mV (Figure 4d and Table S1); specifically, the high valence W 6+ among all treated samples shows an extremely lower concentration in comparison with the initial sample, indicating the oxidation state of the tungsten element is reduced during the alkaline HER electrocatalysis."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,5,5,P26_p5_040,"Contrary to the tendency of W element, the Ni species treated by the increased negative potentials exhibit higher content of high valence Ni 3+ in comparison with the initial sample, suggesting the Ni species with low valence state were oxidized after electrocatalytic reactions (Figure 4e and Table S2). Therefore, combining the change of the valence state on W and Ni species, we can conclude that the electrons transfer from Ni atoms to W sites, which benefits the desorption behavior of the adsorbed H* intermediates (Figure 4f)."
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,5,5,P26_p5_041,"In addition, the produced OH* intermediates can be detached from the catalyst surface with the increase of the negative potentials because of the Coulomb effect, and the metallic substrates can facilitate the charge transfer from the electrode to the WNi4 catalyst. ■ CONCLUSIONS In summary, we have demonstrated the formation mechanism of the WNi4@WO2 heterostructures using in situ variable temperature NAP-XPS technique. Significantly, the asobtained transition metal alloy catalysts exhibit high HER performance in alkaline medium with a low potential at 10 mA/cm 2 and a small Tafel slope, which are more superior than"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,6,6,P26_p6_043,"Experimental details (chemicals, synthesis of WNi4@ W-WO2 and other control samples, characterization, electrode fabrication, electrochemical measurements); W 4f and Ni 2p core level XPS spectra of NiWO4 precursors before and after thermal treatment in Ar atmosphere; low- and high-magnification SEM images of W-Ni nanohybrids; TEM images of bare Ni and WO2 species; W 4f and Ni 2p core level XPS spectra of NiWO4 treated from room temperature to 620 °C under Ar atmosphere using the variable-temperature NAP-XPS measurements; LSV curves of WNi4@W-WO2 powder loading onto GCE and carbon film in 1 M KOH electrolyte; cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of WNi4@ W-WO2, WO2, Ni, and NiWO4 precursors in the voltage range of 0.2-0.6 V at scan rate of 100-10 mV/s; The LSV curves of WO2, Ni, NiWO4, and WNi4@WWO2 after ECSA normalization; concentrations of W and Ni with different valence states after bias voltage treatment (PDF)"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,6,6,P26_p6_044,"■ AUTHOR INFORMATION Corresponding Authors Zhigang Chen - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and NanoBionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China; Email: zgchen2015@sinano.ac.cn Yi Cui - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and Nano-Bionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China; School of Nano Technology and Nano Bionics University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China; orcid.org/ 0000-0002-9182-9038; Email: ycui2015@sinano.ac.cn Authors"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,6,6,P26_p6_045,"Nana Yang - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and Nano-Bionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China; School of Nano Technology and Nano Bionics University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China Ding Ding - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and Nano-Bionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China"
P26,jp1c07465,jp1c07465.pdf,6,6,P26_p6_046,"Chengfeng Zhu - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and NanoBionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China; School of Nano Technology and Nano Bionics University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China Xingxing Gan - Vacuum Interconnected Nanotech Workstation, Suzhou Institute of Nano-Tech and NanoBionics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Suzhou 215123, China; School of Nano Technology and Nano Bionics University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China Complete contact information is available at: https://pubs.acs.org/10.1021/acs.jpcc.1c07465 Author Contributions § N.Y. and Z.C. contributed equally to this work. Notes"
